          "content": {
            "size": 295242,
            "mimeType": "application/json",
            "compression": 0,
            "text": "{\n  \"RequirementGathering\": [\n    {\n      \"area\": \"RequirementGathering\",\n      \"task\": \"Create inventory of all source objects (volume, keys, dependencies)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as an expert Data Engineer. I am currently in the data analysis and discovery phase of a new project and need to create a comprehensive inventory of all source objects in our database. \\r\\n\\r\\nThe target database is {Insert Database Type, e.g., SQL Server / Snowflake / PostgreSQL}.\\r\\n\\r\\nPlease generate optimized SQL scripts using system catalog views/tables to extract the following metadata:\\r\\n\\r\\n•Object Inventory & Volume: A script to list all tables and views in a specific schema, including the object name, object type, and the approximate row count (prefer system metadata tables for row counts rather than running a slow COUNT(*) on every table).\\r\\n•Keys & Constraints: A script to extract all Primary Keys (PK) and Foreign Keys (FK) for the tables, including the schema name, table name, column name, constraint name, and for FKs, the referenced target table and column.\\r\\n•Object Dependencies: A script to map dependencies between objects (e.g., which tables are used by which views, stored procedures, or functions).\\r\\n\\r\\nPlease provide the scripts separately with brief comments explaining how they work and any permissions required to run them.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide separate, optimized SQL scripts tailored to your specified database type that query system catalog views to extract metadata, including an object inventory with approximate row counts, a detailed map of Primary and Foreign Key constraints, and object dependency mappings (e.g., tables to views or stored procedures), complete with explanatory comments and a note on the required execution permissions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"RequirementGathering\",\n      \"task\": \"Create/Capture business definitions, KPIs & metric glossary\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Connect to Power BI Desktop with name **Provide name here**.\\r\\nCapture business definitions / KPIs / metrics from the report\",\n      \"output\": \"All the metric business definitions will be documented from the provided report.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"RequirementGathering\",\n      \"task\": \"Create source‑to‑raw mapping document\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as an expert Data Architect. I am in the data discovery phase and need to create a comprehensive \\\"Source-to-Raw\\\" (Source-to-Target) data mapping document. \\r\\nPlease generate a detailed mapping template in a Markdown table format based on the context provided below. \\r\\n\\r\\n**Context:**\\r\\n• **Source System / Database:** {Insert Source, e.g., Salesforce / SQL Server / SAP}\\r\\n• **Target System / Data Lake:** {Insert Target, e.g., Databricks Bronze Layer / Snowflake Raw / S3}\\r\\n• **Sample Source Tables/Entities:** {Insert 1-3 table names, e.g., Customers, Orders}\\r\\n• **Standard Audit Columns Required:** {Insert any audit columns you need, e.g., load_timestamp, source_file_name, batch_id, or type \\\"Standard\\\"}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements for the Template:**\\r\\nPlease generate a table with the following columns:\\r\\n• Source Schema / Table\\r\\n• Source Column Name\\r\\n• Source Data Type\\r\\n• Target Schema / Table\\r\\n• Target Column Name\\r\\n• Target Data Type\\r\\n• Is Primary Key / Business Key? (Yes/No)\\r\\n• Is PII? (Yes/No)\\r\\n• Transformation / Logic (Since this is to the \\\"Raw\\\" layer, default to 1:1 mapping or basic type casting, plus the audit columns).\\r\\n\\r\\nPopulate the template with realistic mock data based on the tables I provided in the context so I can see how it should be filled out.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a detailed Source-to-Raw data mapping template formatted as a Markdown table, populated with realistic mock data for your specified sample tables, detailing source and target schemas, data types, Primary Key and PII indicators, and 1:1 transformation logic inclusive of your required standard audit columns.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"RequirementGathering\",\n      \"task\": \"Define acceptance criteria for completing analysis phase\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a Senior Solution Architect and Business Analyst responsible for defining the Definition of Done (DoD) and Acceptance Criteria for the analysis and discovery phases of enterprise-grade cloud projects.\\r\\n\\r\\nContext \\r\\nThe goal is to define the acceptance criteria for completing the Analysis Phase within Azure DevOps. We need to ensure that before we move into the Development/Build phase, all technical requirements, risks, and stakeholder expectations have been validated, documented, and formally signed off to prevent scope creep and rework.\\r\\n\\r\\nProject Configuration\\r\\nProject Type: [e.g., Data Migration, Custom App Dev, AI Implementation]\\r\\nTarget Management Tool: Azure DevOps Work Items (Acceptance Criteria field)\\r\\nPhase: Analysis / Discovery\\r\\nCurrent Status: Partial (Copilot to generate templates)\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements & Constraints\\r\\nTechnical Acceptance Criteria\\r\\nDefine the requirements for data mapping, architectural diagrams, and source-to-target specifications.\\r\\nEstablish validation rules for technical feasibility assessments.\\r\\nBusiness & Stakeholder Acceptance Criteria\\r\\nDocument requirements for functional sign-offs and user story refinement.\\r\\nDefine criteria for non-functional requirements (security, performance, compliance).\\r\\nAnalysis Quality Gates\\r\\nOutline the \\\"Definition of Done\\\" for the Analysis phase (e.g., all high-priority user stories estimated, risk log updated).\\r\\nEnsure criteria are written in a way that can be easily validated in Azure DevOps Work Items.\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput Format \\r\\nPlease provide the response in Markdown including:\\r\\nMaster Acceptance Criteria Template: A categorized list of generic criteria for the Analysis phase.\\r\\nAzure DevOps Work Item Mapping: How to structure these criteria within User Stories, Features, and Epics.\\r\\nAnalysis Sign-off Checklist: A final \\\"Quality Gate\\\" checklist for project managers.\\r\\nNon-Functional Requirements (NFR) Criteria: Specific criteria for security and scalability discovery.\\r\\nAI-Enhanced Refinement Guide: Tips on using Copilot to tailor these criteria for specific user stories.\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent generates a Markdown-based framework for defining the Definition of Done (DoD) and Acceptance Criteria for the Analysis/Discovery phase of enterprise cloud projects managed in Azure DevOps.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe output includes a Master Acceptance Criteria template covering technical requirements (data mapping, architecture diagrams, source-to-target specifications) and business validation (stakeholder sign-offs, refined user stories). It also defines Analysis Quality Gates that must be completed before moving to the development phase.\\r\\n\\r\\nAdditionally, the framework explains how to map these criteria to Azure DevOps work items (Epics, Features, User Stories), provides an analysis sign-off checklist for project managers, outlines non-functional requirement discovery criteria (security, scalability, compliance), and includes guidance on using Copilot to refine acceptance criteria for individual user stories.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"RequirementGathering\",\n      \"task\": \"Identify rollback and disaster recovery requirements\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Principal Cloud Architect and Data Engineer** tasked with designing a robust **Disaster Recovery (DR) and Rollback Strategy** for a multi-service Azure Data Platform. The goal is to ensure 100% data consistency and rapid service restoration following deployment failures or regional outages.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide**:\\r\\n\\r\\n* **Services in Scope**: Azure Data Factory (ADF), Databricks (Delta Lake), Azure Synapse, and Microsoft Fabric.\\r\\n* **Primary Source/Sink**: {e.g., ADLS Gen2, SQL Server, Fabric OneLake}\\r\\n* **RPO (Recovery Point Objective)**: {e.g., 15 Minutes / 1 Hour / Zero Data Loss}\\r\\n* **RTO (Recovery Time Objective)**: {e.g., 30 Minutes / 4 Hours}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease provide a comprehensive technical specification including:\\r\\n\\r\\n* **Code & Orchestration Rollback**:\\r\\n1. Define the **Azure DevOps** Git branching and \\\"Revert\\\" strategy for ADF and Synapse artifacts.\\r\\n2. Explain how to manage **ADF Global Parameters** and **Triggers** during a rollback to a \\\"Last Known Good\\\" version.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n* **Data State Recovery (Delta Time Travel)**:\\r\\n1. Provide a **PySpark/SQL code snippet** demonstrating how to use `DESCRIBE HISTORY` and `RESTORE TABLE` to roll back Delta tables to a specific version or timestamp after a failed batch.\\r\\n2. Explain the strategy for handling **partial writes** and \\\"orphan\\\" files in the Data Lake during a failure.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n* **Regional Failover Strategy (DR)**:\\r\\n1. Outline the steps to fail over **Microsoft Fabric** and **Synapse** workspaces to a paired Azure region.\\r\\n2. Define the replication strategy for **Storage Accounts** (RA-GRS) and the redirection of ADF Integration Runtimes.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n* **Post-Rollback Integrity Verification**:\\r\\n1. Provide a **Smoke Test** checklist to verify data lineage and connectivity (e.g., checking Linked Services, Key Vault access, and Row Counts) immediately following a recovery.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**:\\r\\n\\r\\n* Use standard Markdown.\\r\\n* Include distinct blocks for PySpark/SQL logic and DevOps process flows.\\r\\n* Provide a \\\"Rollback Readiness\\\" checklist for the Engineering team at the end.\",\n      \"output\": \"Executing this prompt will generate a comprehensive technical playbook that transforms disaster recovery theory into deployable engineering logic. The AI will produce executable PySpark snippets for Delta Time Travel restoration, step-by-step Azure DevOps workflows for reverting code artifacts, and regional failover procedures for Fabric and Synapse. The final outcome is a functional, \\\"ready-to-execute\\\" manual that ensures data integrity and satisfies strict enterprise RTO/RPO requirements.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"Analysis\": [\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Analyze slowly changing attributes and determine SCD type\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Principal Data Architect and Dimensional Modeler responsible for designing enterprise-grade data warehouse schemas, surrogate key strategies, and Slowly Changing Dimension (SCD) frameworks.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nAnalyze a source schema and generate a comprehensive Slowly Changing Dimension (SCD) and Surrogate Key Strategy Proposal for Subject Matter Expert (SME) review and approval.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need to establish strict dimensional modeling rules before building our ETL/ELT pipelines to ensure historical tracking perfectly aligns with business requirements.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Target Dimension Name:** {Add Dimension Name, e.g., Dim_Employee}\\r\\n**Source Table(s):** {Add Source Tables, e.g., Workday.Workers, Workday.Positions}\\r\\n**Natural / Business Key(s):** {Add Natural Keys, e.g., EmployeeID}\\r\\n**List of Attributes to Analyze:** {Add Attributes, e.g., FirstName, LastName, SSN, Department, JobTitle, Salary, Email}\\r\\n**Business Context:** {Add Context, e.g., HR needs to track organizational hierarchy changes over time, but does not care about the history of personal email addresses.}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nCreate a highly structured, professional proposal document ready for Confluence or Azure DevOps Wiki. Acknowledge that this is a PROPOSAL and format the output so Business Subject Matter Experts (SMEs) can easily review, modify, and sign off. Apply standard Kimball dimensional modeling principles.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n1. Surrogate Key Strategy:\\r\\n• Define the deterministic Surrogate Key (SK) generation pattern.\\r\\n• Recommend a hashing algorithm (e.g., SHA-256 vs MD5) across the Natural Keys.\\r\\n• Include logic to safely handle NULL natural keys (e.g., using coalesce) before hashing.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Code Implementation:\\r\\n• Provide the exact PySpark code snippet (for Databricks/Fabric) to generate the SK.\\r\\n• Provide the exact T-SQL code snippet (for Azure Synapse) to generate the SK.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. AI-Proposed SCD Attribute Matrix:\\r\\n• Analyze the provided attributes and categorize them by proposed SCD Type (Type 0, Type 1, Type 2, or Type 3).\\r\\n• Use the provided Business Context to inform your suggestions.\\r\\n• Write a specific AI Justification for why each type was chosen.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Type 2 Implementation Meta-Columns:\\r\\n• If any columns are recommended as Type 2, define the strict system tracking columns required (e.g., RowEffectiveStartDate, RowEffectiveEndDate, IsCurrentRow).\\r\\n• Define the High Date strategy (e.g., 9999-12-31) for active records.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Sign-Off:\\r\\n• Include a clear approval checklist for stakeholders.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**: \\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown\\r\\n\\r\\nSurrogate Key Strategy with PySpark and T-SQL code blocks\\r\\nAI-Proposed SCD Attribute Matrix formatted as a table with the following exact columns: Attribute Name, Proposed SCD Type, AI Justification, and SME Approved Type (leave this column completely blank)\\r\\nType 2 System Columns definition list\\r\\nSME Sign-Off Checklist\",\n      \"output\": \"Dimensional Modeling & SCD Strategy\\r\\nThis agent provides a professional, SME-ready proposal for the architecture of the Dim_Employee dimension. It delivers a deterministic SHA-256 Hashing strategy for surrogate key generation to ensure platform-agnostic performance across Databricks and Synapse. Furthermore, the agent provides an AI-Proposed SCD Attribute Matrix that translates your specific business context—tracking hierarchy while ignoring email history—into actionable modeling types (Type 1 vs. Type 2), complete with the necessary system audit columns for robust historical reporting\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Create transformation logic inventory & SCD strategy\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a Senior Data Architect and Data Governance expert.\\r\\n\\r\\nCreate a comprehensive Transformation Logic Inventory and Slowly Changing Dimension (SCD) Strategy document for the given **System Context**\\r\\n**Transformation Logic Inventory**\\r\\nDocument all transformation rules from source systems to target data warehouse tables.\\r\\nFor each transformation, capture:\\r\\n• Source system name\\r\\n• Source table and column\\r\\n• Target table and column\\r\\n• Transformation type (standardization, derivation, aggregation, enrichment, filter, lookup, etc.)\\r\\n• Detailed transformation logic (SQL / pseudocode)\\r\\n• Business rule description (business-friendly explanation)\\r\\n• Data type conversion (if any)\\r\\n• Default values applied\\r\\n• Data quality validation rules\\r\\n• Dependencies (upstream/downstream impact)\\r\\n• Present the output in a structured table format suitable for governance documentation.\\r\\n\\r\\n**SCD Strategy**\\r\\n\\r\\nFor each dimension table, define a clear Slowly Changing Dimension strategy.\\r\\nInclude:\\r\\n• Dimension name\\r\\n• Natural key(s)\\r\\n• Surrogate key strategy\\r\\n• Attributes list\\r\\n• SCD type per attribute (Type 1, Type 2, Type 3, Hybrid)\\r\\n• Change detection mechanism (hash comparison, column compare, CDC, MERGE, etc.)\\r\\n• Effective start date logic\\r\\n• Effective end date logic\\r\\n• Current record flag logic\\r\\n• Late-arriving data handling\\r\\n• Soft delete handling\",\n      \"output\": \"The output will produce a comprehensive governance-ready document that inventories all data transformation logic from source systems to target warehouse tables and defines the Slowly Changing Dimension (SCD) strategy for dimension tables.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Identify surrogate key strategy & uniqueness rules\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Principal Cloud Data Architect responsible for designing highly scalable data architectures, specifically focusing on distributed compute environments like Spark and MPP databases.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nGenerate a comprehensive Surrogate Key Strategy and Uniqueness Rules Proposal tailored for a distributed data engineering environment to be presented for Architecture Review Board (ARB) sign-off.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need to establish standard engineering practices for dimension processing, specifically moving away from traditional sequential IDs to distributed-friendly surrogate keys, while ensuring strict data grain and uniqueness.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Target Platform:** {Add Target Platform, e.g., Databricks (Spark SQL/PySpark), Azure Synapse (T-SQL), Fabric}\\r\\n**Dimension / Entity Category:** {Add Entity Category, e.g., Core Enterprise Dimensions (Customer, Product, Location)}\\r\\n**Expected Data Volume:** {Add Expected Volume, e.g., Customers: 50 Million rows, Products: 2 Million rows}\\r\\n**Deduplication Priority:** {Add Deduplication Priority, e.g., If duplicates exist in a single batch, keep the record with the most recent LastModifiedDate}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nCreate a highly structured, professional proposal document ready for ARB review. Do not include conversational filler. You MUST explicitly address the performance implications of sequential IDs in distributed compute environments (Spark/MPP). Provide physical code examples based on the target platform.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n1. Architectural Recommendation for Surrogate Key Generation:\\r\\n• Formally propose the optimal Surrogate Key generation method for the Target Platform (e.g., Cryptographic Hashing vs. Sequences vs. monotonically_increasing_id).\\r\\n• Recommend a specific hashing algorithm (e.g., MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256) based on the Expected Data Volume and collision probability.\\r\\n• Define the Null Handling Rule specifying exactly how NULL natural keys must be handled before hashing to prevent returning a NULL surrogate key (e.g., wrapping keys in COALESCE and converting to UPPERCASE).\\r\\n• Provide the exact PySpark and T-SQL snippets to generate this SK safely.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Uniqueness and Deduplication Rules:\\r\\n• Define the strict framework for ensuring the grain of the dimension remains pure.\\r\\n• Provide the exact Window Function pattern for Intra-Batch Deduplication (using ROW_NUMBER) to resolve multiple updates to the same Natural Key arriving in the same ETL batch, based on the Deduplication Priority.\\r\\n• Define the standard for generating the Unknown Member Strategy (e.g., Hardcoding SK -1 with descriptions of Unknown or Not Applicable).\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Late Arriving Dimension Strategy:\\r\\n• Define how the platform handles Fact records that arrive before their corresponding Dimension records.\\r\\n• Propose the Skeleton Row approach (inserting a hashed SK with a placeholder natural key and Unknown attributes) versus the Wait State approach.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Architect Sign-Off:\\r\\n• Create a Markdown checklist for the Principal Architect and Lead Engineer to formally approve the Hashing Algorithm and Deduplication strategy.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**: \\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown\\r\\n\\r\\nArchitectural Recommendation with PySpark and T-SQL code blocks\\r\\nUniqueness and Deduplication Strategy\\r\\nLate Arriving Dimension Strategy\\r\\nArchitect Sign-Off Checklist\",\n      \"output\": \"Distributed Surrogate Key & Uniqueness Strategy\\r\\nThis agent provides a formal architectural proposal for transitioning from legacy sequential identifiers to distributed-friendly cryptographic surrogate keys. It addresses the performance bottlenecks of centralized identity counters in Spark and MPP environments, ensuring high-concurrency data processing for volumes exceeding 50 Million rows. By enforcing deterministic hashing, intra-batch windowed deduplication, and standardized late-arriving dimension handling, this framework establishes a robust foundation for enterprise-grade dimensional modeling on platforms like Databricks, Synapse, and Fabric.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Document risks, assumptions & dependencies\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a Senior Technical Project Manager and Azure DevOps Lead responsible for ensuring project governance, stakeholder transparency, and risk mitigation in enterprise-grade data and infrastructure initiatives.\\r\\n\\r\\nContext & Vision\\r\\nThe goal is to document and track Risks, Assumptions, and Dependencies (RAD) to ensure project alignment and clear Acceptance Criteria. We need to proactively manage these factors within Azure DevOps to prevent roadblocks and ensure our delivery strategy is auditable and transparent.\\r\\n\\r\\nProject Configuration\\r\\nProject Type: [e.g., Data Platform Migration, Infrastructure Modernization, AI/ML Pipeline]\\r\\nTarget Management Tool: Azure DevOps (Boards/Wiki)\\r\\nRisk/Dependency Horizon: [e.g., Duration of the project, e.g., 6 months]\\r\\nStakeholders: [e.g., Data Engineering Team, Security/Compliance, Business Owners]\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements & Constraints\\r\\nPillar 1: Risk Management\\r\\nIdentify potential technical and operational risks (e.g., data quality issues, security vulnerabilities, resource constraints).\\r\\nDefine mitigation strategies and contingency plans for high-impact risks.\\r\\nPillar 2: Assumptions & Dependencies\\r\\nDocument key assumptions (e.g., source system availability, data format stability).\\r\\nMap technical and organizational dependencies (e.g., cross-team approvals, upstream pipeline readiness).\\r\\nPillar 3: Analysis Acceptance Criteria\\r\\nDefine measurable criteria for \\\"Acceptance\\\" for each risk mitigation or dependency resolution.\\r\\nEnsure clear ownership and \\\"Definition of Done\\\" for management tasks in Azure DevOps.\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput Format \\r\\nPlease provide the response in Markdown including:\\r\\nRAD Log Matrix: A table tracking Risks, Assumptions, and Dependencies with impact levels and ownership.\\r\\nAzure DevOps Strategy: Best practices for using Work Items (Risk/Issue types) and Wiki pages for tracking.\\r\\nMitigation/Resolution Workflow: A step-by-step process for moving a risk from \\\"Identified\\\" to \\\"Mitigated.\\\"\\r\\nAcceptance Criteria Template: A reusable checklist for validating that a dependency has been successfully resolved.\\r\\nReporting Framework: Key metrics to track (e.g., number of open dependencies, risk aging).\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent generates a Markdown-based governance framework for managing Risks, Assumptions, and Dependencies (RAD) within Azure DevOps to ensure transparency and controlled project delivery.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe output includes a RAD Log Matrix to track risks, assumptions, and dependencies with impact level, mitigation plan, and ownership. It also defines an Azure DevOps tracking strategy using Boards work items and Wiki documentation for centralized governance.\\r\\n\\r\\nAdditionally, it provides a risk mitigation workflow for moving items from identified → mitigated, a reusable Acceptance Criteria template to validate dependency resolution, and a reporting framework with key metrics such as open risks, dependency aging, and mitigation progress.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Create consolidated summary document of all analysis findings\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a Lead Data Architect. We have just completed the Data Analysis and Discovery phase for our upcoming data project. \\r\\n\\r\\nI have provided/attached several documents and notes from this phase (such as data profiling results, source-to-raw mappings, ER diagrams, and raw meeting notes). Please review the provided context and generate a consolidated Executive Summary document to present our findings to business stakeholders and technical leadership.\\r\\n\\r\\nPlease structure the document in Markdown format with the following sections:\\r\\n• **Executive Summary:** A high-level overview of the discovery phase and its outcome based on the provided context.\\r\\n• **Scope of Discovery:** The systems, core entities, and data volumes analyzed.\\r\\n• **Key Findings:** A categorized list of the most important discoveries extracted from the documents (e.g., Data Volume, Data Quality issues, Security/PII identified).\\r\\n• **Risks & Mitigations:** A table outlining any risks, blockers, or technical debt mentioned in the context, along with proposed mitigations.\\r\\n• **Next Steps:** Recommended actions to move into the Architecture & Design phase.\\r\\n\\r\\nEnsure the tone is professional, concise, and focused on business value and architectural readiness.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a consolidated Executive Summary document featuring a high-level overview of the discovery phase scope (systems and core entities), a categorized breakdown of key findings such as data volume and quality issues, and a structured table of architectural risks with proposed mitigations, along with clear next steps ready to be presented to business stakeholders and technical leadership.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Assess data migration complexity and risks\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as an expert System Architect. We are in the planning phase for an upcoming system/platform migration and need to assess the technical complexity and implementation risks based on our current architecture. \\r\\n\\r\\nHere is the context for our current system and the planned migration:\\r\\n- **Source Environment/Technology:** [e.g., On-Premises Monolithic Java Application / legacy REST API]\\r\\n- **Target Environment/Technology:** [e.g., AWS EKS Microservices / GraphQL API]\\r\\n- **Key Components/Integrations:** [e.g., 5 distinct modules, hardcoded Active Directory auth, cron-job batch processing, direct DB access]\\r\\n- **Known Pain Points/Legacy Patterns:** [e.g., Tightly coupled database connections, lack of test coverage, heavy reliance on local file storage]\\r\\n- **Migration Goals:** [e.g., Improve scalability, decouple components, zero downtime during switchover]\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on this context, please generate a comprehensive **Technical Migration Complexity & Risk Assessment** document.\\r\\n\\r\\nPlease structure the document in Markdown format with the following sections:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Executive Summary:** A high-level overview of the migration landscape and the overall technical complexity based on the provided inputs.\\r\\n• **Scope of Migration:** The source and target environments, core components, key integrations, and dependencies to be addressed.\\r\\n• **Key Findings:** A categorized list of structural discoveries derived from the context (e.g., architectural tight-coupling, deprecated dependencies, or legacy patterns).\\r\\n• **Risks & Mitigations:** A table outlining identified operational and technical risks (e.g., integration failures, untested code paths, or compatibility mismatches) along with proposed mitigations.\\r\\n• **Migration Strategy:** A recommended phased execution plan based on component dependencies, suggested validation steps for post-migration checks, and a safe rollback strategy.\\r\\n\\r\\nEnsure the tone is professional, concise, and focused on technical readiness.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a structured Markdown document featuring a high-level executive summary of the migration landscape, a mapped scope of the source-to-target environments, a categorized breakdown of architectural findings, a targeted table of technical risks with proposed mitigations, and a phased execution strategy including validation steps and a rollback plan based on your provided conte\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Perform dependency analysis across pipelines, notebooks and reports\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a Lead Data Engineer and Analytics Architect. We need to perform a comprehensive **Dependency Analysis and Data Lineage Mapping** across our existing data pipelines, notebooks, and BI reports to safely plan deployments and understand downstream impacts.\\r\\n\\r\\nHere is the context for our current data ecosystem:\\r\\n• **Orchestration / Pipelines:** [e.g., Apache Airflow / Azure Data Factory / dbt]\\r\\n• **Processing / Notebooks:** [e.g., Databricks (PySpark) / Snowflake Worksheets / Jupyter]\\r\\n• **Downstream Reporting:** [e.g., Power BI / Tableau / Looker]\\r\\n• **Key Core Models/Tables in focus:** [e.g., fact_sales, dim_customers, fact_daily_active_users]\\r\\n• **Goal of this Analysis:** [e.g., Impact analysis for changing the Customer ID format / Deprecating an old upstream ERP database / Documenting currently undocumented legacy pipelines]\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on this context, please generate a structured **Data Dependency and Lineage Assessment** document.\\r\\n\\r\\nPlease structure the document in Markdown format with the following sections:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Executive Summary:** A high-level overview of the dependency landscape, highlighting the critical nodes and potential bottlenecks.\\r\\n• **Upstream Dependencies (Ingestion & Pipelines):** Mapping of source systems, extraction pipelines, and raw data landings feeding the core models.\\r\\n• **Core Transformations (Notebooks & Logic):** A breakdown of the intermediate processing workflows, including scripts/notebooks that clean, join, and aggregate the data.\\r\\n• **Downstream Impacts (BI & Consumers):** A mapped list of dashboards, reports, ML models, and external APIs that rely on the final data products.\\r\\n• **Data Lineage Diagram (Mermaid.js):** A Mermaid.js flowchart code block visualizing the end-to-end data lineage, from upstream origin through intermediate transformations down to final consumption.\\r\\n\\r\\nEnsure the tone is highly analytical, focuses on impact mitigation, and clearly identifies single points of failure or highly coupled assets.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a structured lineage document that categorizes your data flow into upstream ingestion, core transformations (notebooks), and downstream reporting impacts. It will define clear impact radii for the specific tables/models you are focusing on and automatically generate a Mermaid.js flow diagram visualizing the end-to-end data path, which is highly useful for stakeholder reviews and documentation-is perfect for architecture wikis or impact review meetings and change-impact analysis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Determine optimal file partitioning keys to minimize data scanning and improve query performance in the data lake.\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"** Provide System Context here:**\\r\\n**For example:**\\r\\nTechnology used for example pyspark, sql.\\r\\nFrequent Query pattern - filtered by date and region.\\r\\nOccasional Query pattern - Customer Level data.\\r\\nIngestion volume - Billion\\r\\nFrequency - Daily\\r\\nIngestion Type - Batch Incremental.\\r\\nYears of data - 5\\r\\n\\r\\nAct as a senior data engineer designing the system as mentioned in system context. Queries are primarily filtered frequent query pattern available in context, with occasional queries on occasional query pattern. Design an optimal partitioning strategy that minimizes data scanning and improves query performance. Consider partition pruning, data skew, high vs low cardinality columns, small file issues, and optimal file sizes. Provide recommendations, trade-offs, and example implementation.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide recommendation for physical and logical partitioning, file format and z-ordering strategy along with the detailed structured view of the data storage and some starter implementation code.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Map authentication and authorization flows to define Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) levels for various user personas across the application stack.\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Senior Security Architect**. Design a comprehensive **Authentication (AuthN) and Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) flow** for a full-stack application and data platform. The goal is to ensure secure identity management and granular resource access across the entire stack.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide**:\\r\\n• **Authentication Provider**: {e.g., Azure Entra ID, Auth0, AWS Cognito}\\r\\n• **Application Stack**: {e.g., React Frontend, Node.js Backend, Snowflake/Synapse Data Warehouse}\\r\\n• **User Personas**: {e.g., Admin, Data Engineer, Business Analyst, Operational User}\\r\\n• **Security Standard**: {e.g., OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease provide a step-by-step security implementation guide including:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Authentication & Identity Flow**:\\r\\n1. Define the **Identity Provider (IdP)** integration flow for the frontend and backend using JWT (JSON Web Tokens).\\r\\n2. Explain the **Token Validation** logic (signature, issuer, and expiration checks) at the API Gateway or Backend layer.\\r\\n• **RBAC Matrix & Persona Mapping**:\\r\\n1. Create a **Permissions Matrix** mapping your User Personas to specific actions (Create, Read, Update, Delete) across UI elements and API endpoints.\\r\\n2. Show an example of how **Scopes/Claims** within the JWT are used to enforce these permissions.\\r\\n• **Data-Level Security (Row-Level/Column-Level)**:\\r\\n1. Provide a **SQL or PySpark snippet** demonstrating how to implement Row-Level Security (RLS) based on the user's authenticated identity.\\r\\n2. Explain how to mask sensitive columns for restricted personas.\\r\\n• **Backend & Frontend Enforcement**:\\r\\n1. Show a **Middleware code snippet** (e.g., in Node.js/Python) that intercepts requests to verify if a user has the required \\\"Role\\\" before executing the logic.\\r\\n2. Explain the strategy for **Conditional Rendering** in the frontend to hide UI elements based on the user's role.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**:\\r\\n• Use standard Markdown.\\r\\n• Include distinct blocks for Auth logic (Middleware) and SQL/RLS policies.\\r\\n• Provide a \\\"Security Audit Checklist\\\" at the end.\",\n      \"output\": \"Executing this prompt will generate a production-ready Security Specification that bridges user identity with technical access enforcement across the stack. The AI will produce JWT validation middleware, a cross-functional RBAC matrix, and SQL/PySpark Row-Level Security (RLS) policies. The final outcome is a unified security blueprint that ensures users only interact with data and features authorized for their specific persona, effectively mitigating the risk of unauthorized data exposure or privilege escalation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Define telemetry and event-tracking schemas to capture granular user interactions for product analytics and proactive error monitoring.\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a senior data engineer and product analytics expert.\\r\\n\\r\\nTask:\\r\\nDefine telemetry and event-tracking schemas to capture granular user interactions for product analytics and proactive error monitoring.\\r\\n\\r\\nSystem Context:\\r\\n**Provide product name, platform(s), domain, and key features**\\r\\n**Provide user types and high-level user journey (e.g., onboarding, login, search, checkout)**\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements:\\r\\n\\r\\n**Event Taxonomy**\\r\\n• Define a standardized naming convention for events (e.g., entity_action)\\r\\n• Categorize events into user events, system events, error events, and performance events\\r\\n• Ensure naming consistency across all platforms (web, mobile, backend)\\r\\n• Support extensibility for future features\\r\\n\\r\\n**Event Schema Definition**\\r\\nFor each event, define:\\r\\n• Event name\\r\\n• Description of what the event captures\\r\\n• Trigger condition (when the event fires)\\r\\n• Platform (web, mobile, backend, API)\\r\\n\\r\\n**Event Properties:**\\r\\n• user_id (string / UUID)\\r\\n• session_id (string)\\r\\n• timestamp (ISO 8601 format)\\r\\n• feature_name (string)\\r\\n• action_source (UI / API / background job)\\r\\n• additional properties:\\r\\n• **Add custom property 1 (type + description)**\\r\\n• **Add custom property 2 (type + description)**\\r\\n\\r\\n**Standard Metadata (applies to all events)**\\r\\n• app_version (string)\\r\\n• device_type (string)\\r\\n• OS_version (string)\\r\\n• location (optional)\\r\\n• network_type (WiFi / Cellular / Offline)\\r\\n\\r\\n**Error Tracking Schema**\\r\\nDefine a structured schema for capturing errors:\\r\\n• error_type (client / server / network)\\r\\n• error_code (string / number)\\r\\n• error_message (string)\\r\\n• stack_trace (optional)\\r\\n• impacted_feature (string)\\r\\n• severity (low / medium / high / critical)\\r\\n• retry_attempt (number)\\r\\n\\r\\n**Performance Monitoring Events**\\r\\nCapture system performance metrics such as:\\r\\n• API response time\\r\\n• page load time\\r\\n• latency\\r\\n• throughput\\r\\n\\r\\nExample fields:\\r\\n• response_time_ms (number)\\r\\n• request_id (string)\\r\\n• endpoint_name (string)\\r\\n\\r\\n**Data Governance & Best Practices**\\r\\n• Ensure idempotent event tracking (avoid duplicates)\\r\\n• Avoid unnecessary PII collection and ensure compliance\\r\\n• Maintain consistent schema versioning\\r\\n• Implement data validation checks\\r\\n• Define retention and archival policies\\r\\n• Ensure low-latency ingestion for real-time analytics\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Requirements**\\r\\n• Event taxonomy structure\\r\\n• At least 5–7 sample event definitions\\r\\n• JSON schema examples for events\\r\\n• Error event schema example\\r\\n• Performance event schema example\\r\\n• Recommendations for implementation tools (e.g., Segment, Mixpanel, GA4, Datadog)\\r\\n\\r\\n**Optional Custom Inputs**\\r\\n• Analytics tool preference: **Provide tool (e.g., Mixpanel / GA4 / Amplitude / Custom)**\\r\\n• Data warehouse: **Provide warehouse (e.g., Snowflake / BigQuery / Redshift)**\\r\\n• Real-time analytics requirement: **Yes / No**\\r\\n• Compliance requirements: **Provide (e.g., GDPR / HIPAA / None)**\\r\\n\\r\\nConstraints:\\r\\n• Design for distributed systems\\r\\n• Handle high event volume (millions of events per day)\\r\\n• Ensure scalability and low-latency ingestion\\r\\n• Support schema evolution without breaking pipelines\\r\\n• Avoid vague or generic definitions\",\n      \"output\": \"Event Taxonomy\\r\\n\\r\\nNaming Convention: entity_action\\r\\n\\r\\nCategories:\\r\\n\\r\\nuser_login\\r\\n\\r\\nuser_signup\\r\\n\\r\\nproduct_view\\r\\n\\r\\ncart_add\\r\\n\\r\\norder_create\\r\\n\\r\\nsystem_error\\r\\n\\r\\napi_response\\r\\n\\r\\nSample Event Definitions\\r\\nEvent 1: user_login\\r\\n\\r\\nDescription: Triggered when user logs into the system\\r\\n\\r\\nTrigger: Successful authentication\\r\\n\\r\\nPlatform: Web / Mobile\\r\\n\\r\\nEvent 2: product_view\\r\\n\\r\\nDescription: User views a product page\\r\\n\\r\\nTrigger: Page load\\r\\n\\r\\nPlatform: Web\\r\\n\\r\\nEvent 3: cart_add\\r\\n\\r\\nDescription: User adds item to cart\\r\\n\\r\\nTrigger: Click on \\\"Add to Cart\\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nPlatform: Web / Mobile\\r\\n\\r\\nEvent 4: order_create\\r\\n\\r\\nDescription: User places an order\\r\\n\\r\\nTrigger: Checkout success\\r\\n\\r\\nPlatform: Backend\\r\\n\\r\\nEvent 5: system_error\\r\\n\\r\\nDescription: Application error occurred\\r\\n\\r\\nTrigger: Exception thrown\\r\\n\\r\\nPlatform: Backend\\r\\n\\r\\nJSON Event Schema Example\\r\\n{\\r\\n  \\\"event_name\\\": \\\"product_view\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"user_id\\\": \\\"12345\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"session_id\\\": \\\"abc123\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-03-19T12:30:00Z\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"feature_name\\\": \\\"product_page\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"action_source\\\": \\\"UI\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"product_id\\\": \\\"P987\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"category\\\": \\\"electronics\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"app_version\\\": \\\"1.0.0\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"device_type\\\": \\\"mobile\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"OS_version\\\": \\\"Android 14\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"network_type\\\": \\\"WiFi\\\"\\r\\n}\\r\\nError Event Schema\\r\\n{\\r\\n  \\\"event_name\\\": \\\"system_error\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"error_type\\\": \\\"server\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"error_code\\\": \\\"500\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"error_message\\\": \\\"Internal Server Error\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"stack_trace\\\": \\\"NullPointerException...\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"impacted_feature\\\": \\\"checkout\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"severity\\\": \\\"critical\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"retry_attempt\\\": 2,\\r\\n  \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-03-19T12:35:00Z\\\"\\r\\n}\\r\\nPerformance Event Schema\\r\\n{\\r\\n  \\\"event_name\\\": \\\"api_response\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"endpoint_name\\\": \\\"/api/v1/orders\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"response_time_ms\\\": 320,\\r\\n  \\\"request_id\\\": \\\"req-789\\\",\\r\\n  \\\"status_code\\\": 200,\\r\\n  \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-03-19T12:36:00Z\\\"\\r\\n}\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Perform a query-level cost analysis to identify inefficient SQL or Spark operations that may cause unpredicted spikes in cloud consumption.\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a Lead Data Engineer and Cloud Cost Optimization Specialist. I am providing the repository context / codebase for our data solution, which includes our data pipelines, transformation notebooks, SQL scripts, Delta MERGE operations, and schema definitions.\\r\\n \\r\\nPlease review this supplied context to perform a query-level cost analysis identifying inefficient SQL or Spark operations that may cause unpredicted spikes in cloud consumption.\\r\\n \\r\\nPlease structure the document in Markdown format with the following sections:\\r\\n \\r\\nExecutive Summary: A high-level overview of the cost-risk posture across the codebase and a required note stating: \\\"This automated cost analysis was compiled from repository artifacts (notebooks, SQL scripts, Spark jobs, and pipeline definitions); final validation must be completed by platform engineers and domain data owners.\\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nLayer-by-Layer Cost Analysis: A breakdown of the operations and code identifying cost risks at each stage:\\r\\n\\r\\nSource/Ingestion (Bronze): Identify extraction queries using full-reload patterns, unbounded SELECT * without partition filters, or redundant data pulls from source systems.\\r\\n\\r\\nRaw → Curated (Silver): Flag expensive JOIN operations, wide shuffles, CROSS JOINs, repeated full-table scans, transformations lacking predicate pushdown, and any MERGE INTO statements missing merge-key selectivity.\\r\\n\\r\\nCurated → Semantic (Gold): Detect heavy aggregations, non-optimized GROUP BY operations, redundant re-computations of intermediate results, and missing caching or materialization of frequently reused DataFrames.\\r\\n\\r\\nPipeline Orchestration & Scheduling: Review pipeline scheduling cadence for overlapping concurrent runs, unnecessary sequential dependencies, idle compute windows, and jobs running at peak-cost time slots.\\r\\n\\r\\nFlagged Operations Detail: For each flagged query or operation, provide:\\r\\n\\r\\nNotebook / Script path and cell or step reference\\r\\n\\r\\nOperation type (SQL query, Spark transformation, Delta MERGE, DataFrame action, etc.)\\r\\n\\r\\nCost concern category (full scan, shuffle-heavy, unbounded join, redundant recompute, missing partition pruning, schema evolution overhead, over-provisioned schedule)\\r\\n\\r\\nSeverity (High / Medium / Low) based on potential consumption impact\\r\\n\\r\\nRecommended optimization (add filters, convert to incremental load, repartition, cache intermediate results, narrow column selection, adjust schedule, etc.)\\r\\n\\r\\nConsolidated Risk Summary Table: A table listing all flagged operations ranked by estimated cost impact, with columns: Notebook/Script Path, Operation, Concern Category, Severity, and Recommendation.\\r\\n\\r\\nProcessing Cadence Review: A schedule-level analysis identifying notebooks or pipelines that run more frequently than data freshness requires, or that execute during peak-cost windows, with suggested cadence adjustments.\\r\\n\\r\\nCost Hotspot Visualization (Mermaid.js): A Mermaid.js horizontal flowchart (graph LR) code block visually mapping the data flow and annotating the high-severity cost hotspots you identified — highlighting which notebooks, queries, and tables contribute most to consumption spikes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Draft an OpenAPI file to document request and response bodies for a specific endpoint.\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a senior backend engineer and API architect specializing in RESTful API design and OpenAPI specifications.\\r\\n\\r\\nTask:\\r\\nDraft a complete OpenAPI (Swagger) specification file documenting request and response bodies for a given API endpoint.\\r\\n\\r\\nSystem Context:\\r\\n**Provide API name, domain, and purpose of the API**\\r\\n**Provide base URL / server information (e.g., dev, staging, prod)**\\r\\n\\r\\nEndpoint Details:\\r\\n• Endpoint path: **/api/v1/{resource}**\\r\\n• HTTP method: **GET / POST / PUT / DELETE**\\r\\n• Description: **Describe what this endpoint does**\\r\\n• Version: **v1 / v2**\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **OpenAPI Structure**\\r\\n• Use OpenAPI version 3.x\\r\\n• Include info section (title, description, version)\\r\\n• Define servers (dev, staging, production if applicable)\\r\\n• Organize paths and components properly\\r\\n\\r\\n2. **Request Definition**\\r\\n• Define path parameters:\\r\\n• **parameter_name (type, required, description)**\\r\\n• Define query parameters:\\r\\n• **parameter_name (type, required/optional, description)**\\r\\n• Define headers (if applicable):\\r\\n• Authorization, Content-Type, etc.\\r\\n\\r\\nRequest Body:\\r\\n• Content type: application/json\\r\\n• Schema definition with:\\r\\n• **field_name (type, required, description)**\\r\\n• **nested objects / arrays if applicable**\\r\\n• Include example payload\\r\\n\\r\\n3. **Response Definition**\\r\\nDefine multiple response scenarios:\\r\\n\\r\\n• 200 / 201 Success Response:\\r\\n• Schema with fields:\\r\\n• **field_name (type, description)**\\r\\n• Example response\\r\\n\\r\\n• 4xx Client Error Response:\\r\\n• error_code\\r\\n• error_message\\r\\n• validation_errors (if applicable)\\r\\n\\r\\n• 5xx Server Error Response:\\r\\n• error_code\\r\\n• error_message\\r\\n\\r\\n4. **Schema Components (Reusable Models)**\\r\\n• Define reusable schemas under components:\\r\\n• Request model\\r\\n• Response model\\r\\n• Error model\\r\\n• Use proper data types (string, integer, boolean, array, object)\\r\\n• Include format (date-time, email, uuid, etc.)\\r\\n\\r\\n5. **Validation & Constraints**\\r\\n• Required vs optional fields\\r\\n• String length, enum values, patterns\\r\\n• Numeric ranges\\r\\n• Default values\\r\\n\\r\\n6. **Security Definition**\\r\\n• Authentication type: **Bearer Token / API Key / OAuth2**\\r\\n• Define security schemes under components\\r\\n• Apply security at endpoint level\\r\\n\\r\\n7. **Best Practices**\\r\\n• Follow RESTful naming conventions\\r\\n• Keep schema consistent and reusable\\r\\n• Include meaningful descriptions for all fields\\r\\n• Avoid ambiguity in request/response structures\\r\\n• Ensure backward compatibility for future versions\\r\\n\\r\\n8. **Output Requirements**\\r\\n• Complete OpenAPI YAML or JSON file\\r\\n• Well-structured and ready for tools like Swagger UI\\r\\n• Include at least one fully defined endpoint\\r\\n• Include request, response, and error schemas\\r\\n• Include example payloads\\r\\n\\r\\n9. **Optional Custom Inputs**\\r\\n• Authentication method: **Provide (e.g., JWT, OAuth2, API Key)**\\r\\n• Pagination required: **Yes / No**\\r\\n• Rate limiting details: **Provide limits (e.g., 100 req/min)**\\r\\n• Versioning strategy: **URI / Header-based**\\r\\n\\r\\nConstraints:\\r\\n• Follow OpenAPI 3.x specification strictly\\r\\n• Ensure schema is production-ready\\r\\n• Avoid vague or incomplete definitions\\r\\n• Maintain consistency across request and response models\\r\\n• Support extensibility for future endpoints\",\n      \"output\": \"openapi: 3.0.3\\r\\ninfo:\\r\\n  title: E-Commerce Order API\\r\\n  description: API for managing customer orders\\r\\n  version: 1.0.0\\r\\n\\r\\nservers:\\r\\n  - url: https://api.example.com/v1\\r\\n    description: Production server\\r\\n\\r\\npaths:\\r\\n  /orders:\\r\\n    post:\\r\\n      summary: Create a new order\\r\\n      description: Creates an order for a user\\r\\n      security:\\r\\n        - bearerAuth: []\\r\\n      requestBody:\\r\\n        required: true\\r\\n        content:\\r\\n          application/json:\\r\\n            schema:\\r\\n              $ref: '#/components/schemas/OrderRequest'\\r\\n            example:\\r\\n              user_id: \\\"12345\\\"\\r\\n              items:\\r\\n                - product_id: \\\"P987\\\"\\r\\n                  quantity: 2\\r\\n              total_amount: 1500\\r\\n\\r\\n      responses:\\r\\n        '201':\\r\\n          description: Order created successfully\\r\\n          content:\\r\\n            application/json:\\r\\n              schema:\\r\\n                $ref: '#/components/schemas/OrderResponse'\\r\\n              example:\\r\\n                order_id: \\\"ORD123\\\"\\r\\n                status: \\\"created\\\"\\r\\n                total_amount: 1500\\r\\n\\r\\n        '400':\\r\\n          description: Bad request\\r\\n          content:\\r\\n            application/json:\\r\\n              schema:\\r\\n                $ref: '#/components/schemas/ErrorResponse'\\r\\n              example:\\r\\n                error_code: \\\"400\\\"\\r\\n                error_message: \\\"Invalid input\\\"\\r\\n\\r\\n        '500':\\r\\n          description: Server error\\r\\n          content:\\r\\n            application/json:\\r\\n              schema:\\r\\n                $ref: '#/components/schemas/ErrorResponse'\\r\\n\\r\\ncomponents:\\r\\n  schemas:\\r\\n    OrderRequest:\\r\\n      type: object\\r\\n      required:\\r\\n        - user_id\\r\\n        - items\\r\\n      properties:\\r\\n        user_id:\\r\\n          type: string\\r\\n        items:\\r\\n          type: array\\r\\n          items:\\r\\n            type: object\\r\\n            properties:\\r\\n              product_id:\\r\\n                type: string\\r\\n              quantity:\\r\\n                type: integer\\r\\n        total_amount:\\r\\n          type: number\\r\\n\\r\\n    OrderResponse:\\r\\n      type: object\\r\\n      properties:\\r\\n        order_id:\\r\\n          type: string\\r\\n        status:\\r\\n          type: string\\r\\n        total_amount:\\r\\n          type: number\\r\\n\\r\\n    ErrorResponse:\\r\\n      type: object\\r\\n      properties:\\r\\n        error_code:\\r\\n          type: string\\r\\n        error_message:\\r\\n          type: string\\r\\n\\r\\n  securitySchemes:\\r\\n    bearerAuth:\\r\\n      type: http\\r\\n      scheme: bearer\\r\\n      bearerFormat: JWT\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Build end‑to‑end lineage map across source → raw → curated → semantic (tables, pipelines, notebooks, reports)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a Lead Data Engineer and Metadata Architect. I am providing the repository context / codebase for our data solution, which includes our data pipelines, transformation scripts, and schema definitions. \\r\\n\\r\\nPlease review this supplied context to generate an end-to-end lineage map for this solution and list the dependencies per layer (across Source → Raw → Curated → Semantic).\\r\\n\\r\\nPlease structure the document in Markdown format with the following sections:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Executive Summary:** A high-level overview of the data flow and a required note stating: *\\\"This automated lineage map was compiled from repository artifacts; final validation must be completed by domain data owners and engineers.\\\"*\\r\\n• **Layer-by-Layer Dependencies:** A breakdown of the assets and code identifying what happens at each stage:\\r\\n   1. **Source/Ingestion:** Identified source systems and extraction jobs.\\r\\n   2. **Raw/Bronze:** Landing tables, storage paths, or raw schemas.\\r\\n   3. **Curated/Silver:** The specific notebooks, scripts, or pipelines performing data cleaning and joins.\\r\\n   4. **Semantic/Gold:** The final aggregated tables or views intended for reporting.\\r\\n• **Downstream Consumption:** Any identified BI reports, dashboards, or external systems relying on the final semantic layer.\\r\\n• **End-to-End Lineage Flow (Mermaid.js):** A Mermaid.js horizontal flowchart (`graph LR`) code block visually mapping the exact tables, scripts, and views you identified in the codebase from origin to destination.\\r\\n\\r\\nEnsure the tone is technical, trace-oriented, and accurately reflects only the files and code provided in the context.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will scan the codebase context you provide and automatically generate a structured documentation page. This document will trace the flow of data through your medallion architecture, explicitly numbering the SQL scripts, Python notebooks, and source tables functioning at the Ingestion, Raw, Curated, and Semantic layers. Finally, it will output a visual Mermaid.js flow diagram illustrating the exact dependencies—from raw data landing to final BI consumption—ready to be pasted into your engineering wiki, while responsibly noting that engineers must validate the auto-generated lineage derived lineage requires human validation is still required.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Identify M:M relationships and decide bridge table strategy (grain, keys, handling duplicates)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Provide System Context here:**\\r\\n\\r\\nSchema type: Star Schema\\r\\nBusiness domain: sales\\r\\nFact table(s): Sales, Orders, Claims\\r\\nDimension tables: Product, Customer, Promotion, Category\\r\\n\\r\\nKnown or suspected M:M relationships:\\r\\n\\r\\nProduct ↔ Category\\r\\nOrders ↔ Promotion\\r\\nCustomer ↔ Account\\r\\nAct as a senior data architect working on the system described above.\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on the schema type and relationships provided, identify all many-to-many (M:M) relationships between fact and dimension tables or between dimensions.\\r\\n\\r\\nDesign an appropriate bridge table strategy that includes:\\r\\n\\r\\nClearly defining the grain of each bridge table\\r\\nIdentifying primary keys and foreign keys\\r\\nDeciding whether surrogate keys are required\\r\\nHandling duplicate relationships and ensuring data integrity\",\n      \"output\": \"It will generate strategies for creating the bridge table and how can we fit in current modelling along with some started code.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Decide how to handle schema drift (additive changes, type changes, breaking changes)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a Principal Data Engineer and Architect specializing in Modern Data Lakehouse architectures. Your task is to design a robust Schema Drift and Compatibility Strategy to ensure pipeline resilience and data integrity across an evolving data ecosystem.\\r\\n\\r\\nContext to Provide:\\r\\n• Storage Format: {e.g., Delta Lake, Parquet, Avro}\\r\\n• Ingestion Engine: {e.g., Azure Data Factory, Spark Structured Streaming, Fabric Data Factory}\\r\\n• Source Systems: {e.g., NoSQL APIs with fluid schemas, SQL Databases, IoT JSON Payloads}\\r\\n• Downstream Consumer: {e.g., Power BI, ML Models, SQL Analytics}\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements:\\r\\nPlease provide a comprehensive technical strategy for managing schema evolution, including:\\r\\n\\r\\n• Drift Handling Policies:\\r\\n1. Define the rules for Additive Changes (New columns): When to allow \\\"Auto-Merge\\\" vs. when to enforce explicit schema updates.\\r\\n2. Define the strategy for Breaking Changes (Renamed columns, Deleted columns, or Data Type changes): Explain the use of \\\"Dead Letter Queues\\\" or pipeline halts to prevent data corruption.\\r\\n• Technical Implementation (Spark/Delta):\\r\\n1. Provide a PySpark code snippet demonstrating how to enable mergeSchema vs. overwriteSchema and how to catch AnalysisException during drift.\\r\\n2. Explain how to implement a Schema Validator function that checks incoming data against a registered \\\"Contract\\\" before it reaches the Silver layer.\\r\\n• Compatibility Evolution Rules:\\r\\n1. Define the compatibility mode for the architecture (e.g., Backward Compatible, Forward Compatible, or Full).\\r\\n2. Suggest a strategy for Schema Versioning (e.g., using a Schema Registry or a version metadata column) to track changes over time.\\r\\n• Alerting & Governance:\\r\\n1. Propose an Automated Alerting Workflow (e.g., via Azure Monitor or Teams) that triggers whenever a schema change is detected but not yet approved.\\r\\n2. Create a Schema Change Matrix mapping change types to their allowed impact level (e.g., \\\"Auto-Approve\\\" for Additive vs. \\\"Manual Review\\\" for Type changes).\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput Format:\\r\\n• Use standard Markdown.\\r\\n• Include distinct blocks for PySpark/SQL logic.\\r\\n• Provide a \\\"Schema Drift Response Checklist\\\" for Data Engineers at the end.\",\n      \"output\": \"Executing this prompt will generate a technical governance framework that safeguards your data platform against unexpected upstream changes. The AI will produce PySpark validation logic, a versioning strategy, and a compatibility matrix that distinguishes between safe and breaking changes. The final outcome is a proactive drift policy that prevents pipeline failures and ensures that downstream analytics remain stable even as source data evolves, reducing manual troubleshooting time.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Analysis\",\n      \"task\": \"Evaluate need for aggregations, calculation groups, composite models, and query reduction patterns\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Review semantic model design and suggest optimizations for performance and maintainability\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"Architecture\": [\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Architecture\",\n      \"task\": \"Define data lakehouse architecture (medallion - bronze/silver/gold layers)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"** Provide System Context about sources and transformation to generate more acurate recoomendations**\\r\\nAct as a data engineer and design a modern data lakehouse architecture using the Medallion pattern. Clearly explain the Bronze, Silver, and Gold layers, including the type of data stored in each layer, data transformations performed, data quality improvements, and the typical users of each layer. Also describe the data flow from ingestion to analytics and provide examples of datasets in each stage.\",\n      \"output\": \"It generates detailed document for medaalion architecture with examples and the flow for understanding.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Architecture\",\n      \"task\": \"Design workspace architecture for Fabric (workspace per environment vs shared)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a solution architect and design a Microsoft Fabric workspace architecture for an enterprise data platform **Provide Your System Context here** .\\r\\nCompare workspace-per-environment (Dev, Test, Prod) and shared workspace approaches. Explain architecture design, advantages, disadvantages, security considerations, access control, CI/CD integration, and when to use each model. Provide a recommendation based on scalability and governance needs.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide structured information on isolated vs shared workspace architectures with all the details from security to deployment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Architecture\",\n      \"task\": \"Define Semantic model architecture and data modeling strategy\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as an expert Data Architect and Analytics Engineer. We are in the design phase for our analytics platform and need to define a robust **Semantic Model Architecture and Data Modeling Strategy**.\\r\\n\\r\\nHere is the context for our business domain and data requirements:\\r\\n- **Business Domain:** [e.g., E-commerce Retail / Healthcare Claims / SaaS Subscription Metrics]\\r\\n- **Target BI/Analytics Platform:** [e.g., Power BI / Looker / Tableau]\\r\\n- **Key Business Events (Facts):** [e.g., Orders placed, Payments processed, User logins, Subscriptions canceled]\\r\\n- **Core Entities (Dimensions):** [e.g., Customers, Products, Time, Geography, Store Locations]\\r\\n- **Data Complexities/Pain Points:** [e.g., Products change categories over time, complex Many-to-Many relationships between Users and Accounts, slow query performance on current flat tables]\\r\\n- **Primary Consumers:** [e.g., Financial analysts needing granular data, Execs needing high-level daily dashboards]\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on this context, please generate a comprehensive **Semantic Model Architecture & Data Modeling Strategy** document.\\r\\n\\r\\nPlease structure the document in Markdown format with the following sections:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Executive Summary:** A high-level overview of the proposed semantic layer architecture and how it addresses the stated business needs and pain points.\\r\\n• **Modeling Strategy Recommendation:** A recommended modeling approach (e.g., Star Schema, Snowflake, Data Vault, or OBT) with the rationale for why it fits this specific BI platform and use case.\\r\\n• **Core Facts & Dimensions:** A mapped breakdown of the central Fact tables (metrics, granularity) and surrounding Dimension tables (attributes, hierarchies) derived from the inputs.\\r\\n• **Design Patterns for Complexities:** Specific recommendations for handling the identified pain points, such as Slowly Changing Dimensions (SCDs), role-playing dimensions, or bridge tables for many-to-many relationships.\\r\\n• **Conceptual Architecture (Mermaid.js):** A Mermaid.js ER diagram code block visualizing the proposed conceptual data model so I can copy/paste it into Confluence.\\r\\n\\r\\nEnsure the tone is professional, concise, and focused on self-service analytics readiness, governance, and query performance.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a structured Markdown document featuring a high-level executive summary of the semantic layer, a concrete modeling strategy recommendation tailored to your BI tool (e.g., Star Schema for Power BI), a categorized list of proposed Fact and Dimension tables based on your inputs, specific architectural solutions for your stated data complexities (like SCD types or bridge tables), and a Mermaid.js ER diagram visualizing the proposed data relationships.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Architecture\",\n      \"task\": \"Define trade-offs between performance, cost, and complexity\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Cloud Cost Architect and Data Engineer** specialized in Azure Data Platforms. Your task is to design a **Capacity Planning and Cost Optimization Strategy** for a high-performance data ecosystem. The goal is to maximize query performance while maintaining strict budget governance through a tiered complexity model.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide**:\\r\\n• **Estimated Data Volume**: {e.g., 5TB Monthly Growth / 500 Million Rows}\\r\\n• **Workload Profile**: {e.g., Heavy Batch Ingestion (Nightly) / Ad-hoc Analytical Queries / Real-time Power BI DirectQuery}\\r\\n• **Primary Compute**: {e.g., Databricks Serverless / Fabric F-SKU Capacities / Synapse Dedicated Pools}\\r\\n• **Budget Constraints**: {e.g., Target $<X,000$ per month / Reduce cost by 20%}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease provide a comprehensive cost-performance trade-off analysis including:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Compute Sizing & Scaling Strategy**:\\r\\n    1. Define the trade-offs between **Small, Medium, and Large clusters** for Databricks and Synapse based on execution speed vs. cost.\\r\\n    2. Propose a **Scaling Policy** (Autoscaling vs. Fixed Capacity) for Fabric and ADF Integration Runtimes to handle peak vs. off-peak loads.\\r\\n• **Storage & Data Lifecycle Optimization**:\\r\\n    1. Recommend a strategy for **Data Compaction and Vacuuming** in Delta Lake to reduce storage costs and metadata overhead.\\r\\n    2. Explain the use of **Cold/Archive tiers** for historical data and how to automate the transition using ADF or Fabric policies.\\r\\n• **Power BI & Resource Governance**:\\r\\n    1. Analyze the cost impact of **Import Mode vs. DirectQuery** in Power BI and suggest a hybrid approach (Aggregations) to optimize capacity usage.\\r\\n    2. Suggest **Resource Tags and Spending Alerts** to monitor costs at a per-project or per-environment level.\\r\\n• **Complexity vs. Cost Matrix**:\\r\\n    1. Create a table comparing a **\\\"Performance-First\\\"** architecture vs. a **\\\"Cost-Optimized\\\"** architecture, highlighting where complexity increases to save money (e.g., manual caching, pre-aggregated tables).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**:\\r\\n• Use standard Markdown.\\r\\n• Include a comparison table for Performance vs. Cost trade-offs.\\r\\n• Provide a \\\"Cost Optimization Checklist\\\" for monthly reviews at the end.\",\n      \"output\": \"Prompt will generate a strategic financial and technical roadmap for your cloud infrastructure. The AI will produce compute sizing recommendations, a tiered storage lifecycle policy, and a cost-complexity trade-off matrix. The final outcome is a data-driven capacity plan that allows the engineering team to make informed \\\"Business Judgement\\\" decisions on where to invest in performance and where to optimize for budget, preventing unexpected cloud-spend spikes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Architecture\",\n      \"task\": \"Define disaster recovery and business continuity architecture\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Principal Infrastructure Architect and Disaster Recovery Specialist**. Your task is to design a robust **Disaster Recovery (DR) and Business Continuity (BC) Architecture** for a mission-critical Azure Data Platform. The objective is to ensure minimal data loss and rapid service restoration across multiple Azure regions.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide**:\\r\\n• **Primary Region**: {e.g., East US}\\r\\n• **Secondary/DR Region**: {e.g., West US}\\r\\n• **Critical Services**: {e.g., Databricks, Fabric, Synapse, ADF, Power BI}\\r\\n• **Required RPO (Recovery Point Objective)**: {e.g., 15 Minutes / 1 Hour}\\r\\n• **Required RTO (Recovery Time Objective)**: {e.g., 2 Hours / 4 Hours}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease provide a high-level architectural failover and recovery guide including:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Regional Failover Architecture**:\\r\\n    1. Define the strategy for **Storage Redundancy** (e.g., GRS vs. ZRS) to ensure Data Lake availability in the secondary region.\\r\\n    2. Outline the failover mechanism for **ADF Integration Runtimes** and how to redirect pipelines to the secondary workspace.\\r\\n• **Stateful Service Recovery (Databricks/Synapse)**:\\r\\n    1. Explain the process for replicating **Databricks Metastore** and workspace configurations (notebooks/clusters) to the DR region.\\r\\n    2. Propose a recovery path for **Synapse Dedicated SQL Pools** including geo-backup restoration steps.\\r\\n• **Business Continuity for Analytics (Power BI/Fabric)**:\\r\\n    1. Define how **Microsoft Fabric** capacities are managed during a regional outage and the steps to re-bind reports to a secondary capacity.\\r\\n    2. Suggest a strategy for maintaining **Gateway connectivity** if on-premises sources are part of the architecture.\\r\\n• **Recovery Orchestration & Automation**:\\r\\n    1. Design a \\\"Disaster Recovery Workflow\\\" using **Azure Site Recovery** or custom DevOps scripts to automate the spin-up of compute resources in the DR site.\\r\\n    2. Provide a **Post-DR Data Integrity Check** script (SQL or Python) to verify that the RPO has been met after failover.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**:\\r\\n• Use standard Markdown.\\r\\n• Include a visual-style process flow for Regional Failover.\\r\\n• Provide a \\\"Business Continuity Checklist\\\" for stakeholders at the end.\",\n      \"output\": \"This prompt will generate a comprehensive Disaster Recovery and Business Continuity Manual tailored to your specific Azure stack. The AI will produce regional failover procedures, metastore replication strategies, and Power BI report re-binding logic. The final outcome is a structured architectural blueprint that ensures your data platform remains resilient against regional outages, providing clear technical steps to meet your business-defined RTO and RPO targets.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Architecture\",\n      \"task\": \"Design compute vs storage separation strategy\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as an expert Cloud Data Architect. We are in the architectural redesign phase and need to define a **Compute vs. Storage Separation Strategy** to improve scalability, reduce costs, and modernize our data platform/system.\\r\\n\\r\\nHere is the context for our current infrastructure and workloads:\\r\\n• **Current Architecture:** [e.g., On-premises Hadoop cluster / A large monolithic PostgreSQL database on a single massive VM / Tightly coupled EC2 instances with local EBS volumes]\\r\\n• **Workload Characteristics:** [e.g., Heavy nightly batch ETL jobs / High-volume real-time streaming ingestion / Intermittent heavy analytical queries at month-end]\\r\\n• **Current Pain Points:** [e.g., Paying for max compute 24/7 even during idle times / Running out of disk space requires upgrading the entire CPU tier / Difficult to scale compute for sudden reporting spikes]\\r\\n• **Target Environment/Cloud:** [e.g., AWS (S3, Athena, EMR) / Azure (ADLS, Databricks) / Snowflake / GCP (GCS, BigQuery)]\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on this context, please generate a comprehensive **Compute vs. Storage Separation Strategy** document.\\r\\n\\r\\nPlease structure the document in Markdown format with the following sections:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Executive Summary:** A high-level overview of the proposed decoupled architecture and how it directly resolves the stated pain points around cost and elasticity.\\r\\n• **Storage Architecture:** Recommendations for the centralized, persistent storage layer (e.g., Object storage like S3/ADLS, open table formats like Delta/Iceberg) ensuring high durability and low cost.\\r\\n• **Compute Architecture:** Recommendations for the processing layer (e.g., ephemeral clusters, serverless query engines, auto-scaling data warehouses) to handle the specific workloads dynamically.\\r\\n• **Migration & Decoupling Strategy:** A phased execution plan detailing how to migrate from the current tightly coupled state to the target separated state securely and efficiently.\\r\\n• **Target Architecture Diagram (Mermaid.js):** A Mermaid.js flowchart code block visualizing the decoupled flow of data between the shared storage layer and the independent, scalable compute engines.\\r\\n\\r\\nEnsure the tone is professional, concise, and focused on cost-efficiency, elasticity, and modern cloud-native principles.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a structured Markdown document featuring a high-level executive summary of the decoupled architecture, specific technology recommendations for an independent storage tier (like object storage/data lakes) and an elastic compute tier (like serverless or auto-scaling clusters) tailored to your cloud provider, a step-by-step decoupling migration plan, and a Mermaid.js flowchart visualizing the new separated layers that is ready to be pasted into your architectural documentation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Architecture\",\n      \"task\": \"Design cross-workspace sharing and collaboration patterns\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as an Enterprise Data Architect and BI Platform Owner. We are scaling our analytics platform and need to design robust **Cross-Workspace Sharing and Collaboration Patterns** to prevent data silos, reduce duplication, and govern access across different business units.\\r\\n\\r\\nHere is the context for our organizational structure and toolset:\\r\\n• **Core Platform/Technology:** [e.g., Power BI Service / Databricks Unity Catalog / AWS Data Mesh]\\r\\n• **Key Teams/Domains involved:** [e.g., Central Data Engineering, Finance, Global Sales, Regional Marketing]\\r\\n• **Current Pain Points:** [e.g., Every team creates their own siloed datasets, business logic is duplicated, and we lack a single source of truth]\\r\\n• **Data Sensitivity & Governance:** [e.g., High security required; Finance data must be isolated, but aggregated sales data must be shared globally]\\r\\n• **Desired Target State:** [e.g., A Hub-and-Spoke model where the central team provides certified datasets and domain teams build their own reports]\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on this context, please generate a comprehensive **Cross-Workspace Sharing and Collaboration Architecture** document.\\r\\n\\r\\nPlease structure the document in Markdown format with the following sections:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Executive Summary:** A high-level overview of the proposed collaboration architecture and how it resolves the stated pain points (e.g., moving to a Hub-and-Spoke or Data Mesh model).\\r\\n• **Workspace Topology:** A recommended structure for the workspaces/domains (e.g., separating Data/Engineering workspaces from Report/Consumption workspaces).\\r\\n• **Publishing & Endorsement Workflow:** The lifecycle of a dataset from development to a \\\"certified/promoted\\\" shared asset ready for cross-team consumption.\\r\\n• **Security & Access Controls:** Best practices for managing access (RBAC), implementing Row-Level Security (RLS) across boundaries, and data cataloging strategies.\\r\\n• **Collaboration Architecture (Mermaid.js):** A Mermaid.js flowchart code block visualizing the flow of data from central data workspaces out to domain-specific consumption workspaces.\\r\\n\\r\\nEnsure the tone is professional, governance-focused, and actionable for administrative setup.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a structured Markdown document outlining a comprehensive governance strategy tailored to your chosen toolset. It will include a recommended workspace topology (like a Hub-and-Spoke model), a clear workflow for certifying and publishing datasets across team boundaries, strategies for role-based access and data security (like RLS)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Architecture\",\n      \"task\": \"Define what belongs in Lakehouse vs Warehouse vs Eventhouse and separation rules per domain\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Gemini said\\r\\nAct as a Principal Data Architect specializing in Microsoft Fabric. Your task is to design a Multi-Workload Separation Strategy to define exactly how data should be routed between Lakehouse, Data Warehouse, and Eventhouse based on specific domain use cases and technical requirements.\\r\\n\\r\\nContext to Provide:\\r\\n• Data Sources: {e.g., IoT Streaming, ERP (SQL), Unstructured Logs, Excel/Files}\\r\\n• Primary Use Cases: {e.g., Machine Learning, Financial Reporting, Real-time Dashboarding}\\r\\n• Technical Persona: {e.g., Data Scientists (Python/Spark), SQL Analysts (T-SQL), Citizen Developers}\\r\\n• Performance Requirement: {e.g., Sub-second latency for streaming vs. high-volume batch processing}\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements:\\r\\nPlease provide a comprehensive decision-making framework and separation guide including:\\r\\n\\r\\n• Workload Selection Logic:\\r\\n1. Define the technical criteria for choosing Lakehouse (Spark/Delta) vs. Data Warehouse (SQL) for transformation and gold-layer storage.\\r\\n2. Identify the specific triggers for using Eventhouse (KQL) for high-velocity, real-time telemetry data.\\r\\n• Cross-Domain Separation Rules:\\r\\n1. Design a Workspace Architecture that separates domains (e.g., Finance, Sales, IoT) while maintaining a shared \\\"OneLake\\\" foundation.\\r\\n2. Explain how to manage Data Shortcuts between workloads to minimize data duplication across different engines.\\r\\n• Access & Governance Model:\\r\\n1. Recommend the optimal Compute Engine for different user personas (e.g., Spark for Data Science, SQL Endpoint for Analytics).\\r\\n2. Define how to handle Schema Governance and evolution differently across the Lakehouse and the Warehouse.\\r\\n• Implementation Matrix:\\r\\n1. Create a \\\"Use Case vs. Workload\\\" matrix mapping typical scenarios (e.g., Time-Series Analysis, ACID transactions, AI/ML Training) to the recommended Fabric service.\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput Format:\\r\\n• Use standard Markdown.\\r\\n• Include a clear Decision Tree or comparison table for the three workloads.\\r\\n• Provide a \\\"Workload Placement Checklist\\\" for architects at the end.\",\n      \"output\": \"Executing this prompt will generate a strategic decision framework for your Fabric implementation. The AI will produce a workload selection matrix, a workspace separation plan, and clear decision rules that define when to use Spark-based Lakehouses versus SQL-based Warehouses. The final outcome is a structured architectural guide that helps the team avoid \\\"architecture sprawl\\\" by ensuring every dataset is stored and processed in the engine most optimized for its specific use case and user persona.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Architecture\",\n      \"task\": \"Define authentication, authorization, and access control model\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as an expert Security Architect and Identity Access Management (IAM) Specialist. We are building/upgrading our platform and need to define a comprehensive **Authentication, Authorization, and Access Control Model**.\\r\\n\\r\\nHere is the context for our application and security requirements:\\r\\n• **System Overview:** [e.g., A B2B SaaS application / An internal HR reporting dashboard / A customer-facing e-commerce site]\\r\\n• **Identity Provider (IdP) / Tools:** [e.g., Azure AD (Entra ID) / Okta / Auth0 / AWS Cognito]\\r\\n• **Key User Roles/Personas:** [e.g., Super Admin, Tenant Admin, Standard User, Read-Only Guest, System API accounts]\\r\\n• **Authorization Model Preference:** [e.g., Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) / Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC)]\\r\\n• **Data Sensitivity & Compliance:** [e.g., Contains highly sensitive PII, subject to GDPR and SOC2 / Basic public retail data]\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on this context, please draft a comprehensive **Access Control & Identity Model** document.\\r\\n\\r\\nPlease structure the document in Markdown format with the following sections:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Executive Summary:** A high-level overview of the proposed security model and chosen standards (e.g., OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect, SAML).\\r\\n• **Authentication Strategy:** The method for verifying identity, including recommendations for Single Sign-On (SSO), Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA), and token lifecycles.\\r\\n• **Authorization & Access Matrix:** A structured table mapping the provided User Roles to specific system permissions (Create, Read, Update, Delete) across core domains.\\r\\n• **Granular Security Controls:** Recommendations for implementing data-level restrictions, such as Row-Level Security (RLS) or data masking based on the user's role.\\r\\n• **Authentication Flow Diagram (Mermaid.js):** A Mermaid.js sequence diagram visualizing the user login flow, token issuance, and resource validation.\\r\\n\\r\\nEnsure the tone is professional, secure-by-design, and ready for review by our InfoSec team.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will generate a structured security document detailing the authentication protocols (like OAuth2/OIDC) and MFA strategies tailored to your IdP. It will construct a clear RBAC or ABAC permission matrix mapping your specific personas to CRUD actions, provide guidelines for row-level constraints or data masking, and output a Mermaid.js sequence diagram illustrating the secure login and token validation workflow.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Architecture\",\n      \"task\": \"Define how reports, dashboards, and ad‑hoc queries consume data\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a Lead Data Architect and BI Strategist. We are finalizing the presentation layer of our data platform and need to comprehensively **Define Data Consumption Patterns** for reports, dashboards, and ad-hoc queries.\\r\\n\\r\\nHere is the context for our data consumers and platform capabilities:\\r\\n• **Core Data Storage / Warehousing:** [e.g., Snowflake / Google BigQuery / AWS Redshift]\\r\\n• **Primary BI / Reporting Tools:** [e.g., Tableau / Power BI / Looker / Metabase]\\r\\n• **Primary Consumer Personas:** [e.g., C-Suite Execs (mobile dashboards), Financial Analysts (heavy ad-hoc Excel users), Data Scientists (Jupyter notebooks)]\\r\\n• **Performance Requirements:** [e.g., Sub-second response for dashboards, tolerance for minutes on large analytic queries]\\r\\n• **Current Bottlenecks:** [e.g., Too many direct queries to the Data Warehouse slowing it down / Reports timing out / Uncontrolled ad-hoc queries causing cost spikes]\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on this context, please draft a comprehensive **Data Consumption Architecture & Strategy** document.\\r\\n\\r\\nPlease structure the document in Markdown format with the following sections:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Executive Summary:** A high-level overview of the tailored consumption strategy designed to balance performance, cost, and user experience.\\r\\n• **Dashboard & Reporting Patterns:** Define the architectural approach for standard reporting (e.g., using imported/extracted extracts, materializing views, or leveraging BI caching layers).\\r\\n• **Ad-Hoc Querying & Exploration:** Establish the boundaries, tools, and guardrails for power users performing ad-hoc analysis (e.g., DirectQuery/Live Connection usage, dedicated query clusters, or sandbox workspaces).\\r\\n• **Data Export & External Tooling:** Governance around the use of 'Analyze in Excel', API endpoints for custom apps, and data extraction limits.\\r\\n• **Consumption Flow Diagram (Mermaid.js):** A Mermaid.js flowchart visualizing how different user personas route through various BI tools, caching layers, and ultimately hit the underlying database.\\r\\n\\r\\nEnsure the tone is practical, performance-oriented, and governs cost while maximizing self-service capability.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a structured consumption strategy document that maps your specific user personas to their optimal query methods (e.g., Extract vs. Live Connection). It will include clear guidelines on caching, data materialization for fast dashboard loads, and guardrails for ad-hoc queries to control warehouse costs, alongside a Mermaid.js diagram illustrating the end-to-end data consumption flow from the warehouse to the user interface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Architecture\",\n      \"task\": \"Define how security propagates from data → cube → report\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a Senior Data Security Architect specializing in the Microsoft Data Stack (Azure, Fabric, Power BI). Your task is to design an End-to-End Security Propagation Architecture that ensures a \\\"Single Source of Truth\\\" for security as data moves from the storage layer through the semantic model to the end-user report.\\r\\n\\r\\nContext to Provide:\\r\\n• Data Storage Layer: {e.g., ADLS Gen2, Fabric Lakehouse, Synapse SQL}\\r\\n• Semantic Layer: {e.g., Power BI Semantic Model (Import/DirectLake), Analysis Services}\\r\\n• Authentication Mechanism: {e.g., Service Principal, Managed Identity, User Delegation/SSO}\\r\\n• Compliance Needs: {e.g., GDPR, HIPAA, Internal PII masking}\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements:\\r\\nPlease provide a detailed security propagation blueprint including:\\r\\n\\r\\n• Storage Layer Security (Data):\\r\\n1. Define the implementation of Row-Level Security (RLS) and Object-Level Security (OLS) at the SQL or Delta Lake level.\\r\\n2. Explain the use of Microsoft Entra ID (Azure AD) passthrough to ensure the identity of the person viewing the report is the same identity hitting the database.\\r\\n• Semantic Layer Security (Cube):\\r\\n1. Define how DAX-based RLS roles in Power BI map to the underlying security filters in the data source.\\r\\n2. Explain the strategy for External Contributor access—how to restrict specific tables or measures within the \\\"cube\\\" for different business units.\\r\\n• Presentation Layer Security (Report):\\r\\n1. Outline the Workspace-level permissions (Viewer, Contributor, Member) and how they interact with underlying data permissions.\\r\\n2. Describe the propagation of Sensitivity Labels (Microsoft Purview) from the database to the Power BI dashboard to prevent unauthorized exports.\\r\\n• Security Propagation Matrix:\\r\\n1. Create a table showing the flow of a single user request: User Identity → Power BI App → Semantic Model → Data Engine → Storage, detailing where the \\\"Gatekeeper\\\" sits at each hop.\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput Format:\\r\\n• Use standard Markdown.\\r\\n• Include a clear Propagation Flow Diagram (text-based or Mermaid style).\\r\\n• Provide an \\\"E2E Security Audit Checklist\\\" at the end.\",\n      \"output\": \"Prompt will generate a comprehensive Security Traceability Matrix and architectural guide. The AI will produce a step-by-step breakdown of how Identity Delegation works across your stack, ensuring that a user’s permissions are \\\"inherited\\\" correctly from the raw data all the way to the visual report. The final outcome is a foolproof security blueprint that prevents \\\"leaky\\\" reports where users might inadvertently see data they aren't authorized to access, ensuring compliance and data privacy at every hop.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Architecture\",\n      \"task\": \"Define where business logic resides across ETL, curated layers, and cubes\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a Principal Data Architect and ETL Framework Designer. Your task is to design a Transformation and Business Logic Layering Architecture that clearly separates technical processing from business-driven calculations across the Medallion (Bronze/Silver/Gold) architecture.\\r\\n\\r\\nContext to Provide:\\r\\n• Primary Tooling: {e.g., Databricks (Spark), Fabric (SQL/Notebooks), Synapse}\\r\\n• Data Architecture: {e.g., Medallion (Bronze/Silver/Gold) / Star Schema}\\r\\n• Downstream Consumer: {e.g., Power BI Semantic Models / Data Science Notebooks}\\r\\n• Logic Complexity: {e.g., Simple Aggregations / Complex Financial Reconciliations / Regulatory Reporting}\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements:\\r\\nPlease provide a comprehensive logic distribution guide including:\\r\\n\\r\\n• Layered Logic Definition:\\r\\n1. Define the role of the Bronze/Raw Layer (Schema enforcement and technical metadata only).\\r\\n2. Define the Silver/Curated Layer logic: Standardizing formats, deduplication, and cross-reference joining.\\r\\n3. Define the Gold/Business Layer logic: Where complex business KPIs, period-over-period calculations, and domain-specific filters reside.\\r\\n• Compute Engine Selection:\\r\\n1. Recommend when to use Spark (Python/Scala) for heavy-lift technical transformations vs. SQL for business-friendly logic in the Gold layer.\\r\\n2. Explain the role of the Semantic Layer (Power BI/Cubes) in handling \\\"Last-Mile\\\" logic (e.g., dynamic currency conversion or measures).\\r\\n• Governance & Reusability:\\r\\n1. Propose a strategy for a Centralized Logic Repository (e.g., dbt models or Spark functions) to prevent \\\"Dark Logic\\\" (calculations hidden in individual reports).\\r\\n2. Explain how to handle Reference Data (Master Data) so it is injected consistently across all layers.\\r\\n• Logic Traceability Matrix:\\r\\n1. Create a table mapping common tasks (e.g., Data Type Casting, Null Handling, Revenue Calculation, User Personalization) to the specific architectural layer where they should be executed.\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput Format:\\r\\n• Use standard Markdown.\\r\\n• Include a structured Logic Distribution Table.\\r\\n• Provide a \\\"Logic Layering Audit Checklist\\\" for code reviewers at the end.\",\n      \"output\": \"Executing this prompt will generate a Business Logic Blueprint that eliminates architectural ambiguity. The AI will produce a Layered Transformation Matrix, clear rules for SQL vs. Spark usage, and a governance plan for Semantic Layer measures. The final outcome is a structured guide that ensures your transformations are modular, scalable, and \\\"DRY\\\" (Don't Repeat Yourself), preventing the common pitfall of having inconsistent business logic scattered across multiple ETL pipelines and reports.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"Design\": [\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Document authentication mechanisms (OAuth, Service Principal, Keys, MI)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Task:\\r\\nCreate a “Document Authentication & Access Control Requirements” section based on an existing authentication analysis.\\r\\n\\r\\nUser must provide:\\r\\n- System/Application Name\\r\\n- Deployment Environment(s) (Dev/Test/Prod)\\r\\n- Supported Authentication Mechanisms (OAuth 2.0, Service Principal, API Keys, Managed Identity)\\r\\n- User Types (end users, internal services, external integrations)\\r\\n- Access Model (RBAC roles, permissions, least-privilege expectations)\\r\\n- Compliance or Security Standards (if any)\\r\\n\\r\\nInstructions:\\r\\nUse the provided inputs and the completed authentication analysis to define authentication requirements, access control rules, and security controls (token lifecycle, secret management, auditing). Keep the content generic, concise, and implementation-ready for enterprise documentation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Document standardization rules (date format, currency normalization, etc.)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a data governance and document standardization expert.\\r\\nConnect to Power BI Desktop with name **Provide name here**.\\r\\n\\r\\nCreate a comprehensive \\\"Document Standardization Rules\\\" specification. Consider the connected report for reference\\r\\nThe document should define clear, enforceable formatting and normalization rules for:\\r\\nDate and time formats\\r\\nCurrency formatting and normalization\\r\\nNumber formatting (decimals, thousands separators)\\r\\nTime zones\\r\\nText casing standards\\r\\nSpecial characters and encoding\\r\\nCountry and region codes\\r\\nUnits of measurement\\r\\nBoolean values\\r\\nNull / missing values handling\\r\\nFile naming conventions\\r\\nVersioning format\\r\\n\\r\\nFor each rule, provide:\\r\\nStandard format\\r\\nExamples of valid values\\r\\nExamples of invalid values\\r\\nNormalization logic (how non-standard values should be converted)\\r\\nRationale for the rule\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput format:\\r\\nUse clear section headings\\r\\nUse tables where helpful\\r\\nKeep language precise and implementation-ready\\r\\nAvoid vague recommendations\",\n      \"output\": \"It will generate a detailed standardization document based on the report provided.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Create conceptual data model (entities & relationships)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as an expert Data Modeler and Enterprise Architect. I am in the data discovery and design phase and need to create a Conceptual Data Model to understand the core business entities and their relationships.\\r\\n\\r\\nHere is the context for our project:\\r\\n**Business Domain / Use Case:** {Insert Domain, e.g., E-commerce Sales / Healthcare Patient Records / SaaS Billing}\\r\\n**Key Entities or Raw Tables identified so far:** {Insert 3-7 main entities, e.g., Customers, Orders, Products, Invoices, Payments}\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on this context, please generate the initial Entity-Relationship (ER) structure:\\r\\n• **Core Entities:** Provide a list of the main business entities with a brief, 1-sentence business definition for each. Include 2-3 key attributes (like primary keys or major descriptive fields) just for context.\\r\\n• **Relationships & Cardinality:** Define how these entities relate to each other. Specify the cardinality (1:1, 1:N, M:N) and state the business rule clearly (e.g., \\\"1-to-Many: A Customer can place zero or many Orders, but an Order belongs to exactly one Customer\\\").\\r\\n• **Visual Diagram (Mermaid.js):** Generate a Mermaid.js ER diagram code block representing this conceptual model so I can copy/paste it into a Markdown viewer or Confluence to visualize the architecture.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a conceptual data model breakdown featuring core business entities with concise definitions and key attributes, explicit relationships with cardinality and business rules, and a Mermaid.js ER diagram code block ready to be pasted into a Markdown viewer or Confluence for visualization.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Capture scalability, partitioning & data growth expectations\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a Senior Data Architect responsible for designing high-performance, scalable, and future-proof data solutions.\\r\\n\\r\\nContext\\r\\nThe goal is to document performance, scalability, and data growth expectations for our data platform using [INSERT YOUR TECHNOLOGIES, e.g., Azure Databricks, Microsoft Fabric, Snowflake, etc.]. We need to define clear strategies for data partitioning, distribution, and storage scaling to ensure our architecture remains robust as our data volume, velocity, and user concurrency increase.\\r\\n\\r\\nProject Configuration\\r\\nTechnologies: [List your stack, e.g., Databricks, Synapse, Fabric]\\r\\nKey Workloads: [e.g., Real-time streaming, Batch ETL, BI Reporting]\\r\\nGrowth Horizon: [e.g., 1-year, 3-year projections]\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements & Constraints\\r\\nPillar 1: Performance & Scalability\\r\\nDefine strategies for horizontal and vertical scaling for the compute resources identified in my technology stack.\\r\\nDocument concurrency handling strategies for end-users and reporting tools.\\r\\nPillar 2: Data Partitioning & Storage\\r\\nDefine best practices for data partitioning (e.g., by time, geography) to optimize query pruning.\\r\\nAddress storage growth expectations and cold/hot storage tiering for my chosen technologies.\\r\\nPillar 3: Growth & Maintenance\\r\\nInclude strategies for handling data drift and schema evolution.\\r\\nOutline specific performance monitoring metrics relevant to my stack (e.g., partition skew, spill to disk, query latency).\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput Format \\r\\nPlease provide the response in Markdown including:\\r\\nScalability Matrix: A comparison table for scaling approaches across the technologies I listed.\\r\\nPartitioning Strategy Guide: Best practices for data partitioning and file sizing to avoid performance bottlenecks.\\r\\nPerformance Monitoring Framework: Key performance indicators (KPIs) to track growth and system health.\\r\\nGrowth Projection Worksheet: A template for documenting expected data volume, row counts, and storage costs.\\r\\nTechnical Best Practices Checklist: Guidelines for optimizing performance under high concurrency.\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent generates a Markdown-based architecture guide for planning performance, scalability, and data growth for the specified data platform (e.g., Databricks, Synapse, Fabric, Snowflake).\\r\\n\\r\\nThe output includes a Scalability Matrix for compute scaling strategies, a Partitioning Strategy Guide for optimal data partitioning and file sizing, and a Performance Monitoring Framework with key KPIs such as query latency, partition skew, and storage growth.\\r\\n\\r\\nIt also provides a Growth Projection Worksheet for estimating future data volumes and costs, along with a Technical Best Practices Checklist to optimize performance, handle schema evolution, and support high concurrency as the platform scales.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Create an approach document for ingestion type (Full, Incremental, CDC) including watermark / delta logic\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a senior data platform architect.\\r\\n\\r\\nCreate a comprehensive approach document for designing data ingestion strategies supporting:\\r\\nFull Load\\r\\nIncremental Load\\r\\nChange Data Capture (CDC)\\r\\nYou can give general idea on when to use which type of load pattern\\r\\n\\r\\nAt the end provide a summary table for what should be the load type for the below systems.\\r\\nSystem Context:\\r\\n**Describe source systems, data volume, latency expectations, Transformation platform, target platform, batch/streaming, etc.**\\r\\n**Insert your context here **\\r\\n\\r\\nThe document must be implementation-focused\\r\\n\\r\\nConstraints:\\r\\nAssume distributed system\\r\\nAssume at-least-once execution\\r\\nAssume possibility of pipeline restarts\\r\\nAvoid vague guidance\\r\\nProvide concrete implementation patterns\",\n      \"output\": \"[Describe source systems, data volume, latency expectations, Transformation platform, target platform, batch/streaming, etc.]\\r\\n1. Cosmos DB, 10 years of data in date format, every day data is published in source for previous day so we need that latest data is published daily in the source for the previous day, so we need to ingest the latest data, Databricks, ADLS, batch.\\r\\n2. SQL , Hundreds of rows , Updates daily, ADF, ADLS, Batch\\r\\n\\r\\nIt will generate detailed documentation on the load types and suggest an incremental load for Cosmos and a full load for SQL based on the provided system context above.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Create an approach document for landing zones (ADLS / OneLake), file formats (Parquet, Delta, CSV, JSON) and batch vs near-real-time ingestion\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a senior data platform architect.\\r\\n\\r\\nCreate a comprehensive approach document covering:\\r\\n• Landing Zone Architecture (ADLS / OneLake)\\r\\n• File Format Strategy (Parquet, Delta, CSV, JSON)\\r\\n• Batch vs Near-Real-Time Ingestion Design\\r\\n• Provide very concise Comparison between different available options.\\r\\n• Avoid providing code snippets\",\n      \"output\": \"A detailed document consisting of pros, cons, use cases, etc. for different formats and designs will be generated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Create an approach document for partitioning strategy, dependency & trigger strategy, retry & timeout policies\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a senior data engineer specialized in optimizations / orchestration / reliability\\r\\nCreate a technical approach document that outlines best strategies for the below\\r\\nThe document should include the following sections:\\r\\n**Partitioning Strategy**\\r\\n• Explain the purpose of partitioning\\r\\n• Describe common partitioning techniques (e.g., by time, by key, by hash).\\r\\n• Include considerations for scalability, performance, and data skew.\\r\\n• Provide examples of when to use each partitioning approach.\\r\\n• How to decide the partitioning column\\r\\n\\r\\n**Dependency & Trigger Strategy**\\r\\n• Define dependency management between tasks or services.\\r\\n• Describe types of triggers (e.g., time-based, event-based, completion-based).\\r\\n• Explain strategies for handling complex dependency graphs.\\r\\n• Include examples of how triggers interact with dependencies to start tasks efficiently.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Retry & Timeout Policies**\\r\\n• Describe common retry strategies (e.g., fixed interval, exponential backoff, max attempts).\\r\\n• Provide guidelines for setting timeout values per task type.\\r\\n• Include examples of policies for handling failures and preventing cascading errors.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Technologies: Provide context here**\",\n      \"output\": \"Technologies: ADB and ADF for batch processing\\r\\n\\r\\nThe prompt will deliver a practical, implementation-focused guide for designing distributed data pipelines, covering partitioning, dependency management, and retry/timeout strategies. It explains how to choose partitioning columns and techniques for scalability and query performance, details trigger types and provides retry and timeout policies to ensure reliability and prevent cascading failures. The document includes examples and best practices for each strategy and concludes with a concise summary table of recommendations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Create an approach document for parallelism & throttling strategy, error handling & failure paths\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Principal Azure Data Architect responsible for designing scalable, resilient, and enterprise-grade data platform architectures across Azure Data Factory (ADF), Microsoft Fabric, Azure Synapse Analytics, and Azure Databricks.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nGenerate a comprehensive \\\"Approach Document for Parallelism, Throttling, and Error Handling\\\" tailored specifically to the provided data assets and source systems.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need to establish a strict engineering framework to ensure high-volume data ingestion scales horizontally without overwhelming source systems or breaching platform limits, while maintaining robust error handling, dead-lettering, and alerting.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Target Source Systems:** {Add Target Source Systems, e.g., On-Prem SQL Server, Dynamics 365 API}\\r\\n**High-Volume / Critical Tables:** {Add High-Volume Tables, e.g., Fact_Sales, GL_Entries}\\r\\n**Standard / Low-Volume Tables:** {Add Low-Volume Tables, e.g., Dim_Customer, Dim_Location}\\r\\n**Expected Frequency & Volume:** {Add Expected Frequency and Volume, e.g., Daily loads, ~50GB per day total}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nCreate a highly structured, professional proposal document ready for Confluence or Azure DevOps Wiki. Do not include conversational filler, introductory fluff, or generic high-level statements. You MUST explicitly cover implementation details across ADF, Fabric, Synapse, and Databricks. You MUST reference the specific tables, sources, and volumes provided in the context to define exact configurations (e.g., if a table is huge, define its specific partitioning strategy).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n1. Tailored Parallelism and Concurrency Strategy:\\r\\n• Define how the ingestion framework will scale horizontally for the specific tables mentioned.\\r\\n• Define the exact strategies for the Pipeline Orchestration ForEach activity (Sequential vs. Parallel, max batch counts) based on the table volumes provided.\\r\\n• Define compute parallelism at the Compute Level (Databricks/Fabric Spark). Explicitly state the Auto-scaling configurations, Spark partition tuning (spark.sql.shuffle.partitions), and dynamic allocation required for High-Volume Tables versus Standard Tables.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Throttling and Resource Protection Strategy:\\r\\n• Define how the platform will prevent overwhelming the specific Target Source Systems mentioned.\\r\\n• Define Source Protection rules, including concurrent connection limits, API rate limiting strategies (HTTP 429 backoffs), or read-replica utilization specific to the sources provided.\\r\\n• Define Platform Limits management, including handling workspace concurrency limits across Databricks (Job concurrency) and Fabric (Capacity Unit / CU throttling) to ensure daily loads complete within SLAs.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Error Handling and Failure Paths:\\r\\n• Define the standardized failure lifecycle for this framework.\\r\\n• Define the exact On-Failure execution paths at the Pipeline Level (ADF/Fabric/Synapse). Explain pipeline retries (Retry count, interval) versus activity-level retries.\\r\\n• Define strategies at the Compute Level (Databricks/Spark) for PySpark try/except blocks and explicit exception raising to fail the orchestrator.\\r\\n• Define the strategy at the Data Level (Dead-Lettering) for handling bad records specifically for critical tables, utilizing native features like Databricks badRecordsPath or quarantine zones without failing the entire batch.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Alerting and Actionable Monitoring:\\r\\n• Define the integration with Azure Monitor and Log Analytics.\\r\\n• Map out the matrix of who gets alerted and when (e.g., Sev 1 for framework failures vs. Sev 3 for single-table data quality drops).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**: \\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown\\r\\n\\r\\nTailored Parallelism and Concurrency Strategy details and code blocks\\r\\nThrottling and Resource Protection Strategy details\\r\\nError Handling and Failure Paths code and architecture blocks\\r\\nAlerting and Actionable Monitoring matrix\",\n      \"output\": \"The agent provides a highly structured, production-ready engineering framework designed to scale high-volume data ingestion across Azure Data Factory, Synapse, Fabric, and Databricks. It delivers a tiered parallelism strategy tailored to your specific high-volume assets like Fact_Sales and GL_Entries, ensuring that throughput is maximized while strictly protecting source systems such as On-Prem SQL Server and Dynamics 365 through advanced throttling and backoff logic. It incorporates automated \\\"Dead-Letter\\\" patterns and a multi-level alerting matrix.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Create an approach document for managed identity usage, Key Vault integration, credential storage and RBAC for storage & pipelines\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Role:\\r\\nYou are a Senior Azure Data Engineer responsible for designing secure, enterprise-grade data access architecture.\\r\\n\\r\\nContext:\\r\\nWe need to implement secure, passwordless access from Azure services to data sources using Managed Identity, Azure Key Vault, RBAC, and secure networking. The goal is to eliminate hardcoded credentials, enforce least-privilege access, and follow Azure security best practices.\\r\\n\\r\\nEnvironment:\\r\\n\\r\\nData Sources:\\r\\n- <Add Data Source 1> (e.g., ADLS Gen2)\\r\\n- <Add Data Source 2> (e.g., Azure SQL Database)\\r\\n- <Add Data Source 3> (e.g., Cosmos DB, Blob Storage, REST API, etc.)\\r\\n\\r\\nServices Accessing Data:\\r\\n- <Add Service 1> (e.g., Fabric, Synapse, Databricks, Data Factory, Function App, etc.)\\r\\n- <Add Service 2>\\r\\n\\r\\nTask:\\r\\nCreate a practical Secure Data Access approach guide explaining how to securely authenticate and access the data sources using Managed Identity, Key Vault, and RBAC.\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Explain authentication using Azure AD and Managed Identity (System vs User Assigned)\\r\\n- Define RBAC roles using least privilege (include access matrix table)\\r\\n- Explain when and how to use Azure Key Vault for secrets\\r\\n- Describe credential management strategy (prioritize passwordless access)\\r\\n- Show secure access flow including authentication and authorization\\r\\n- Define network security setup (Private Endpoints, firewall, disable public access)\\r\\n- Provide step-by-step implementation using Azure CLI or PowerShell\\r\\n- Include working code examples for connecting using Managed Identity\\r\\n- Include troubleshooting guidance for common access issues\\r\\n- Provide risks and mitigation table\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput Format:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Markdown with clear headings and structured sections\\r\\n- Include RBAC tables and Mermaid flow diagrams\\r\\n- Include Azure CLI commands and code examples\\r\\n- Keep content practical, developer-focused, and security-review ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Document ingestion runbook (how-to-run, restart logic, troubleshooting steps)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Senior Site Reliability Engineer (SRE) / Technical Writer** tasked with creating a detailed **Document Ingestion Runbook** for our data pipeline.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Ingestion Tool/Language**: {e.g., Python Script / Azure Data Factory / Java application / Shell Script}\\r\\n• **Source System**: {e.g., SFTP Folder / REST API / SharePoint / Kafka Trend}\\r\\n• **Target/Destination**: {e.g., Azure Blob Storage / AWS S3 / SQL Database}\\r\\n• **Trigger Mechanism**: {e.g., Cron Job / Event Grid Trigger / Manual execution via CLI}\\r\\n• **Logging Location**: {e.g., Azure Monitor / Local .log files / Splunk}\\r\\n• **Restart Strategy**: {e.g., Delete partial files and re-run / Resume from last Checkpoint ID}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease create a runbook that serves as a \\\"First Responder\\\" guide for the document ingestion process. The document must be structured as follows:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **System Overview**:\\r\\n  1. Briefly explain the architecture (Source -> Processing -> Target).\\r\\n  2. List critical prerequisites (e.g., VPN connection, specific User Identity).\\r\\n• **Standard Operating Procedures (How-to-Run)**:\\r\\n  1. Provide the exact step-by-step commands to start the ingestion service locally and in production.\\r\\n  2. Include the required environment variables or configuration flags.\\r\\n  3. Structure: Use separate code blocks for different environments (e.g., DEV vs PROD).\\r\\n• **Restart & Recovery Logic**:\\r\\n  1. **Graceful Shutdown**: How to stop the process without corrupting data.\\r\\n  2. **Recovery**: If the job fails halfway, do we need to clean up partial files? Or does the system automatically handle duplicates?\\r\\n  3. **Force Restart**: How to clear the \\\"watermark\\\" or \\\"offset\\\" to re-process old documents if needed.\\r\\n• **Troubleshooting Guide**:\\r\\n  1. Create a \\\"Symptom -> Potential Cause -> Fix\\\" table.\\r\\n  2. Include common errors specific to the tools listed above (e.g., API Rate Limits, File Lock issues, Auth Token expiration).\\r\\n  3. Provide 2-3 specific log search queries or grep commands to find errors quickly.\\r\\n• **Validation**:\\r\\n  1. How does the engineer know the system is healthy after a restart? (e.g., Check row counts, verify file timestamps).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**:\\r\\n• Use standard Markdown.\\r\\n• Use bold warnings for destructive actions (like deleting data).\\r\\n• Use code blocks for all terminal commands.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a comprehensive Markdown runbook outlining the system architecture and prerequisites, step-by-step execution commands using code blocks for both local and production environments, and targeted restart and recovery procedures featuring bold warnings for distinct destructive actions. Additionally, it will include a troubleshooting table mapping common symptoms to fixes with specific log search queries, and validation steps to verify system recovery and health.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Define the relationship model (Star/Snowflake)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Semantic Model Architect** tasked with designing a **Star Schema** for Power BI / Microsoft Fabric using the fact and dimension tables in the attached Excel file.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please attach your Excel file and fill in these details if applicable):\\r\\n• **Business Domain**: {e.g., Retail Sales, Financial Reporting, HR Analytics}\\r\\n• **Primary BI Tool**: {e.g., Power BI Premium, Microsoft Fabric}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease analyze the attached data and extract the specific design components for the Semantic Model:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Data Modeling Core**:\\r\\n  1. **Fact Tables**: Identify fact table(s), their grain, foreign keys, and measures.\\r\\n  2. **Dimension Tables**: Identify dimension tables with primary keys and attributes.\\r\\n• **Relationships Definition**:\\r\\n  1. Define From Table → To Table (Join Columns).\\r\\n  2. Define Cardinality (1:M / M:1 / M:M).\\r\\n  3. Define Cross-filter Direction (Single / Both).\\r\\n  4. Specify Active vs Inactive (especially for role-playing dimensions).\\r\\n• **Advanced Modeling**:\\r\\n  1. Flag role-playing dimensions and suggest `USERELATIONSHIP()` patterns.\\r\\n  2. Flag bridge tables and outline many-to-many handling.\\r\\n• **Optimization & Storage**:\\r\\n  1. Recommend the optimal storage mode per table (Import / DirectQuery / Dual).\\r\\n  2. Identify high-cardinality columns that should be excluded from the model.\\r\\n• **Calculations & Security**:\\r\\n  1. Suggest 5-10 base DAX measures derived from the metric columns.\\r\\n  2. Identify sensitive/PII columns that require RLS (Row-Level Security) or CLS (Column-Level Security).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Provide Markdown tables detailing all table relationships.\\r\\n• Include a text-based star schema diagram.\\r\\n• Output the DAX measures, optimization, and security recommendations in well-structured format.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide fact/dimension table identification, relationship mapping tables (cardinality, cross-filter, active/inactive), role-playing dimension and bridge table flags, storage mode recommendations, high-cardinality exclusions, base DAX measures, PII/security flags, and a text-based star schema diagram.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Ensure naming conventions and standard formatting\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Principal Power BI & Fabric Semantic Model Architect responsible for maintaining enterprise-grade, highly governed, and perfectly formatted semantic models.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nGenerate automated Tabular Editor C# scripts and Best Practice Analyzer (BPA) rules to strictly enforce enterprise naming conventions, DAX formatting, and display folder organization across a Semantic Model.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need to establish and automate strict enterprise naming conventions, display folders, and DAX formatting rules across a semantic model using Tabular Editor to ensure consistency and improve the developer/consumer experience.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Table Naming Rules**: {Remove prefixes 'Dim_', 'Fact_', 'vw_'. Replace underscores with spaces. Title Case.}  \\r\\n**Column Naming Rules**: {Replace underscores with spaces. Split PascalCase into words.}  \\r\\n**Hidden Columns Policy**: {Hide all columns ending in 'Key', 'ID', or 'SK'. Move them to a Display Folder named \\\"System Data\\\".}  \\r\\n**Measure Folder Strategy**: {Move all measures into a Display Folder called \\\"_Measures\\\" at the top of their respective tables.}  \\r\\n**DAX Formatting Required?**: {YES - execute Tabular Editor native FormatDax method on all measures}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nProvide the COMPLETE, copy-pasteable Tabular Editor C# Advanced Scripts and JSON. Do not use conversational filler. Output code in structured markdown blocks. The scripts MUST utilize System.Text.RegularExpressions where necessary for advanced string manipulation (like splitting PascalCase). The scripts must loop through all Tables, Columns, and Measures dynamically.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Tabular Editor C# Script: Master Naming and Formatting Engine**:  \\r\\n1. Generate a single, comprehensive C# script to execute in Tabular Editor that applies the rules defined in the User Context.  \\r\\n2. Table Renaming: Loop through Model.Tables. Apply Regex or String.Replace to enforce the Table Naming Rules.  \\r\\n3. Column Renaming and Hiding: Loop through Model.AllColumns. Apply the Column Naming Rules. If the column matches the Hidden Columns Policy, set .IsHidden = true and set .DisplayFolder.  \\r\\n4. Measure Organization: Loop through Model.AllMeasures. Assign their .DisplayFolder based on the Measure Folder Strategy.  \\r\\n5. DAX Formatting: If DAX Formatting Required is YES, loop through all measures and execute .FormatDax() to standardize the code indentation.\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Best Practice Analyzer (BPA) JSON Rules (Ongoing Enforcement)**:  \\r\\n1. To prevent future developers from breaking these naming conventions, generate the raw JSON for Tabular Editor BPA rules.  \\r\\n2. Rule 1: Flag any Table name that starts with Dim_, Fact_, or vw_.  \\r\\n3. Rule 2: Flag any Column name that contains an underscore.  \\r\\n4. Rule 3: Flag any Column name ending in Key or ID that is NOT hidden (IsHidden = false).\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Operational Runbook**:  \\r\\n1. Provide a brief 3-step Markdown runbook.  \\r\\n2. Step 1: Back up the .pbip or Model.bim file before running the script.  \\r\\n3. Step 2: Execute the C# Master Script in Tabular Editor.  \\r\\n4. Step 3: Import the BPA JSON rules to maintain ongoing governance.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**:  \\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Complete Tabular Editor C# Master Script**  \\r\\n2. **Tabular Editor BPA Rules JSON payload code block**  \\r\\n3. **Markdown Operational Runbook code block**\",\n      \"output\": \"Enterprise Semantic Model Governance Suite\\r\\nThis agent provides a production-ready automation suite tailored to strictly enforce your specific naming and organizational policies within the Tabular Editor environment. It delivers a comprehensive C# \\\"Master Engine\\\" that programmatically cleans prefixes like 'Dim_' and 'Fact_', standardizes PascalCase into readable words, and enforces your \\\"System Data\\\" and \\\"_Measures\\\" folder strategies. By integrating a persistent Best Practice Analyzer (BPA) framework, this agent ensures that your model remains clean, governed, and perfectly formatted for an optimal Power BI consumer experience.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Ensure measure folder organization (Display Folders)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Principal Power BI & Fabric Semantic Model Architect responsible for designing, automating, and governing enterprise-grade semantic models with a focus on consumer usability and metadata organization.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nGenerate a comprehensive Tabular Editor C# Advanced Script and Best Practice Analyzer (BPA) rules to strictly enforce enterprise measure organization using nested Display Folders across a Semantic Model.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need to automate the organization of measures into a logical, hierarchical folder structure based on naming conventions and metadata properties. This ensures that as the model scales, the field pane remains intuitive for business users.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Target Model**: {Add Model Name, e.g., Enterprise_Sales_Dataset}  \\r\\n**Root Folder Strategy**: {Place all measures in a root folder named \\\"_Measures\\\" at the top of their respective home tables.}  \\r\\n**Sub-Folder Rules (Time Intelligence)**: {If name ends with 'YTD', 'MTD', or 'QTD', move to \\\"_Measures\\\\Time Intelligence\\\". If contains 'YoY' or 'MoM', move to \\\"_Measures\\\\Variances\\\".}  \\r\\n**Sub-Folder Rules (Formatting/Types)**: {If contains '%', 'Margin', or 'Rate', move to \\\"_Measures\\\\Ratios\\\".}  \\r\\n**Hidden / Base Measures Rule**: {If IsHidden = true or starts with '_', move to \\\"_System\\\\_Hidden_Base_Measures\\\".}  \\r\\n**Default Catch-All Folder**: {If no match, move to \\\"_Measures\\\\Core Metrics\\\".}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nProvide the COMPLETE, copy-pasteable Tabular Editor C# Advanced Script and JSON. Do not use conversational filler. Output code in structured markdown blocks. The script MUST handle nested display folders (using the `\\\\` delimiter) and utilize string matching or Regex to route measures dynamically.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Tabular Editor C# Script: The Display Folder Routing Engine**  \\r\\n1. The script MUST loop through Model.AllMeasures.  \\r\\n2. Implement evaluation logic to check measure names and the IsHidden property against the specified rules.  \\r\\n3. Dynamically assign the .DisplayFolder property using the backslash (\\\\) delimiter for nesting.  \\r\\n4. Ensure idempotent execution (can be run multiple times without path duplication).\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Best Practice Analyzer (BPA) JSON Rules (Ongoing Enforcement)**  \\r\\n1. Rule 1: Flag any Measure where the DisplayFolder property is blank or empty.  \\r\\n2. Rule 2: Flag any Measure that is NOT located inside the designated Root Folder Strategy path (e.g., does not start with \\\"_Measures\\\" or \\\"_System\\\").\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Operational Runbook**  \\r\\n1. Provide a brief 3-step Markdown runbook for execution and deployment.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**:  \\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown\\r\\n\\r\\n### 1. Tabular Editor C# Script: The Display Folder Routing Engine\\r\\n\\r\\n```csharp\\r\\n/* Display Folder Routing Engine\\r\\n   Principal Power BI & Fabric Semantic Model Architect\\r\\n*/\\r\\n\\r\\nusing System.Text.RegularExpressions;\\r\\n\\r\\nforeach (var m in Model.AllMeasures)\\r\\n{\\r\\n    string targetFolder = \\\"\\\";\\r\\n\\r\\n    // Rule 1: Hidden / Base Measures\\r\\n    if (m.IsHidden || m.Name.StartsWith(\\\"_\\\"))\\r\\n    {\\r\\n        targetFolder = @\\\"_System\\\\_Hidden_Base_Measures\\\";\\r\\n    }\\r\\n    // Rule 2: Time Intelligence (YTD, MTD, QTD)\\r\\n    else if (Regex.IsMatch(m.Name, @\\\"(YTD|MTD|QTD)$\\\", RegexOptions.IgnoreCase))\\r\\n    {\\r\\n        targetFolder = @\\\"_Measures\\\\Time Intelligence\\\";\\r\\n    }\\r\\n    // Rule 3: Variances (YoY, MoM)\\r\\n    else if (m.Name.Contains(\\\"YoY\\\") || m.Name.Contains(\\\"MoM\\\"))\\r\\n    {\\r\\n        targetFolder = @\\\"_Measures\\\\Variances\\\";\\r\\n    }\\r\\n    // Rule 4: Ratios and Formatting\\r\\n    else if (m.Name.Contains(\\\"%\\\") || m.Name.Contains(\\\"Margin\\\") || m.Name.Contains(\\\"Rate\\\"))\\r\\n    {\\r\\n        targetFolder = @\\\"_Measures\\\\Ratios\\\";\\r\\n    }\\r\\n    // Rule 5: Default Catch-All\\r\\n    else\\r\\n    {\\r\\n        targetFolder = @\\\"_Measures\\\\Core Metrics\\\";\\r\\n    }\\r\\n\\r\\n    // Apply the folder assignment\\r\\n    m.DisplayFolder = targetFolder;\\r\\n}\\r\\n\\r\\nInfo(\\\"Measure Folder Routing Complete. Hierarchical organization has been applied to all measures.\\\");\",\n      \"output\": \"This agent provides a production-ready automation framework designed to programmatically organize your semantic model's measure library into a clean, hierarchical structure. It delivers a specialized C# \\\"Routing Engine\\\" that evaluates measure metadata and naming patterns to dynamically assign nested Display Folders, such as \\\"_Measures\\\\Time Intelligence\\\" or \\\"_System_Hidden_Base_Measures\\\". By coupling this with custom Best Practice Analyzer (BPA) rules, the agent establishes a persistent governance layer that prevents \\\"metadata drift\\\" and ensures your field pane remains intuitive for business users as the model scales.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Create model documentation (tables, relationships, measures, KPIs, RLS)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Generate detailed documentation for the data model including:\\r\\nTables, their relationships, measures, KPIs, and Row-Level Security (RLS) settings\\r\\nFormat documentation as markdown or HTML for easy consumption.\",\n      \"output\": \"Based on the model provided, it will document all the metadata by section.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Define report layout, visual design, and UX strategy\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as an **Expert Power BI Developer and UI/UX Designer** tasked with designing a Power BI report and suggesting a comprehensive **layout and visual strategy**.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Business Goal**: {e.g., Track monthly sales performance, monitor supply chain logistics, analyze customer churn}\\r\\n• **Target Audience**: {e.g., C-level executives, regional sales managers, daily operations team}\\r\\n• **Key KPIs/Metrics**: {e.g., Total Revenue, Profit Margin, YoY Growth, Number of Active Customers}\\r\\n• **Key Dimensions/Slicers**: {e.g., Date/Month, Region, Product Category, Salesperson}\\r\\n• **Report Constraints**: {e.g., Needs to fit on a single page, needs a dark theme, must be mobile-friendly, max 5 visuals per page}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease provide a detailed report design plan based on the provided context, including:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Report Architecture**:\\r\\n  1. **Page Structure**: How many pages should the report have and what is the purpose of each?\\r\\n  2. **Layout Grid**: For each page, suggest a layout (e.g., Top banner for KPIs, left pane for slicers, main body for charts).\\r\\n• **Visuals & Interactivity**:\\r\\n  1. **Visual Selection**: Recommend the best Power BI visual types for each metric/dimension and explain why.\\r\\n  2. **UX/UI Best Practices**: Provide tips on colors, navigation, or interactions (drill-throughs, tooltips, bookmarks) to make the report more intuitive.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Provide a clear, structured Markdown response outlining the page layouts and visual recommendations.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a page structure blueprint, layout grid with visual placement zones, visual type recommendations per KPI/dimension, and UX/UI best practices for colors, slicers, navigation, and interactivity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Design\",\n      \"task\": \"Create documentation (pages, visuals, KPIs, RLS, data sources)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Senior Power BI Solution Architect / Documentation Specialist** tasked with producing audit-ready technical documentation for the currently connected Power BI Desktop model.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease generate documentation using ONLY the extracted metadata from your available Power BI MCP tools. Make no assumptions or inferences. If data is missing or inaccessible, mark it as \\\"Not Available\\\". Since the MCP server cannot read report-level UI elements (Pages, Visuals, Workspaces), focus strictly on the Semantic Model architecture.\\r\\n\\r\\nInclude the following sections:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Semantic Model Overview**:\\r\\n  1. **Model Name**: (from connection).\\r\\n  2. **Total Statistics**: Total number of Tables and Measures.\\r\\n  3. **Business Subject Area**: Inferred based on table names.\\r\\n• **Data Sources & Partitions**:\\r\\n  1. **Source Types**: Extracted from M queries.\\r\\n  2. **Connection Mode**: Import, DirectQuery, or Dual.\\r\\n  3. **Source Locations**: Databases or file paths.\\r\\n• **Data Model Architecture**:\\r\\n  1. **Tables**: Infer Fact vs. Dimension based on relationships.\\r\\n  2. **Relationships**: From/To, Cardinality, and Cross-Filter Direction.\\r\\n  3. **Calculated Tables**: Name and DAX definition.\\r\\n• **Measures & KPIs** (Table format):\\r\\n  1. **Details**: Measure Name, Home Table, and DAX Expression.\\r\\n  2. **Complexity Flag**: Flag heavy iterators like SUMX or complex CALCULATE modifiers for performance review.\\r\\n• **Security (Row-Level Security)**:\\r\\n  1. **Details**: Role Name, Filtered Tables, and DAX Filter Expressions.\\r\\n• **Advanced Features** (If present):\\r\\n  1. **Features**: Document Calculation Groups, Field Parameters, and Incremental Refresh Policies.\\r\\n• **Out of Scope (Report Layer)**:\\r\\n  1. **Disclaimer**: Explicitly state that Report Pages, Visuals, Bookmarks, and Workspace metadata are not documented as they reside outside the semantic model accessible by the MCP server.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Output in Structured Markdown using tables where applicable.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide structured, audit-ready technical documentation extracted directly from the connected model, including table/measure inventory, relationship mappings, DAX definitions with complexity flags, RLS roles, data source details, and advanced feature usage — all in Markdown tables with no assumptions or inferences.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"Development\": [\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Perform data profiling (row count, null %, duplicates, distributions)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Perform comprehensive data profiling on below sources\\r\\nSource:\\r\\n**Provide System Context here**\\r\\n\\r\\n**Profiling Requirements:**\\r\\n\\r\\n• Total row count and column count\\r\\n• Data types for each column\\r\\n• Null count and null percentage per column\\r\\n• Duplicate row count (full row duplicates)\\r\\n• Unique value count per column\\r\\n• Summary statistics for numeric columns (min, max, mean, median, std dev)\\r\\n• Top 10 value distribution for categorical columns (count + %)\\r\\n• Identify data quality issues:\\r\\n• Columns with >30% nulls\\r\\n• Constant columns\\r\\n• High cardinality columns\\r\\n• Skewed numeric distributions\\r\\n\\r\\n**Generate:**\\r\\n• Optimized SQL queries for SQL Server profiling\\r\\n• Or PySpark/Pandas code as per the feasibility for other sources.\\r\\n• Output results in a structured summary report format\\r\\n• Ensure scalability for large datasets\",\n      \"output\": \"The prompt will generate a comprehensive data profiling framework for the specified data source, designed to evaluate structure, quality, and statistical characteristics of the dataset. It will provide optimized SQL Server queries or PySpark/Pandas code to compute metrics such as row/column counts, null percentages, duplicates, unique values, numeric statistics, and categorical value distributions. The results will be organized into a structured profiling report format designed to scale for large datasets.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Create Linked Services for source systems, ADLS/Blob, Key Vault, REST APIs, databases etc.\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are an Azure ETL engineer.\\r\\n\\r\\nCreate a comprehensive set of linked service definitions for the following environments and sources:\\r\\n\\r\\nData lake storage (ADLS Gen2, Azure Blob Storage, OneLake)\\r\\nSecrets management (Azure Key Vault)\\r\\nREST APIs (with authentication)\\r\\nRelational databases (SQL Server, PostgreSQL, Oracle, MySQL)\\r\\nCloud-native services (Snowflake, Databricks, Synapse, Fabric, etc.)\\r\\n\\r\\n**For each linked service, generate:**\\r\\n•Detailed description of the service\\r\\n•Purpose\\r\\n•Security considerations\\r\\n•Connectivity requirements\\r\\n•Authentication method\\r\\n•Network constraints (private endpoints, firewall rules, VNET)\\r\\n•Configuration Template (JSON / YAML / ARM / Terraform style)\\r\\n\\r\\n**Include required properties**\\r\\n•Parameterization for environment variables (dev/test/prod)\\r\\n•Example values (mocked safely, do NOT include secrets)\\r\\n•Optional properties with explanation\\r\\n•Dependency & Ordering Notes\\r\\n\\r\\n**Final Summary on** \\r\\n•Linked Service Name\\r\\n•Type (ADLS / Blob / REST API / Database / Key Vault)\\r\\n•Description\\r\\n•Configuration Template\\r\\n•Edge Cases & Recommendations\",\n      \"output\": \"The prompt will generate a comprehensive set of Azure Linked Service designs for multiple data sources. For each service, it will include detailed descriptions, purpose, authentication methods, security considerations, network requirements, and connectivity constraints. It will also provide parameterized configuration templates (JSON/YAML/ARM/Terraform) with safe example values suitable for dev/test/prod environments. Finally, the document will include a summary table listing linked service names, types, descriptions, templates, and key recommendations or edge cases.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Create pipelines for full load and incremental load\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a senior data engineering architect and Azure Data Factory / Synapse / Data Pipeline expert.\\r\\nCreate a comprehensive design and JSON templates for pipelines that support the following system context: \\r\\n1. Full Load\\r\\n2. Incremental Load (using watermark / delta logic)\\r\\n\\r\\nSystem Context:\\r\\n**Describe source system(s), target storage or database, Load Type .**\\r\\n**Provide Context here** \\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements:\\r\\n1. Pipeline Design\\r\\n• Separate pipelines for full load and incremental load\\r\\n• Modular and parameterized design\\r\\n• Support multi-environment deployment (dev/test/prod)\\r\\n• Idempotent and safe to rerun\\r\\n• Include error handling and retry policies\\r\\n• Logging and monitoring hooks\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Activities\\r\\nInclude the following activities in JSON templates:\\r\\n• Lookup / Get Max Watermark\\r\\n• Copy Data (source → staging → target)\\r\\n• Data Validation (row counts, checksums)\\r\\n• Stored Procedure / SQL Script execution\\r\\n• If Condition / Conditional Execution\\r\\n• ForEach loops (if partitioned)\\r\\n• Set Variable / Parameter handling\\r\\n• Error / Failure handling (Retry / Alert / DLQ)\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Watermark / Delta Logic\\r\\n• Incremental load should include watermark column logic\\r\\n• Handle late-arriving or out-of-order data\\r\\n• Store watermark state in metadata table or file\\r\\n• Example: last_updated_timestamp, version_number, or surrogate key\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Pipeline JSON Output\\r\\n• Provide JSON templates for both full load and incremental load pipelines\\r\\n• Include all activities and dependencies\\r\\n• Parameterized for source, target, schema, and environment\\r\\n• Include placeholders for credentials and connection names\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Best Practices\\r\\n• Avoid hardcoding\\r\\n• Include retry policies and exponential backoff\\r\\n• Include logging for audit and reconciliation\\r\\n• Performance optimization tips (parallel copy, partitioning)\\r\\n• Safe rerun and idempotency strategy\\r\\n\\r\\n6. Edge Cases & Recovery\\r\\n• Partial failure handling\\r\\n• Reprocessing logic\\r\\n• Deduplication on rerun\\r\\n• Handling of schema drift\\r\\n• Alerting strategy for failed runs\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput Structure:\\r\\n\\r\\n• Pipeline Name\\r\\n• Type (Full / Incremental)\\r\\n• JSON Template (activities, dependencies, parameters)\\r\\n• Description of each activity\\r\\n• Watermark / Delta logic explanation\\r\\n• Error handling and recovery strategy\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nConstraints:\\r\\n\\r\\n• Assume distributed system\\r\\n• Assume large-scale datasets (100M+ rows)\\r\\n• Assume multiple source tables\",\n      \"output\": \"[Describe source system(s), target storage or database, Load Type.]\\r\\n\\r\\n1. abfss://mycontainer@myadlsaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/folder1/folder2/date/ (contains multiple Parquet files), target:  abfss://mycontainer@myadlsaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/folder1/folder2/target/ with the same structure as the source, but the only change is the target folder. Load type: Incremental with respect to date.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. SQL Server - sqlserver1, ADLS - abfss://mycontainer@myadlsaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/SQL/ (output file). Load type: Full load.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe prompt will generate JSON templates based on the system context provided above.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement watermark logic for incremental ingestion\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a senior data engineer and ETL architect specialized in incremental ingestion pipelines.\\r\\n\\r\\nTask:\\r\\nImplement watermark-based incremental ingestion from source to target systems, and generate pipeline JSON with activities ready for deployment.\\r\\n\\r\\nSystem Context:\\r\\n**Provide source system(s), target storage/database, watermark column**\\r\\n**Provide context here**\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Watermark Logic\\r\\n• Identify new or updated records using a reliable column (timestamp, version, or surrogate key)\\r\\n• Store the last successfully processed watermark in a metadata table or file\\r\\n• Include rollback/retry strategy in case of failures\\r\\n• Support parallel processing across multiple tables or partitions\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Incremental Load Patterns\\r\\nGenerate implementation-ready logic patterns for:\\r\\n• SQL query extracting only new/updated rows\\r\\n• Pipeline Copy activity (source → staging → target)\\r\\n• Delta / Merge / Upsert patterns for target tables\\r\\n• Parameterized pseudocode or code snippets\\r\\n• Handling partitions or batch chunks\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Pipeline JSON\\r\\nCreate full pipeline JSON including:\\r\\n• Activities: Lookup, Copy Data, If Condition, ForEach, Stored Procedure / SQL Script, Set Variable, Validation activities\\r\\n• Parameters for source, target, schema, environment\\r\\n• Dependencies between activities\\r\\n• Retry and error-handling policies\\r\\n• Watermark read/update activity\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Metadata & Observability\\r\\n• Watermark tracking table design example:\\r\\n• Columns: pipeline_name, table_name, last_watermark_value, last_run_id, run_status, updated_at\\r\\n• Logging row counts and errors\\r\\n• Alerting on missing or invalid watermark\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Best Practices\\r\\n• Avoid full table scans\\r\\n• Use partitioned reads if applicable\\r\\n• Ensure consistent data types\\r\\n• Parameterize all sensitive info (connection strings, keys)\\r\\n• Include performance and scalability considerations\\r\\n\\r\\n6. Output Requirements\\r\\n• Watermark tracking table schema\\r\\n• Incremental load query/pipeline snippet\\r\\n• Step-by-step explanation of watermark logic\\r\\n• JSON pipeline template with all activities, parameters, and dependencies\\r\\n• Error handling & recovery strategy\\r\\n• Late-arriving data handling strategy\\r\\n• Observability / logging recommendations\\r\\n• Idempotency & deduplication strategy\\r\\n\\r\\nConstraints:\\r\\n• Assume distributed system\\r\\n• Assume large datasets (100M+ rows)\\r\\n• Support reruns and partial failures\\r\\n• Parameterize environment-specific variables\\r\\n• Avoid vague guidance\",\n      \"output\": \"[Provide source system(s), target storage/database, watermark column]\\r\\nADLS -1 , ADLS - 2 , LastUpdatedDate\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on the system context, the prompt will generate the JSON templates for incremental load and update the watermark table.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement dynamic file naming logic\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a Principal Azure Data Engineer. Your task is to generate a complete, production-ready, metadata-driven ingestion framework supporting FULL and INCREMENTAL loads across Azure Data Factory (ADF), Microsoft Fabric, Azure Synapse, and Databricks (PySpark).\\r\\n \\r\\nSTRICT EXECUTION RULES:\\r\\n1. NO SNIPPETS. Provide the COMPLETE, copy-pasteable PySpark notebook code and FULL SQL scripts. \\r\\n2. No conversational filler or introductory fluff. Output code in structured markdown blocks.\\r\\n3. Every solution must be 100% parameterized with zero hardcoded values.\\r\\n \\r\\n 1. Mandatory Parameters\\r\\nThe framework (Pipelines & PySpark) must accept and utilize the following parameters dynamically:\\r\\n• p_source_type (Table | File) — Indicates whether the source is a database table or a raw file.\\r\\n• p_source_name / p_source_file_details (string) — Example: schema.table OR /raw/source_system/file.csv\\r\\n• p_target_table_name (string) — Name of the target table where the data will be loaded.\\r\\n• p_load_type (FULL | INCREMENTAL) — Defines whether the pipeline performs a full load or incremental load.\\r\\n• p_watermark_column (string) — Column used to track incremental changes (e.g., ModifiedDate, CreatedDate, ID).\\r\\n• p_incremental_granularity (DAY | MONTH | ID | CUSTOM) — Determines incremental processing logic.\\r\\n• p_custom_filter (string, optional) — Custom filtering condition when CUSTOM incremental logic is used.\\r\\n• p_partition_column (string) — MUST be used to partition the data at the destination (e.g., YearMonth, EventDate).\\r\\n• p_target_base_path (string) — Base path where the processed data will be stored.\\r\\n• p_file_format (parquet | delta | csv) — Specifies the output file format for the target dataset.\\r\\n2. SQL DDL: Metadata Control Table\\r\\nProvide the COMPLETE SQL DDL for a scalable control table supporting multiple sources.\\r\\nColumns: SourceType, SourceName, TargetTableName, WatermarkColumn, PartitionColumn, IncrementalGranularity, LastWatermarkValue, MaxWatermarkInLastRun, LoadType, LastRunStatus, LastRunStartTime, LastRunEndTime, IsActive.\\r\\n- Include the complete SQL Stored Procedure to update this table (On Success / On Failure).\\r\\n \\r\\n 3. Dynamic Path, Partitioning & File Naming Logic\\r\\nThe target folder structure MUST auto-adjust based on the incremental granularity and the requested partition column. \\r\\n- DAY Incremental: `/base_path/<source>/<table>/<partition_column_value>/<year>/<month>/<day>/`\\r\\n- MONTH Incremental: `/base_path/<source>/<table>/<partition_column_value>/<year>/<month>/`\\r\\n \\r\\nFile Name Format: `<Source>_<Table>_<LoadType>_<PartitionColumnValue>_<yyyyMMddHHmmss>.<format>`\\r\\n \\r\\nProvide the exact string interpolation/expressions to generate these paths and filenames for:\\r\\n1. ADF / Synapse / Fabric Pipelines (Dynamic Content Expressions)\\r\\n2. Databricks (PySpark)\\r\\n \\r\\n 4. COMPLETE PySpark Implementation (The Core Engine)\\r\\nGenerate the FULL, production-grade PySpark code (Databricks/Fabric compatible). \\r\\nThe PySpark code MUST explicitly execute the following:\\r\\n1. Read parameters (using dbutils.widgets).\\r\\n2. Read the Control Table to fetch the LastWatermarkValue.\\r\\n3. Dynamically read from the source (handle `p_source_type` = Table vs File).\\r\\n4. Apply the Incremental Logic:\\r\\n   - DAY: `filter(col(watermark) > last_watermark)`\\r\\n   - MONTH: trunc date comparison\\r\\n   - ID: `filter(col(watermark) > last_watermark)`\\r\\n   - CUSTOM: apply `p_custom_filter`\\r\\n5. Extract the MAX watermark from the newly read dataframe.\\r\\n6. Write the data to the target:\\r\\n   - MUST explicitly use `.partitionBy(p_partition_column)`.\\r\\n   - MUST save the file using the dynamically generated File Name and Path from Section 3.\\r\\n7. Wrap in robust Try/Catch block. If successful, write back to the Control Table with the new MAX watermark. If failed, log the error, update Control Table status to 'FAILED', and raise the exception.\\r\\n  5. ADF / Fabric / Synapse Orchestration\\r\\nProvide the exact dynamic expressions needed inside the pipeline activities:\\r\\n1. Lookup Activity: Query to fetch control table metadata.\\r\\n2. Notebook Activity: Passing all parameters to the PySpark notebook.\\r\\n3. On-Failure path logic.\",\n      \"output\": \"Metadata-Driven Enterprise Ingestion Framework\\r\\nThis agent provides a production-hardened, unified ingestion framework designed for scalability and idempotency across Azure Databricks, Fabric, and Synapse. It delivers a centralized Metadata Control Table.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement schema validation during ingestion\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Principal Azure Data Engineer responsible for designing secure, enterprise-grade, metadata-driven ingestion frameworks across Azure Data Factory (ADF), Microsoft Fabric, Azure Synapse Analytics, and Databricks.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nGenerate a complete, production-ready, metadata-driven ingestion framework supporting FULL and INCREMENTAL loads utilizing dynamic parameters, a metadata control table, and scalable PySpark logic.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need to build a reusable, 100% parameterized ingestion engine that handles both database tables and raw files, automatically manages watermarks, and dynamically routes data into partitioned data lake directories without any hardcoded values.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Source Type:** {Add Source Type, e.g., Table | File}\\r\\n**Source Name / File Details:** {Add Source Name, e.g., schema.table OR /raw/source_system/file.csv}\\r\\n**Target Table Name:** {Add Target Table Name}\\r\\n**Load Type:** {Add Load Type, e.g., FULL | INCREMENTAL}\\r\\n**Watermark Column:** {Add Watermark Column, e.g., ModifiedDate, CreatedDate, ID}\\r\\n**Incremental Granularity:** {Add Granularity, e.g., DAY | MONTH | ID | CUSTOM}\\r\\n**Custom Filter:** {Add Custom Filter (Optional)}\\r\\n**Partition Column:** {Add Partition Column, e.g., YearMonth, EventDate}\\r\\n**Target Base Path:** {Add Target Base Path}\\r\\n**File Format:** {Add File Format, e.g., parquet | delta | csv}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nCreate a complete, production-ready implementation. Do not use conversational filler or introductory fluff. Provide COMPLETE, copy-pasteable PySpark notebook code and FULL SQL scripts (no snippets). Every solution must be 100% parameterized with zero hardcoded values.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n1. SQL DDL for Metadata Control Table:\\r\\n• Provide the complete SQL DDL for a scalable control table supporting multiple sources.\\r\\n• Required Columns: SourceType, SourceName, TargetTableName, WatermarkColumn, PartitionColumn, IncrementalGranularity, LastWatermarkValue, MaxWatermarkInLastRun, LoadType, LastRunStatus, LastRunStartTime, LastRunEndTime, IsActive.\\r\\n• Include the complete SQL Stored Procedure to update this table (On Success / On Failure).\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Dynamic Path, Partitioning, and File Naming Logic:\\r\\n• The target folder structure MUST auto-adjust based on the incremental granularity and the requested partition column.\\r\\n• For DAY Incremental: /base_path/<source>/<table>/<partition_column_value>/<year>/<month>/<day>/\\r\\n• For MONTH Incremental: /base_path/<source>/<table>/<partition_column_value>/<year>/<month>/\\r\\n• Define the File Name Format exactly as: <Source>_<Table>_<LoadType>_<PartitionColumnValue>_<yyyyMMddHHmmss>.<format>\\r\\n• Provide the exact string interpolation and expressions to generate these paths and filenames for ADF/Synapse/Fabric Pipelines (Dynamic Content Expressions) and Databricks (PySpark).\\r\\n\\r\\n3. COMPLETE PySpark Implementation (The Core Engine):\\r\\n• Generate the FULL, production-grade PySpark code (Databricks/Fabric compatible).\\r\\n• The code MUST explicitly read all parameters using dbutils.widgets.\\r\\n• The code MUST read the Control Table to fetch the LastWatermarkValue.\\r\\n• The code MUST dynamically read from the source handling the Source Type parameter (Table vs File).\\r\\n• Apply the Incremental Logic based on Granularity (DAY: filter col > last_watermark, MONTH: trunc date comparison, ID: filter col > last_watermark, CUSTOM: apply custom filter).\\r\\n• Extract the MAX watermark from the newly read dataframe.\\r\\n• Write the data to the target explicitly using .partitionBy(Partition Column) and save using the dynamically generated File Name and Path.\\r\\n• Wrap the entire process in a robust Try/Catch block. If successful, write back to the Control Table with the new MAX watermark. If failed, log the error, update Control Table status to FAILED, and raise the exception.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. ADF / Fabric / Synapse Orchestration:\\r\\n• Provide the exact dynamic expressions needed inside the pipeline activities.\\r\\n• Provide the Lookup Activity query to fetch control table metadata.\\r\\n• Provide the exact syntax for passing all parameters to the Notebook Activity.\\r\\n• Provide the On-Failure path logic.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**: \\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown\\r\\n\\r\\nSQL DDL and Stored Procedure complete code blocks\\r\\nDynamic Path and File Naming Expressions (ADF and PySpark)\\r\\nComplete PySpark Implementation (The Core Engine) code block\\r\\nADF/Fabric/Synapse Orchestration dynamic expressions\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent provides a production-hardened, unified ingestion framework designed for scalability and idempotency across Azure Databricks, Fabric, and Synapse. It delivers a centralized Metadata Control Table architecture to govern FULL and INCREMENTAL loads, coupled with a high-performance PySpark Engine that dynamically handles schema-to-table or file-to-table migrations. By utilizing parameterized partitioning and automated watermark management, this framework ensures that daily, monthly, or ID-based increments are captured precisely, audited, and stored in a structured presentation layer.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement logging & audit activities (start time, end time, counts, status)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Principal Data Engineer responsible for designing high-performance, platform-agnostic data engineering frameworks and developer utilities.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nGenerate a highly abstracted, plug-and-play Python logging utility for Spark-based ingestion notebooks, along with a minimalist notebook template demonstrating its usage.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need a standardized logging framework to improve the developer experience (DX) and maintain high performance. The framework must abstract away logging complexity without degrading Spark performance. Logs must be written as structured JSON to standard output (using Python's logging module), which native Azure telemetry (Log Analytics / App Insights) can automatically capture.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Notebook Name:** {Add Notebook Name, e.g., load_dim_customer}\\r\\n**Source Path:** {Add Source Path, e.g., /raw/sales/data.csv}\\r\\n**Target Table:** {Add Target Table, e.g., gold.dim_customer}\\r\\n**Environment:** {Add Environment, e.g., dev | test | prod}\\r\\n**Pipeline Run ID:** {Add Pipeline Run ID}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nProvide the COMPLETE, copy-pasteable PySpark code. Do not use conversational filler. Output code in structured markdown blocks. The solution MUST be 100% platform-agnostic (ADF, Fabric, Synapse, Databricks).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n1. The Logging Utility Class (IngestionLogger):\\r\\n• Generate a robust Python class using standard logging and json modules.\\r\\n• Implement __init__(notebook_name, source_path, target_table, environment, pipeline_run_id) to initialize the logger, store context, and configure the Python logging module to output JSON strings.\\r\\n• Implement start() to immediately log a structured JSON message with executionStatus = 'STARTED' and the UTC start time.\\r\\n• Implement end_success(rows_written=None, target_df=None, dq_error_count=0, bad_record_count=0) to set status to 'COMPLETED' and log the UTC end time.\\r\\n• CRITICAL PERFORMANCE RULE: end_success must accept either an integer rows_written OR a target_df. If target_df is passed, it must safely calculate .count(), but include inline comments warning the developer about Spark DAG re-computation if the DF is not cached.\\r\\n• Implement end_failure(exception_object) to accept the Python exception object, extract the error type, message, and stacktrace. Log a structured JSON message with executionStatus = 'FAILED' and the UTC end time. It MUST explicitly RE-RAISE the exception so the orchestrator (ADF/Fabric) fails the activity.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. The Notebook Template (Usage Guide):\\r\\n• Generate a clean PySpark notebook template demonstrating how a developer uses this utility.\\r\\n• Cell 1: Parameter Initialization (Dynamic platform agnostic code to get pipeline_run_id, env, source, target).\\r\\n• Cell 2: Initialization and Start (Instantiate IngestionLogger and call start()).\\r\\n• Cell 3: Core Logic Wrapped in Try/Except (Developer ingestion logic).\\r\\n• Cell 4: End Success (Passing integer to avoid DAG re-compute or target_df).\\r\\n• Cell 5: End Failure (Call end_failure(e)).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**: \\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown\\r\\n\\r\\nThe complete IngestionLogger utility class Python code block\\r\\nThe minimalist Notebook Template Python code block\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent provides a production-grade, platform-agnostic logging framework designed to standardize telemetry across Databricks, Fabric, Synapse, and ADF. It delivers the IngestionLogger class, which serializes notebook metadata into structured JSON strings for automatic capture by Azure Monitor and Log Analytics. By abstracting the logging lifecycle into start(), end_success(), and end_failure() methods, this utility ensures high developer experience (DX) while protecting Spark performance through optimized row-counting logic and native exception re-raising.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement failure handling workflows & alerting mechanisms\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Principal Data Engineer responsible for designing high-performance, platform-agnostic data engineering frameworks and developer utilities.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nGenerate a highly abstracted, plug-and-play Python logging utility for Spark-based ingestion notebooks, along with a minimalist notebook template demonstrating its usage.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need a standardized logging framework to improve the developer experience (DX) and maintain high performance. The framework must abstract away logging complexity without degrading Spark performance. Logs must be written as structured JSON to standard output (using Python's logging module), which native Azure telemetry (Log Analytics / App Insights) can automatically capture.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Notebook Name:** {Add Notebook Name, e.g., load_dim_customer}\\r\\n**Source Path:** {Add Source Path, e.g., /raw/sales/data.csv}\\r\\n**Target Table:** {Add Target Table, e.g., gold.dim_customer}\\r\\n**Environment:** {Add Environment, e.g., dev | test | prod}\\r\\n**Pipeline Run ID:** {Add Pipeline Run ID}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nProvide the COMPLETE, copy-pasteable PySpark code. Do not use conversational filler. Output code in structured markdown blocks. The solution MUST be 100% platform-agnostic (ADF, Fabric, Synapse, Databricks).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n1. The Logging Utility Class (IngestionLogger):\\r\\n• Generate a robust Python class using standard logging and json modules.\\r\\n• Implement __init__(notebook_name, source_path, target_table, environment, pipeline_run_id) to initialize the logger, store context, and configure the Python logging module to output JSON strings.\\r\\n• Implement start() to immediately log a structured JSON message with executionStatus = 'STARTED' and the UTC start time.\\r\\n• Implement end_success(rows_written=None, target_df=None, dq_error_count=0, bad_record_count=0) to set status to 'COMPLETED' and log the UTC end time.\\r\\n• CRITICAL PERFORMANCE RULE: end_success must accept either an integer rows_written OR a target_df. If target_df is passed, it must safely calculate .count(), but include inline comments warning the developer about Spark DAG re-computation if the DF is not cached.\\r\\n• Implement end_failure(exception_object) to accept the Python exception object, extract the error type, message, and stacktrace. Log a structured JSON message with executionStatus = 'FAILED' and the UTC end time. It MUST explicitly RE-RAISE the exception so the orchestrator (ADF/Fabric) fails the activity.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. The Notebook Template (Usage Guide):\\r\\n• Generate a clean PySpark notebook template demonstrating how a developer uses this utility.\\r\\n• Cell 1: Parameter Initialization (Dynamic platform agnostic code to get pipeline_run_id, env, source, target).\\r\\n• Cell 2: Initialization and Start (Instantiate IngestionLogger and call start()).\\r\\n• Cell 3: Core Logic Wrapped in Try/Except (Developer ingestion logic).\\r\\n• Cell 4: End Success (Passing integer to avoid DAG re-compute or target_df).\\r\\n• Cell 5: End Failure (Call end_failure(e)).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**: \\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown\\r\\n\\r\\nThe complete IngestionLogger utility class Python code block\\r\\nThe minimalist Notebook Template Python code block\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent provides a production-grade, platform-agnostic logging framework designed to standardize telemetry across Databricks, Fabric, Synapse, and ADF. It delivers the IngestionLogger class, which serializes notebook metadata into structured JSON strings for automatic capture by Azure Monitor and Log Analytics. By abstracting the logging lifecycle into start(), end_success(), and end_failure() methods, this utility ensures high developer experience (DX) while protecting Spark performance through optimized row-counting logic and native exception re-raising.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Create schedule triggers and event-based triggers as needed\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Senior Data Engineer / DevOps Specialist** tasked with the design and implementation of **Pipeline Triggers (Scheduled & Event-Based)** for our data ingestion workflows.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Orchestration Platform**: {e.g., Azure Data Factory (ADF) / Synapse / Databricks Jobs}\\r\\n• **Target Pipeline/Notebook Name**: {e.g., PL_Master_Ingest / Ingest_Daily_Raw}\\r\\n• **Output Format Needed**: {e.g., ARM Template (JSON) / Python (DAG) / PowerShell Script}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease generate the configuration or code to create the following two types of triggers based on the Orchestration Platform listed above.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Scheduled Trigger (Time-Based)**\\r\\n• **Name**: Trigger_Daily_Batch\\r\\n• **Frequency**: {e.g., Daily / Weekly}\\r\\n• **Time**: {e.g., 06:00 UTC}\\r\\n• **Concurrency**: Ensure that if the previous run is still running, the new run {e.g., Queues / Does not start / Runs in parallel}.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Event-Based Trigger (Storage Events)**\\r\\n• **Name**: Trigger_On_File_Arrival\\r\\n• **Event Source**: {e.g., Azure Blob Storage}\\r\\n• **Event Type**: {e.g., BlobCreated / ObjectCreated}\\r\\n• **Path Filter**:\\r\\n  1. **Container/Bucket**: {e.g., raw-data}\\r\\n  2. **Folder/Prefix**: {e.g., landing/sales/}\\r\\n  3. **File Extension**: {e.g., .csv / .parquet}\\r\\n• **Logic**: The trigger should pass the `@triggerBody().fileName` (or equivalent) as a parameter to the pipeline.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Provide the exact code block or JSON definition required to deploy these triggers.\\r\\n• If using a UI-based tool (like ADF), provide a bulleted list of the standard settings to configure.\\r\\n• Include a comment on how to start/activate the trigger after deployment.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide the code block or JSON configuration required to deploy scheduled time-based and event-driven storage triggers, detailing concurrency rules and dynamic file-name parameters, along with UI configuration steps and instructions for activating the triggers post-deployment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement dependency checks (file arrival, upstream completion, control tables)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Principal Data Engineer responsible for robust data orchestration, dependency management, and file arrival polling architectures across Azure Data Factory (ADF), Microsoft Fabric, Azure Synapse, and Azure Databricks.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nGenerate a robust \\\"Dependency Check & File Sniffer\\\" framework that handles file arrival polling and upstream control table validation to ensure downstream pipelines do not execute prematurely.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need a gating mechanism that halts pipeline execution until two exact conditions are met: the upstream control table confirms the upstream dependency has successfully completed today, and the expected files have physically arrived in the target location.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Source Data Location:** {Add Source Path, e.g., abfss://landing@myaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/sales/inbound/}\\r\\n**Expected File Pattern:** {Add File Pattern, e.g., sales_daily_*.parquet}\\r\\n**Check Modification Date?:** {Add YES or NO, e.g., YES - files must be modified today}\\r\\n**Max Wait Time (Timeout SLA):** {Add Timeout SLA in Minutes, e.g., 60}\\r\\n**Upstream Control Table:** {Add Control Table, e.g., audit.pipeline_runs}\\r\\n**Upstream Job Name:** {Add Upstream Job, e.g., SAP_Daily_Extract}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nProvide the COMPLETE, copy-pasteable PySpark notebook code and pipeline expressions. Do not use conversational filler. Output code in structured markdown blocks. The PySpark solution MUST handle polling (waiting for files) with configurable timeouts and sleep intervals.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n1. The Goal: File Arrival and Upstream Sniffer:\\r\\n• Create a robust gating mechanism that halts pipeline execution until both conditions are met.\\r\\n• Condition 1: The Upstream Control Table confirms the upstream dependency (Upstream Job Name) has successfully completed today.\\r\\n• Condition 2: The expected files matching the Expected File Pattern have physically arrived in the Source Data Location (and, if requested, were modified today).\\r\\n\\r\\n2. The PySpark Sniffer Utility (DependencySniffer):\\r\\n• Generate a complete Python class (compatible with Databricks/Fabric/Synapse).\\r\\n• Implement __init__(...) to accept the user context parameters dynamically.\\r\\n• Implement check_upstream_status() to query the control table. If the upstream job status is not 'COMPLETED' for the current date, raise a DependencyNotMetError.\\r\\n• Implement poll_for_files(polling_interval_sec=60) using dbutils.fs (or mssparkutils.fs dynamically) to list files in the source directory.\\r\\n• The poller must loop and check if files matching the pattern exist.\\r\\n• If Check Modification Date is YES, filter out files older than today's date.\\r\\n• Sleep and retry until the files arrive OR the Max Wait Time SLA is exceeded.\\r\\n• If it times out, explicitly raise a TimeoutError to fail the orchestrator pipeline.\\r\\n• Implement execute_checks() to run both the upstream check and the file poller.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Native ADF / Fabric Pipeline Equivalent (The Orchestrator Approach):\\r\\n• Provide the exact configuration and dynamic content expressions for ADF and Fabric Pipelines using native UI activities.\\r\\n• Define a Validation Activity or Get Metadata + Until Loop pattern to sniff the exact Source Data Location and File Pattern.\\r\\n• Define a Lookup Activity query to check the Control Table status.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**: \\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown\\r\\n\\r\\nThe complete DependencySniffer PySpark class and execution cell utilizing the User Context variables code block\\r\\nThe exact UI configuration steps and dynamic expressions for the ADF/Fabric pipeline equivalent code block\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent provides a production-grade gating framework designed to prevent \\\"phantom executions\\\" in downstream pipelines. It delivers a dual-layered validation strategy: a DependencySniffer PySpark class for complex polling logic and an equivalent ADF/Fabric Orchestrator pattern for native UI-driven workflows. By synchronizing upstream metadata status with physical file arrival, this framework ensures that your ingestion pipelines only trigger when data is both logically complete and physically present.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Configure ADF monitoring dashboards\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Role:\\r\\nYou are a Senior Azure Data Engineer responsible for implementing monitoring and observability for Azure Data Factory.\\r\\n \\r\\nContext:\\r\\nWe need to configure monitoring dashboards for Azure Data Factory to track pipeline runs, failures, performance, and operational health. The goal is to enable proactive monitoring, troubleshooting, and reporting using Azure-native monitoring tools and best practices.\\r\\n \\r\\nEnvironment:\\r\\n \\r\\nAzure Data Factory:\\r\\n- <Add ADF Name>\\r\\n \\r\\nMonitoring Tools:\\r\\n- <Add Tool> (Azure Monitor / Log Analytics / Azure Dashboard / Power BI / Fabric / Application Insights)\\r\\n \\r\\nEnvironments:\\r\\n- Dev\\r\\n- Test\\r\\n- Prod\\r\\n \\r\\nTask:\\r\\nCreate a practical Monitoring Setup Guide for Azure Data Factory, including dashboard creation, logging configuration, alerting, and best practices.\\r\\n \\r\\nRequirements:\\r\\n \\r\\n- Configure diagnostic settings and send logs to Log Analytics\\r\\n- Explain key monitoring metrics (pipeline runs, activity runs, trigger runs, failures, duration)\\r\\n- Create monitoring dashboards using Azure Monitor / Log Analytics / Power BI\\r\\n- Provide KQL queries for monitoring pipeline status, failures, and performance\\r\\n- Configure alerts for pipeline failures and SLA breaches\\r\\n- Define monitoring strategy for Dev, Test, and Prod environments\\r\\n- Include troubleshooting steps for common pipeline failures\\r\\n- Follow Azure monitoring and observability best practices\\r\\n \\r\\nOutput Format:\\r\\n \\r\\n- Markdown with clear headings and structured sections\\r\\n- Include KQL queries and dashboard examples\\r\\n- Include step-by-step configuration instructions\\r\\n- Include alert configuration examples\\r\\n- Keep it practical, concise, and production-ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Create ingestion control tables (watermark table, audit table, status table)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Senior Data Engineer / Database Architect** tasked with the design and writing of the DDL (Data Definition Language) scripts for a robust **Ingestion Control Framework**.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Target Database Platform**: {e.g., Azure SQL / Snowflake / Databricks Delta / PostgreSQL / Oracle}\\r\\n• **Schema Name**: {e.g., ctl / meta / config}\\r\\n• **Identity Column Logic**: {e.g., IDENTITY(1,1) / AUTOINCREMENT / SEQUENCE / UUID}\\r\\n• **Date Function**: {e.g., GETUTCDATE() / CURRENT_TIMESTAMP / SYSDATE}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease create the SQL CREATE TABLE statements for the following three control tables, adhering to best practices for naming conventions, data types, and indexing for the Target Database Platform listed above.\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Watermark Table (Control_Watermark)**:\\r\\n  1. **Purpose**: To store the high-water mark logic for incremental data loading.\\r\\n  2. **Required Columns**: SourceSystem, TableName, WatermarkColumnName, WatermarkValue (support for both Date and Int types), and LastUpdated.\\r\\n  3. **Constraint**: Ensure (SourceSystem, TableName) is unique.\\r\\n• **Audit Table (Control_Audit_Log)**:\\r\\n  1. **Purpose**: To log the execution history of data pipelines for monitoring and debugging.\\r\\n  2. **Required Columns**: ExecutionID (Unique Run ID), PipelineName, SourceTableName, TargetTableName, StartTime, EndTime, DurationSeconds, Status (Success/Fail), RowsRead, RowsWritten, and ErrorMessage (nullable).\\r\\n  3. **Indexing**: Suggest an index for fast querying by PipelineName and StartTime.\\r\\n• **Status/Queue Table (Control_Batch_Status)**:\\r\\n  1. **Purpose**: To track the active state of specific files or batches currently moving through the system.\\r\\n  2. **Required Columns**: BatchID, FileName (or ObjectName), CurrentProcessingStage (e.g., 'Raw', 'Curated'), Status (e.g., 'Pending', 'Processing', 'Failed'), RetryCount, CreatedDate, and UpdatedDate.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**:\\r\\n• Provide a single SQL block formatted for easy execution.\\r\\n• Include comments explaining the purpose of specific columns.\\r\\n• Include a standard INSERT statement example for initializing a row in the Watermark table.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a single, executable SQL code block containing DDL statements to create three control tables (Control_Watermark, Control_Audit_Log, and Control_Batch_Status), utilizing platform-specific syntax for identity columns, indexes, and unique constraints, alongside explanatory inline comments and a sample INSERT statement for initializing a new high-water mark record.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement metadata-driven pipeline pattern (if required)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a Lead Data Architect and Integration Expert specializing in Enterprise Orchestration using Azure Data Factory (ADF), Synapse, or Microsoft Fabric. You excel at building dynamic, metadata-driven frameworks that minimize manual intervention and maximize scalability.\\r\\n\\r\\n[Context & Vision]\\r\\nThe goal is to design and implement an end-to-end metadata-driven ingestion and refresh framework. This framework must decouple the orchestration logic from the physical data assets, allowing new tables to be onboarded simply by updating a configuration table. We aim for a \\\"Single Source of Truth\\\" for pipeline behavior, using a Parent-Child execution model to handle diverse refresh strategies (Daily, Restatement, FME).\\r\\n\\r\\nProject Configuration (Variables)\\r\\nOrchestration Tool: {ADF / Synapse / Fabric}\\r\\nMetadata Store: {METADATA_TABLE} (Configuration details)\\r\\nTracking Store: {WATERMARK_TABLE} (Delta/Watermark logic)\\r\\nSource/Sink: {SOURCE} to {BRONZE/LAKEHOUSE}\\r\\nLog Store: {INGESTION_LOG_TABLE}\\r\\nRefresh Strategies: [Daily, Restatement, FME]\\r\\nEnvironment: [DEV / UAT / PROD]\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements & Constraints\\r\\nPillar 1: Parent (Orchestrator) Logic\\r\\nLookup & Filter: Retrieve active configurations and identify delta records by comparing {LastModifiedColumn} against {WATERMARK_TABLE}.\\r\\nDynamic Iteration: Use a ForEach activity to iterate over filtered tables, passing runtime parameters (TableName, LoadType, RefreshType, etc.) to child pipelines.\\r\\nPillar 2: Child Pipeline (Execution) Logic\\r\\nDynamic Invocation: Invoke specific logic based on {RefreshType} using Notebooks or Dataflows.\\r\\nIdempotency: Implement schema handling and idempotent writes to ensure data consistency during re-runs.\\r\\nLogging: Record start_time, end_time, row_count, and status in {INGESTION_LOG_TABLE}.\\r\\nPillar 3: Post-Ingestion & Governance\\r\\nWatermark Update: Update {WATERMARK_TABLE} only upon successful child execution.\\r\\nDownstream Triggers: Automatically refresh Semantic Models or trigger dependent table groups.\\r\\nError Handling: Implement centralized retry policies and failure handling across all stages.\\r\\nPillar 4: Technical Standards\\r\\nZero Hardcoding: The framework must be 100% metadata-driven and environment-agnostic.\\r\\nScalability: New tables must be onboardable via metadata entries alone.\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput Format \\r\\nPlease provide the response in Markdown including:\\r\\nMetadata & Log DDL: (Schema for {METADATA_TABLE}, {WATERMARK_TABLE}, and {INGESTION_LOG_TABLE}).\\r\\nParent Pipeline Architecture: (High-level logic flow and activity configuration).\\r\\nChild Pipeline Design: (Parameter mapping and conditional execution logic).\\r\\nTransformation Snippets: (Code/JSON for Notebook or Dataflow implementation).\\r\\nError Handling Workflow: (Visual or descriptive strategy for failure recovery and logging).\\r\\nImplementation Checklist: (Step-by-step guide to deploying the framework across environments).\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent provides a metadata-driven ingestion and refresh framework for ADF/Synapse/Fabric that dynamically processes tables using configurations stored in a metadata table. A parent pipeline identifies tables requiring processing using watermark logic and triggers child pipelines through a ForEach loop with runtime parameters.\\r\\n\\r\\nChild pipelines execute incremental or full loads via Notebooks/Dataflows, ingesting data from the source into the Fabric Lakehouse (Bronze layer) with idempotent writes and schema handling.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe framework includes centralized logging, watermark updates, dependency triggers, and automatic semantic model refresh, ensuring a scalable, parameterized, and re-runnable ingestion architecture.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement schema drift handling mechanism (ADF Mapping Data Flow or PySpark)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are an Azure Data Factory Architect and Data Integration Specialist specializing in resilient, schema-agnostic ingestion patterns and Mapping Data Flow (MDF) optimization.\\r\\n\\r\\n[Context & Vision]\\r\\nThe goal is to design and detail an implementation to handle schema drift using ADF Mapping Data Flows for a Bronze landing pipeline. We must ensure that pipelines are \\\"drift-aware\\\"—meaning they do not fail when source schemas change (added/removed columns or type changes). The system must automatically ingest drifted data while maintaining a detailed audit trail of these changes for downstream governance.\\r\\n\\r\\nProject Configuration (Variables)\\r\\nOrchestration Tool: Azure Data Factory (Mapping Data Flows)\\r\\nSource Type: [e.g., SQL Server, SAP, REST, Parquet]\\r\\nSink/Destination: [e.g., ADLS Gen2 Delta, Azure SQL]\\r\\nMetadata Table: [Name of table storing source-to-sink mappings]\\r\\nLogging Store: INGESTION_LOG_TABLE\\r\\nEnvironment: [DEV / UAT / PROD]\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements & Constraints\\r\\nPillar 1: Data Flow Transformation Settings\\r\\nSource: Enable Allow Schema Drift. Provide guidance on when to enable Infer drifted column types based on performance vs. accuracy.\\r\\nSink: Enable Allow Schema Drift and Auto-mapping. Provide an alternative approach using Rule-based mapping for scenarios where auto-mapping is insufficient.\\r\\nDynamic Parameterization: Show how to pass parameters (Table, Schema, Load Type, Target Path, RunId) from the Pipeline to the Data Flow.\\r\\nPillar 2: Drift Handling Strategy\\r\\nNew Columns: Logic to land them in Bronze automatically without manual intervention.\\r\\nMissing Columns: Logic to populate nulls or default values to prevent pipeline breakage.\\r\\nType Changes: Define the behavior for safe casting and how to handle incompatible type shifts.\\r\\nPillar 3: Pipeline Activity Sequence\\r\\nWorkflow: Define the sequence: Lookup Metadata → ForEach → Execute Data Flow → Log Result.\\r\\nDrift Detection & Logging: Propose a schema for INGESTION_LOG_TABLE to track runId, dataset, detected_columns, action_taken, and status.\\r\\nPillar 4: Best Practices & Guardrails\\r\\nAddress the implications of late-binding and where strict schema enforcement should occur (e.g., Silver/Gold layers).\\r\\nDefine Failure Handling and an alerting strategy for critical drift events.\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput Format\\r\\nPlease provide the response in Markdown including:\\r\\nSection A: Architecture Overview: A conceptual flow of the drift-handling process.\\r\\nSection B: ADF Configuration Checklist: Step-by-step pipeline settings.\\r\\nSection C: Data Flow Transformations: Detailed list of transformations including key expressions and Pattern Rules (byName / byPosition).\\r\\nSection D: Logging Schema & DDL: SQL DDL for the logging table and sample data rows.\\r\\n\\r\\nSection E: Operations & Alerts: Strategy for failure handling and notification triggers.\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent provides a schema-drift–tolerant ingestion design using Azure Data Factory Mapping Data Flow to ensure pipelines continue running even when the source schema changes. The solution enables Allow Schema Drift in the source and sink transformations, with Auto-mapping to automatically land new columns into the Bronze layer. Optional Infer Drifted Column Types helps detect types for new fields dynamically, while rule-based or pattern-based mappings can be used when stricter control is required.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe pipeline passes dynamic parameters (table name, schema, load type, target path, runId) from the pipeline to the data flow and follows a metadata-driven pattern: Lookup metadata → ForEach table → Execute Data Flow → Log results. The drift strategy ensures new columns are ingested automatically, missing columns are filled with null/defaults, and type changes are handled with safe casting.\\r\\n\\r\\nAll drift events and execution details are logged into an INGESTION_LOG_TABLE (runId, dataset, detected columns, action taken, status, error), enabling monitoring, retries, and alerts while keeping Bronze ingestion resilient and scalable.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement archival/purging logic for landing zone files\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are an experienced data engineer who wants to clean up the workspaces by implementing a data purge or archival process for outdated or expired records\\r\\n**Provide System Context**\\r\\n\\r\\nCreate the script for the below:\\r\\nRequirements:\\r\\n1. Identify records eligible for purge based on:\\r\\n• Retention policy (e.g., older than X days/months/years)\\r\\n• Expiration date column\\r\\n• Status flag (e.g., IsActive = false)\\r\\n2. Parameterize retention period (config-driven, not hardcoded)\\r\\n3. Support soft delete (flag update) OR hard delete (physical removal)\\r\\n4. Log purge activity including:\\r\\n• Table name\\r\\n• Number of records deleted\\r\\n• Execution timestamp\\r\\n5. Handle large tables efficiently (batch deletes if needed)\\r\\n6. Include error handling and transaction control\\r\\n7. Make the process reusable for multiple tables\\r\\nFollow best practices for performance, auditability, and recoverability.\",\n      \"output\": \"The prompt will generate a reusable, production-ready purge/archival framework for cleaning outdated data across multiple tables. It will include configurable retention policies, parameterized logic, and support for both soft deletes (status flag) and hard deletes (physical removal). The code will also implement batch processing for large tables, transaction control, error handling, and logging of purge activity such as table name, deleted record count, and execution timestamp.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Set up Unity Catalog with catalog → schema → table governance model\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a senior Data Engineer.\\r\\nGenerate a Databricks Unity Catalog setup script that creates a hierarchical governance model with catalog → schema → table. \\r\\n**Provide system context here:**\\r\\nCatalog, Schema, table and columns\\r\\n\\r\\nThe script should:\\r\\n1. Create a catalog with the name provided in system context.\\r\\n2. Create a schema with the name provided in system context inside the catalog.\\r\\n3. Create a table with the name provided in system context inside the schema with columns provided in the system context\\r\\n4. Assign access privileges as provided in system context:\\r\\nOutput the full  script ready to run in a Databricks notebook.\",\n      \"output\": \"The script is generated for Databricks to create the catalog, schema, and tables, and to assign the required access permissions based on the provided system input. For example \\r\\nSystem Context:\\r\\nCatalog :  Data_POC\\r\\nSchema : Bronze\\r\\ntable and columns: Product with columns id(int), product_name (string), product_family (string)\\r\\ntable and columns: DimTime with columns dateid(int), fiscalmonth (string), fiscalyear (string)\\r\\nAccess :\\r\\n- Catalog-level: Grant `USAGE` to role `data_analyst`.\\r\\n- Schema-level: Grant `CREATE` and `USAGE` to role `data_engineer`.\\r\\n- Table-level: Grant `SELECT` to role `data_scientist` and `INSERT` to role `data_engineer`.\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on this input, the generated script will create the Bronze schema within the Data_POC catalog, create the Product and DimTime tables with the specified columns, and apply the defined access permissions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Create raw → bronze ingestion transformation logic\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Principal Data Engineer responsible for designing secure, modular, and production-ready data ingestion pipelines across Azure Databricks, Azure Synapse, and Microsoft Fabric.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nGenerate a production-ready, highly modular PySpark notebook script for a \\\"Raw to Bronze\\\" ingestion pipeline.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need a standardized, secure, and parameter-driven PySpark notebook to extract raw data, apply base transformations and audit columns, perform sanity checks, and load the results into a Bronze Delta table.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Linked Service / Auth Details:** {Add Auth Details, e.g., Linked Service Name: 'LS_ADLS', or KeyVault scope: 'kv-prod', secret: 'spn-key'}\\r\\n**Source Details:** {Add Source Details, e.g., Format: JSON, Path: abfss://raw@lake.dfs.core.windows.net/sales/2026/02/23/}\\r\\n**Target Bronze Details:** {Add Target Details, e.g., Path: abfss://bronze@lake.dfs.core.windows.net/sales/, Table: default.bronze_sales}\\r\\n**Required Transformations:** {Add Transformations, e.g., Convert all column names to snake_case, cast 'Amount' to Decimal}\\r\\n**Basic Sanity Checks:** {Add Sanity Checks, e.g., 'TransactionID' must not be null, total row count must be > 0}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nProvide the COMPLETE, copy-pasteable PySpark notebook code. Do not use conversational filler. Output code in structured markdown blocks. The code must be parameter-driven, handle authentication securely (no plain-text secrets), and ensure the write operation uses the Delta Lake format.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n\\r\\n• Authentication and Setup:\\r\\n1. Generate the PySpark logic to securely connect to the source storage.\\r\\n2. If the user context mentions a Linked Service (Synapse/Fabric), provide the mssparkutils.credentials.getToken or Linked Service connection logic.\\r\\n3. If the user context mentions Key Vault (Databricks), provide the dbutils.secrets.get logic for Service Principals.\\r\\n\\r\\n• Data Extraction (Raw Layer):\\r\\n1. Read the data from the Source Details using the correct Spark format loader.\\r\\n2. Handle corrupt records gracefully (e.g., use mode=\\\"PERMISSIVE\\\" and columnNameOfCorruptRecord if it is JSON or CSV).\\r\\n\\r\\n• Bronze Transformations:\\r\\n1. Apply the transformations specified in the Required Transformations parameter.\\r\\n2. MANDATORY BRONZE COLUMNS: Regardless of user input, you MUST append the following audit columns to the dataframe: _bronze_ingestion_timestamp (current UTC timestamp) and _source_file_path (using pyspark.sql.functions.input_file_name()).\\r\\n\\r\\n• Sanity Checks (Basic Data Quality):\\r\\n1. Implement the validations defined in the Basic Sanity Checks parameter before writing to the target.\\r\\n2. Use native PySpark filter/count operations.\\r\\n3. If a sanity check fails (e.g., row count is 0, or primary keys are null), explicitly raise ValueError with a descriptive message to fail the pipeline.\\r\\n\\r\\n• Data Loading (Bronze Layer):\\r\\n1. Write the transformed dataframe to the Target Bronze Details in Delta format.\\r\\n2. Use mode(\\\"append\\\") with mergeSchema=\\\"true\\\".\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**: \\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown\\r\\n\\r\\nExactly ONE complete Python code block containing the fully structured PySpark notebook, divided into clear logical sections with inline comments explaining the logic\",\n      \"output\": \"Secure \\\"Raw to Bronze\\\" Ingestion Pipeline\\r\\nThis agent provides a production-hardened PySpark notebook designed for secure, modular, and metadata-driven data ingestion into the Bronze layer. It delivers a fully parameterized script that handles secure authentication via Azure Key Vault or Linked Services, applies required schema transformations, and appends mandatory audit columns such as _bronze_ingestion_timestamp and _source_file_path. Furthermore, the agent integrates a robust \\\"Gatekeeper\\\" logic to execute Sanity Checks (e.g., Null checks, volume validation) before committing data to the Delta Lake.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement bronze → silver cleansing & standardization logic\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Principal Data Engineer responsible for designing modular, scalable, and production-ready data transformation pipelines across Azure Databricks, Azure Synapse, and Microsoft Fabric.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nGenerate a production-ready, highly modular PySpark script for a \\\"Bronze to Silver\\\" ETL pipeline that performs robust MERGE (Upsert) operations and strictly adheres to organizational coding standards.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need a standardized, modular PySpark codebase to cleanse, standardize, deduplicate, and merge incremental data from the Bronze layer into the Silver layer Delta tables.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Organization Coding Standards:** {Add Coding Standards, e.g., PEP8, use modular Python functions with Google-style docstrings, prefix DataFrames with df_, use PySpark native functions}\\r\\n**Source Bronze Details:** {Add Source Details, e.g., Table: default.bronze_sales, Watermark Column: _bronze_ingestion_timestamp}\\r\\n**Target Silver Details:** {Add Target Details, e.g., Table: default.silver_sales, Primary Key(s): ['TransactionID', 'StoreCode'], Partition By: 'sales_month'}\\r\\n**Required Transformations & Cleansing:** {Add Transformations, e.g., Deduplicate by keeping the latest record based on UpdatedDate. Trim whitespace from string columns. Cast OrderDate to DateType.}\\r\\n**End Product Requirements:** {Add Requirements, e.g., Implement SCD Type 1 (Overwrite existing records). Ensure the final schema exactly matches the target table.}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nProvide the COMPLETE, copy-pasteable PySpark notebook code. Do not use conversational filler. Output code in structured markdown blocks. The code MUST strictly adhere to the Organization Coding Standards provided. The pipeline MUST perform a robust MERGE (Upsert) operation using the PySpark DeltaTable API.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n\\r\\n• Modular Design (Coding Standards Enforcement):\\r\\n1. Generate the script using a modular design. Do not write one long procedural block.\\r\\n2. Create distinct Python functions for each logical step with appropriate docstrings.\\r\\n3. Function 1: read_bronze_data(...) to read the incremental Bronze data.\\r\\n4. Function 2: apply_cleansing(...) to handle generic cleansing (e.g., whitespace trimming, null handling).\\r\\n5. Function 3: apply_business_rules(...) to handle the specific standardization and type casting defined in Required Transformations.\\r\\n6. Function 4: deduplicate_records(...) using PySpark Window functions to select the latest record per Primary Key.\\r\\n\\r\\n• Cleansing and Standardization Logic:\\r\\n1. Implement the logic defined in Required Transformations strictly using pyspark.sql.functions (e.g., F.trim(), F.coalesce(), F.upper()).\\r\\n2. Append a mandatory _silver_processing_timestamp column to track when the record was promoted.\\r\\n\\r\\n• Silver Layer MERGE (Upsert) Logic:\\r\\n1. Implement the target load using the DeltaTable.forName() or DeltaTable.forPath() API.\\r\\n2. Construct the dynamic merge condition using the Primary Key(s) defined in Target Silver Details.\\r\\n3. Implement the Upsert logic based on the End Product Requirements (e.g., .whenMatchedUpdateAll() and .whenNotMatchedInsertAll()).\\r\\n4. Include schema evolution logic (mergeSchema) if required by the context.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**: \\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown\\r\\n\\r\\nExactly ONE complete Python script code block containing all the modular functions and the main execution block (if __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\": or sequential execution flow) that ties them together, including comprehensive inline comments and error handling\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent provides a production-hardened PySpark framework designed to promote data from the Bronze to Silver layer using an idempotent UPSERT (MERGE) strategy. Following strict organizational coding standards, the script encapsulates cleansing, type-standardization, and window-based deduplication into modular, testable Python functions. By utilizing the DeltaTable API, the framework ensures that historical records in the Silver layer are updated with the latest state (SCD Type 1) while maintaining a strict schema and appending necessary audit metadata.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement silver → gold aggregations & business transformations\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are an Expert Data Engineer specializing in PySpark, Medallion Architecture (Silver to Gold), and performance tuning for large-scale analytical datasets.\\r\\n\\r\\n[Context & Vision]\\r\\nThe goal is to generate a robust PySpark script to transform data from a Silver layer table to a Gold layer table. The focus is on transitioning from cleaned, normalized data (Silver) to aggregated, business-ready datasets (Gold) that are optimized for reporting and BI tools. We must ensure the code is modular, performant, and follows Delta Lake best practices.\\r\\n\\r\\nProject Configuration (Variables)\\r\\n• Source Table (Silver): <Insert Source Table Name>\\r\\n• Target Table (Gold): <Insert Target Table Name>\\r\\n• Source Schema: <List key columns and their types, e.g., id:string, amount:double>\\r\\n• Write Mode: Overwrite (Delta Format)\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements & Constraints\\r\\n\\r\\n• Pillar 1: Analytical Aggregations\\r\\n1. Metrics: Perform the following calculations: <Specify calculations, e.g., sum(amount), avg(duration)>.\\r\\n2. Grouping: Define the grain of the Gold table: grouped by [Insert Columns, e.g., user_id, transaction_date].\\r\\n3. Naming: All aggregated columns must include descriptive aliases.\\r\\n\\r\\n• Pillar 2: Business Logic & Transformations\\r\\n1. Conditional Logic: Apply specific business rules: <Describe logic, e.g., use F.when() to create a 'status' column based on threshold X>.\\r\\n2. Filtering: Include any specific business filters (e.g., \\\"only include active customers\\\").\\r\\n\\r\\n• Pillar 3: Performance & Standards\\r\\n1. Efficiency: Use native PySpark DataFrame functions (avoid UDFs where possible).\\r\\n2. Documentation: Provide inline comments explaining the transformation steps and business logic.\\r\\n3. Storage: Ensure the output is saved in Delta format.\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput Format\\r\\nPlease provide the response in Markdown including:\\r\\n\\r\\n• Environment Setup: (Necessary imports and Spark session configuration).\\r\\n• Transformation Script: (The complete, ready-to-run PySpark code).\\r\\n• Data Quality Check: (A summary of how nulls or edge cases in the aggregations are handled).\\r\\n• Delta Table DDL: (The SQL command to create or update the Gold table schema).\\r\\n• Optimization Tips: (Brief advice on partitioning or Z-Ordering for this specific Gold dataset).\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent generates a production-ready PySpark script to transform data from the Silver layer to the Gold layer following Medallion Architecture best practices. The script reads the source table, applies business transformations and aggregations using efficient PySpark DataFrame operations, and writes the final dataset to the target table in Delta format with overwrite mode.\\r\\n\\r\\nIt includes clear column aliases for aggregations, implements business logic using PySpark functions, and contains inline comments for readability and maintenance. The result is a scalable and analytics-ready Gold dataset optimized for BI reporting and downstream analytics.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement Delta Lake optimization logic (OPTIMIZE, Z-Order, VACUUM)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a senior Data Engineer.\\r\\nWrite a Databricks Delta Lake optimization SQL/PySpark script that performs optimization based on the system context provided.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Provide System Context:**\\r\\nOptimization to be performed, catalog , schema, table details \\r\\n\\r\\nThe script should:\\r\\n\\r\\n• Take the following user inputs:\\r\\n1. catalog_name: name of the catalog\\r\\n2. schema_name: name of the schema\\r\\n3. table_name: name of the Delta table to optimize\\r\\n4. zorder_columns: list of columns for Z-Ordering (optional)\\r\\n5. retention_hours: number of hours to retain old files for VACUUM (default 168)\\r\\n\\r\\n• Generate SQL/PySpark commands to:\\r\\n1. Optimize the table\\r\\n2. Apply Z-Ordering if zorder_columns are provided\\r\\n3. Vacuum the table with the specified retention period\\r\\n\\r\\n• Include comments explaining each step.\\r\\n\\r\\n• Ensure the script can be run directly in a Databricks notebook.\\r\\n\\r\\n• Don't add to the complexity of the code\",\n      \"output\": \"The script is generated to perform optimization tasks based on the provided system input and may also recommend additional optimization opportunities where applicable. For example \\r\\nSystem Context:\\r\\nPerform OPTIMIZE, Z-ORDER,  VACUUM wherever applicable \\r\\nCatalog  \\r\\nCatalog :  Data_POC\\r\\nSchema : Bronze\\r\\ntable and z-order columns: FactBR and column - date\\r\\nVacuum: Table DateTime with retention for 4 years.\\r\\nApply any other optimization feasible\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on this system context, the generated script will execute the specified optimizations  and will also include additional optimization steps wherever feasible.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement merge/upsert logic for incremental/CDC loads\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are an Expert Data Engineer specializing in Databricks, Spark optimization, and Delta Lake internals. You have deep expertise in implementing the Medallion Architecture and managing complex ACID transactions using Delta Merge logic.\\r\\n\\r\\n[Context & Vision]\\r\\nThe goal is to write a robust PySpark script to perform an incremental data transformation and load it into a target Delta table using an \\\"Upsert\\\" (Merge) strategy. We need to move beyond simple overwrites by implementing a high-performance MERGE INTO pattern that synchronizes source updates and inserts while maintaining data integrity and supporting schema evolution.\\r\\n\\r\\nProject Configuration (Variables)\\r\\n• Source Table/View: <Insert Source Table Name>\\r\\n• Target Delta Table: <Insert Target Delta Table Name>\\r\\n• Primary Key / Merge Condition: <e.g., 'user_id' AND 'event_date'>\\r\\n• Watermark Column: <e.g., updated_at or processing_time>\\r\\n• Incremental Filter: [Insert Logic, e.g., source.updated_at > target.max_watermark]\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements & Constraints\\r\\n\\r\\n• Pillar 1: Incremental Extraction & Transformation\\r\\n1. Watermarking: Implement logic to filter the source data based on the provided watermark column to ensure only new or changed records are processed.\\r\\n2. Aggregations/Logic: Apply the following transformation: <Describe logic, e.g., calculate daily sum of sales per user>.\\r\\n\\r\\n• Pillar 2: Delta Merge (Upsert) Logic\\r\\n1. Match Found: Update existing records in the target table with the new values from the source.\\r\\n2. No Match Found: Insert new records from the source into the target table.\\r\\n3. Implementation: Use the DeltaTable Python API (merge) for better readability and performance.\\r\\n\\r\\n• Pillar 3: Schema & Performance\\r\\n1. Schema Evolution: Ensure the merge operation supports Schema Evolution (e.g., using mergeSchema options) to handle new incoming columns.\\r\\n2. Optimization: Include explicit handling for performance, such as whenNotMatchedBySource (if applicable) or specific join hints.\\r\\n3. Documentation: Provide detailed inline comments explaining the merge criteria and the MATCHED vs NOTMATCHED logic.\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput Format \\r\\nPlease provide the response in Markdown including:\\r\\n\\r\\n• Environment & Imports: (Necessary Spark and Delta Lake library imports).\\r\\n• Watermark Retrieval Logic: (Code to fetch the last processed timestamp from the target).\\r\\n• Transformation & Merge Script: (The complete, parameterized PySpark script).\\r\\n• Schema Evolution Strategy: (Code snippet showing how to handle drifting source columns).\\r\\n• Performance & Tuning Guide: (Recommendations for Z-ORDERING or partitioning to optimize future merges).\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent generates a production-ready PySpark script that performs incremental data transformation and loading into a Delta table using an UPSERT (MERGE) strategy. The script reads the source table, filters new or updated records using a watermark column, and applies the required transformations or aggregations.\\r\\n\\r\\nUsing DeltaTable merge syntax, records are matched based on the defined primary key—existing records are updated, and new records are inserted. The script also supports schema evolution and includes comments explaining the merge logic, ensuring a scalable and efficient pipeline for maintaining curated Delta tables.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement watermark and CDC logic inside notebooks\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a senior data engineer\\r\\nCreate a notebook that implements watermarking and CDC (Change Data Capture) logic. Please generate step-by-step notebook that includes below requirements \\r\\n\\r\\n**Provide system context:**\\r\\n**User Inputs Required:**\\r\\nSource data location (e.g., S3 path, database table)\\r\\nTarget data location (e.g., S3 path, database table)\\r\\nKey column(s) for CDC (primary key or unique identifier)\\r\\nTimestamp column for watermark logic\\r\\nMode of CDC (e.g., append-only, upsert)\\r\\nWatermark table location\\r\\n\\r\\n**Watermark Logic:**\\r\\nAutomatically identify the latest timestamp in the target\\r\\nFilter the source data to only process new or updated records since the last watermark\\r\\nUpdate the watermark after each successful run\\r\\n\\r\\n**CDC Logic:**\\r\\nDetect inserts and updates based on key and timestamp columns\\r\\nMerge changes into the target dataset\\r\\n\\r\\n**Notebook Requirements:**\\r\\nInclude code cells for each step with clear comments\\r\\nInclude functions that can be reused for multiple tables\",\n      \"output\": \"The script generates the starter code for implementing incremental data load patterns based on the provided system context. For example:\\r\\nSource ADLS Path - abfss://salesdata@contosodata.dfs.core.windows.net/2026/source/Invoice/ date wise column\\r\\nTarget ADLS Path - abfss://salesdata@contosodata.dfs.core.windows.net/2026/taget/Invoice/ date wise column\\r\\nKey column(s) for CDC (primary key or unique identifier) - Date column\\r\\nTimestamp column for watermark logic: Date column\\r\\nMode of CDC (e.g., append-only, upsert): Append\\r\\nWatermark table location - abfss://salesdata@contosodata.dfs.core.windows.net/2026/watermarks/\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on this system context, the generated script provides the initial implementation for the incremental load pattern, processes the data from the source to the target, and updates the watermark table to track the last processed timestamp for subsequent runs.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement schema drift/change detection & handling\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a Principal Data Engineer. Generate a production-ready, highly modular PySpark utility class for \\\"Schema Drift and Change Detection\\\". \\r\\n\\r\\nSTRICT EXECUTION RULES:\\r\\n- NO SNIPPETS. Provide the COMPLETE, copy-pasteable PySpark code.\\r\\n- No conversational filler. Output code in structured markdown blocks.\\r\\n- The utility MUST be highly reusable across all notebooks.\\r\\n- Do NOT use string matching for schemas; use deep programmatic comparison of Spark `StructField` objects (name and dataType) to ignore column order differences.\\r\\n- The solution MUST be 100% platform-agnostic (ADF, Fabric, Synapse, Databricks).\\r\\n\\r\\n [USER CONTEXT & SCOPE - READ THIS FIRST]\\r\\n- Metadata Storage Path: <ENTER_METADATA_PATH e.g., abfss://config@lake.dfs.core.windows.net/schemas/>\\r\\n- Table / Entity Name: <ENTER_TABLE_NAME e.g., source_sales_data>\\r\\n- Drift Policy: <ENTER_POLICY e.g., FAIL on removed/changed columns. ALLOW and log added columns.>\\r\\n\\r\\n 1. The Goal: First-Run Storage & Future-Run Comparison\\r\\nCreate a reusable Python class (`SchemaDriftDetector`) that an engineer can invoke in any notebook. \\r\\n- **First Run:** If no baseline schema exists for the `Table Name` at the `Metadata Storage Path`, it must extract the schema from the incoming DataFrame, serialize it natively using `StructType.json()`, save it to the storage path, and return a \\\"Baseline Created\\\" status.\\r\\n- **Future Runs:** It must load the stored JSON, deserialize it back into a PySpark `StructType`, and compare it against the incoming DataFrame's schema.\\r\\n\\r\\n 2. The Utility Class (`SchemaDriftDetector`)\\r\\nGenerate the robust Python class with the following methods:\\r\\n- `__init__(df, table_name, metadata_base_path)`: Initializes the detector with the incoming PySpark DataFrame and routing details.\\r\\n- `_save_schema(schema_struct)`: Saves the `StructType` to the metadata path (abstracting platform-specific file writers using `dbutils.fs.put` or standard Spark write).\\r\\n- `_load_schema()`: Safely attempts to load the schema from the metadata path. Returns `None` if it doesn't exist.\\r\\n- `compare_schemas(baseline_schema, current_schema)`: The core diffing engine. It must return three distinct lists:\\r\\n   1. `added_columns`: Columns in current but not in baseline.\\r\\n   2. `missing_columns`: Columns in baseline but missing in current.\\r\\n   3. `changed_columns`: Columns where the name exists in both, but the `dataType` has changed.\\r\\n- `execute_drift_check()`: The main orchestration method. It handles the First-Run vs Future-Run logic and applies the user's `Drift Policy` (e.g., raising a `SchemaDriftException` if missing/changed columns are detected, or simply printing a warning for added columns).\\r\\n\\r\\n 3. The Notebook Template (Developer Experience)\\r\\nGenerate a clean, 2-cell PySpark notebook template demonstrating how a developer actually imports and uses this utility in their daily ingestion notebooks.\\r\\n\\r\\nCELL 1: Setup\\r\\n ... load incoming df_raw ...\\r\\n_table_name = \\\"raw_transactions\\\"\\r\\np_metadata_path = \\\"abfss://framework@myworkspace/schema_baselines/\\\"\\r\\n\\r\\n CELL 2: Invoke Reusable Drift Check\\r\\ndetector = SchemaDriftDetector(df=df_raw, table_name=p_table_name, metadata_base_path=p_metadata_path)\\r\\n\\r\\ntry:\\r\\n    drift_report = detector.execute_drift_check()\\r\\n    print(drift_report)\\r\\nexcept Exception as e:\\r\\n     Fails the pipeline explicitly if the policy is breached\\r\\n    raise e\\r\\n\\r\\n Deliverables Required\\r\\nProvide exactly two code blocks:\\r\\n1. The complete `SchemaDriftDetector` utility class, fully commented and ready to be saved as a shared module.\\r\\n2. The Notebook Template demonstrating how to call it.\",\n      \"output\": \"Platform-Agnostic Schema Drift & Change Detection Suite\\r\\nThis agent provides a production-grade, reusable PySpark utility designed to enforce strict schema governance across distributed data platforms. It delivers the SchemaDriftDetector class, which leverages native Spark StructType serialization to perform deep programmatic diffing of data structures while remaining agnostic to column ordering. By automating the \\\"First-Run\\\" baseline creation and subsequent \\\"Future-Run\\\" drift analysis, this utility ensures that upstream schema changes are intercepted, logged, or blocked according to your specific organizational drift policies.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement deduplication logic (primary/business keys)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Senior Data Engineer** tasked with the implementation of **Data Deduplication Logic** for a specific DataFrame in my notebook.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Target DataFrame**: {e.g., df_sales}\\r\\n• **Business Key(s)**: {e.g., \\\"TransactionID\\\", \\\"StoreID\\\"}\\r\\n• **Tie-Breaker Column**: {e.g., \\\"ModifiedDate\\\" desc}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease generate the code to deduplicate the Target DataFrame based on the uniqueness of the Business Keys provided above.\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Window Function Strategy (Preferred)**:\\r\\n  1. Use a Window function (`row_number()`) partitioned by the Business Keys.\\r\\n  2. Order by the Tie-Breaker Column to keep the latest record.\\r\\n  3. Filter the result to keep only the row where `rn == 1`.\\r\\n• **Validation**:\\r\\n  1. Include a quick assertion or print statement afterward to verify that `count()` matches `distinct(Business Keys).count()`.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Provide a clean, copy-pasteable code block using the variable names provided.\\r\\n• Include a brief comment on whether a `.repartition()` is recommended before this operation given the keys.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a standalone deduplication code block that isolates unique records using a window function on the business keys, and raises a descriptive error to halt the pipeline if the final row count doesn't match the distinct key count.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement late arriving & outlier data handling strategy\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Senior Data Engineer / Streaming Architect** tasked with proposing a design pattern for **Late Data Handling and Outlier Detection** based on my current notebook.\\r\\n\\r\\nReview the code in my active editor. I need to plan how to handle anomalies in this pipeline. Please analyze the dataframes and transformations visible and suggest a code pattern (without modifying my file yet) for the following:\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Late-Arriving Data Strategy**:\\r\\n  1. Identify which column logically serves as the Event Time.\\r\\n  2. **Proposal**: Show me a code snippet for splitting the stream into two paths:\\r\\n     • `df_current`: Data within a defined threshold (e.g., last 3 days).\\r\\n     • `df_late`: Data older than the threshold (to be routed to a quarantine table).\\r\\n  3. **Question**: Should this be a filter, a separate branch, or a specific Watermark strategy given my current code structure?\\r\\n• **Outlier Detection Strategy**:\\r\\n  1. Identify which Numerical Value column is the best candidate for anomaly detection.\\r\\n  2. **Proposal**: Suggest a snippet to calculate a Z-Score or static threshold logic.\\r\\n  3. **Action**: Show how to add an `is_outlier` flag (Boolean) to the main DataFrame rather than dropping the rows.\\r\\n• **Validation**:\\r\\n  1. Suggest a quick query or print statement I can add to the end of the job to monitor the count of \\\"Late\\\" vs. \\\"Outlier\\\" records per batch.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Provide the suggestions in a Markdown block with comments explaining the logic.\\r\\n• Do not overwrite existing code; just draft the new logic for me to review.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a markdown block with PySpark snippets that examine your active notebook to identify event time and numeric columns, branch the pipeline into current and late data streams, append an is_outlier boolean flag using a Z-score or static threshold, and include a validation query to monitor late and outlier record counts per batch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement audit logging tables (pipeline run, counts, status, errors)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Principal Data Architect responsible for designing robust, enterprise-grade data platform architectures, focusing on metadata management, auditing, and control logging across Azure and Databricks ecosystems.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nGenerate the complete Data Definition Language (DDL) scripts and interaction logic for a robust \\\"Audit and Control Logging\\\" framework.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need to establish a standardized, cross-platform auditing mechanism to track pipeline executions, row counts, and failures across our data ecosystem, supporting both traditional relational databases (Azure SQL/Synapse) and modern data lakes (Databricks/Fabric).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Schema / Database Name:** {Add Schema Name, e.g., metadata, config, or dbo}\\r\\n**Audit Table Name:** {Add Audit Table Name, e.g., pipeline_execution_audit}\\r\\n**Data Types Requirement:** {Add Preference, e.g., Use UTC for all timestamps. Use VARCHAR(MAX) or STRING for error messages.}\\r\\n**Custom Audit Columns Needed:** {Add Custom Columns, e.g., trigger_type, databricks_job_id, compute_cost}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nProvide the COMPLETE, copy-pasteable SQL and PySpark code. Do not use conversational filler. Output code in structured markdown blocks. The DDL must be provided in TWO formats: 1. T-SQL (for Azure SQL / Synapse Dedicated Pools) and 2. Spark SQL / Delta (for Databricks / Fabric Lakehouses). Use proper Identity/Auto-increment syntax specific to each SQL dialect.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n1. The Audit Table DDL (The Schema):\\r\\n• Generate the complete CREATE TABLE scripts for the Audit Table Name in both T-SQL and Spark SQL (Delta format).\\r\\n• The table MUST include the following standard enterprise columns, plus any defined in the Custom Audit Columns Needed: audit_id (Primary Key, Auto-increment/Identity), pipeline_run_id (String/Varchar - Orchestrator ID), pipeline_name (String/Varchar), table_name (String/Varchar), execution_status (String/Varchar e.g., 'STARTED', 'COMPLETED', 'FAILED'), start_time (Timestamp), end_time (Timestamp, Nullable), rows_read (BigInt, Nullable), rows_written (BigInt, Nullable), error_message (String/Varchar, Nullable).\\r\\n\\r\\n2. T-SQL Stored Procedures (For Synapse/ADF Interaction):\\r\\n• Generate two T-SQL Stored Procedures for ADF/Synapse pipelines to call via the Stored Procedure Activity.\\r\\n• Create sp_LogExecutionStart: Inserts a new row with 'STARTED' status and start_time = GETUTCDATE(). It MUST return the newly generated audit_id so the pipeline can pass it to the next step.\\r\\n• Create sp_LogExecutionEnd: Updates the existing row (based on audit_id) with the final status ('COMPLETED' or 'FAILED'), end_time, row counts, and error messages.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. PySpark Delta Interaction Logic (For Databricks/Fabric):\\r\\n• Provide a PySpark utility class (AuditLogger) that interacts with the Delta audit table.\\r\\n• Implement log_start(...) using Spark SQL INSERT INTO to create the initial 'STARTED' record.\\r\\n• Implement log_end(...) using the Delta Lake MERGE INTO or UPDATE API to finalize the record based on the pipeline_run_id and table_name.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**: \\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown\\r\\n\\r\\nT-SQL DDL Script code block\\r\\nSpark SQL (Delta) DDL Script code block\\r\\nT-SQL Stored Procedures (sp_LogExecutionStart and sp_LogExecutionEnd) code block\\r\\nPySpark AuditLogger Utility Class code block\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent provides a production-hardened auditing framework designed to provide end-to-end visibility across hybrid data platforms. It delivers dual-dialect DDL scripts for both relational (T-SQL) and lakehouse (Delta) environments, ensuring your metadata remains synchronized regardless of the compute engine. By utilizing specialized stored procedures for orchestrators and a modular PySpark utility class, this framework ensures comprehensive audit logging.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement control tables (pipeline config, watermark, audit, status)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a senior data engineer\\r\\nCreate a notebook that implements control tables and also generates schemas for each table.\\r\\n\\r\\nControl Tables to Implement with Schema:\\r\\nPipeline Configuration Table: stores pipeline id, name, source/target locations, schedule, is_active\\r\\nWatermark Table: pipeline id, entity name, last_processed_ts, last_record_id\\r\\nAudit Table: stores run metadata including pipeline name, start time, end time, records processed, errors, etc., with correct types.\\r\\nStatus Table: stores pipeline name, current status (running, success, failed), last updated timestamp\\r\\n\\r\\nGenerate the template code for the above\",\n      \"output\": \"AuditLogger\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement data quality rules programmatically (Great Expectations / Custom)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Data Quality Engineer** tasked with adding a **Data Quality Validation Step** to the current notebook for the processing layer. Reference the active dataframe/table in my code to implement a programmatic check before writing data to the next layer.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Implementation Style**: {e.g., Great Expectations / PySpark / Pandas}\\r\\n• **Completeness Target**: {e.g., ColumnName}\\r\\n• **Uniqueness Target**: {e.g., KeyColumn}\\r\\n• **Validity Target & Range**: {e.g., NumericColumn, Min-Max}\\r\\n• **Format Target & Regex**: {e.g., StringColumn, Pattern}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease scaffold a function or code block that enforces the following rules:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Data Quality Rules**:\\r\\n  1. **Completeness**: Identify rows where the {Completeness Target} is null.\\r\\n  2. **Uniqueness**: Identify duplicates based on the {Uniqueness Target}.\\r\\n  3. **Validity**: Ensure the {Validity Target} is within the specified {Min-Max} range.\\r\\n  4. **Format**: Ensure the {Format Target} matches the required Regex {Pattern}.\\r\\n• **Execution Logic**:\\r\\n  1. Separate the data into `valid_df` (passed checks) and `error_df` (failed checks).\\r\\n  2. Add a `failure_reason` column to `error_df` indicating which rule was broken.\\r\\n• **Action on Failure**:\\r\\n  1. If `error_df` is not empty, write it to a separate location/table named `_error_records` and proceed with `valid_df`.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Insert the code block directly after the transformation logic but before the final write.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a validation code block inserted before the final write step that applies rules for completeness, uniqueness, validity, and format, splits the data into a valid_df and an error_df with an appended failure_reason column, and routes the invalid records to a separate error table while allowing the valid data to continue down the pipeline.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement error handling and rerun framework (idempotent design)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a Senior Data Engineer and Site Reliability Engineer (SRE) specializing in building resilient, \\\"self-healing\\\" data pipelines. You have deep expertise in operational observability, idempotency patterns, and automated recovery strategies for enterprise data platforms.\\r\\n\\r\\n[Context & Vision]\\r\\nThe goal is to design a Python/PySpark framework that moves beyond simple transformations by embedding robust error handling, a clear rerun strategy, and guaranteed idempotency. We need to ensure that if a job fails mid-stream, it can be restarted for the same batch without manual data cleanup or risk of duplication, providing a production-ready \\\"Runbook\\\" for on-call engineers.\\r\\n\\r\\nProject Configuration (Variables)\\r\\nJob Name: <Insert Job Name, e.g., Silver_to_Gold_Sales>\\r\\nTarget Table/Path: <Insert Target Path/Table>\\r\\nBatch/Partition Column: <Insert column used for reruns, e.g., processing_date or batch_id>\\r\\nOrchestrator: [e.g., Azure Data Factory, Airflow, or Databricks Workflows]\\r\\nRequirements & Constraints\\r\\nPillar 1: Error Handling & Logging\\r\\nTry-Except Framework: Wrap the core transformation logic in a robust error-handling block.\\r\\nObservability: Log detailed error metadata (Error Message, Stack Trace, Timestamp, JobID) to a centralized metadata table or external monitoring tool (e.g., Azure Monitor, CloudWatch).\\r\\nExit Codes: Use dbutils.notebook.exit() or sys.exit(1) to ensure the orchestrator correctly registers a \\\"Failed\\\" status.\\r\\nPillar 2: Idempotency & Rerun Strategy\\r\\nPre-load Cleanup: Implement a \\\"Cleanup\\\" function that identifies and deletes existing data for the current <Batch/Partition Column> before the new load begins.\\r\\nAtomic Writes: Ensure the load process is atomic to prevent partial data states in the event of a crash.\\r\\nPillar 3: Modular Architecture\\r\\nCode Structure: Organize the script into modular functions (e.g., extract(), transform(), load(), cleanup()).\\r\\nParameterization: Ensure the <Batch/Partition Column> value is passed as a runtime parameter for flexible backfills.\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput Format \\r\\nPlease provide the response in Markdown including:\\r\\nModular PySpark Template: (Complete script with separated functions and error handling).\\r\\nIdempotent Cleanup Logic: (The specific code used to prune existing partitions/batches before a rerun).\\r\\nMetadata Logging Schema: (Recommended structure for the Error_Logs table).\\r\\nOperational Runbook: A concise section explaining:\\r\\nManual Triggering: How to pass parameters for a specific backfill date.\\r\\nFailure Analysis: Common failure points (Schema mismatch, Source missing).\\r\\nEscalation: Alerting steps and contact protocols.\\r\\nSRE Best Practices: A list of guardrails for maintaining pipeline SLOs (Service Level Objectives).\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent generates a production-ready Python/PySpark pipeline framework with robust error handling, idempotent rerun logic, and modular design (separate extract, transform, load functions).\\r\\n\\r\\nThe framework includes a cleanup step for the specified batch/partition column to prevent duplicate data during reruns. The main logic is wrapped in try–except blocks, logs failures to monitoring systems, and exits using sys.exit(1) or dbutils.notebook.exit() so orchestrators detect failures.\\r\\n\\r\\nIt also includes a Markdown runbook describing manual rerun steps, common failure points, and alerting procedures for operational support.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Optimize notebooks (partitioning, caching, checkpointing, broadcast joins)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Senior Data Engineer / Spark Optimization Expert** tasked with optimizing the currently open PySpark notebook for **performance and memory efficiency**.\\r\\n\\r\\nReview the code in my active editor. I am experiencing performance issues with this job (e.g., slow shuffles or spills). Please analyze the code and propose specific changes for:\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Partitioning**:\\r\\n  1. Look at my read and write steps.\\r\\n  2. Suggest if I should add `repartition()` or `coalesce()` based on the transformations used, or if I should change the `partitionBy` column in the final write.\\r\\n• **Caching**:\\r\\n  1. Identify any DataFrames that are reused multiple times in the logic.\\r\\n  2. Suggest where to insert `.cache()` or `.persist()` and where to `.unpersist()` them.\\r\\n• **Join Strategy**:\\r\\n  1. Look at my joins. If any DataFrame appears small (like a lookup table), refactor the code to use a `broadcast()` join hint.\\r\\n• **Configuration**:\\r\\n  1. Based on the volume of data processed in this script, suggest 3 key Spark configurations (like `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions`) I should set at the top of the notebook.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Provide the optimization suggestions in a well-formatted Markdown response.\\r\\n• Include refactored code snippets where applicable, clearly explaining the \\\"before\\\" and \\\"after\\\" impact.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a well-formatted Markdown response containing \\\"before and after\\\" PySpark code snippets that optimize partitioning with repartition or coalesce, apply caching to reused DataFrames, use broadcast hints for small joins, and recommend three key Spark configurations to improve performance and reduce shuffle spills.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement Databricks job orchestration (jobs, multi-task workflows)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a Principal Cloud Data Architect. Generate a complete, production-ready Databricks Multi-Task Workflow configuration (Jobs API 2.1+ JSON format), along with an Operational Runbook for error handling and the rerun framework.\\r\\n\\r\\nSTRICT EXECUTION RULES:\\r\\n- NO SNIPPETS. Provide the COMPLETE, copy-pasteable JSON configuration.\\r\\n- No conversational filler. Output configurations and runbook in structured markdown blocks.\\r\\n- The JSON payload MUST use the modern `tasks` array format with `depends_on` for task dependencies.\\r\\n- Incorporate explicit retry policies, timeouts, and alerting configurations.\\r\\n\\r\\n**USER CONTEXT & SCOPE - READ THIS FIRST**\\r\\n- Job Name: <ENTER_JOB_NAME e.g., Daily_Sales_Ingestion_Pipeline>\\r\\n- Compute Strategy: <ENTER_COMPUTE e.g., Use a shared Job Cluster (Standard_DS3_v2, autoscale 2-8 nodes) for all tasks to save costs>\\r\\n- Workflow Tasks & Dependencies: <ENTER_TASKS e.g., 1. raw_to_bronze, 2. bronze_to_silver (depends on 1), 3. silver_to_gold (depends on 2), 4. data_quality_check (depends on 3)>\\r\\n- Alerting Destination: <ENTER_ALERTS e.g., Email alerts to data-ops@mycompany.com on Failure and Timeout>\\r\\n- External Orchestrator: <ENTER_ORCHESTRATOR e.g., Triggered by ADF via Linked Service>\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Databricks Multi-Task Workflow Configuration (JSON)\\r\\nGenerate the complete JSON payload required to create this job via the Databricks REST API or Terraform.\\r\\n- Define the `job_clusters` array based on the `Compute Strategy` to share a single automated cluster across all tasks.\\r\\n- Define the `tasks` array based on the `Workflow Tasks & Dependencies`.\\r\\n- For each task, include:\\r\\n  - `notebook_task` with a parameterized path.\\r\\n  - `depends_on` to establish the DAG.\\r\\n  - `max_retries`: Set to 1 for transient failures, with a `min_retry_interval_millis`.\\r\\n  - `timeout_seconds`: To prevent runaway compute costs.\\r\\n- Define `email_notifications` or `webhook_notifications` at the Job level for failures.\\r\\n 2. The External Orchestrator Integration (ADF / Fabric / Synapse)\\r\\nProvide the exact dynamic configuration needed for the external orchestrator to trigger this specific Databricks workflow.\\r\\n- If the `External Orchestrator` is ADF or Synapse, provide the JSON configuration for the \\\"Web Activity\\\" (using Jobs API `/api/2.1/jobs/run-now`) OR the native \\\"Databricks Job Activity\\\".\\r\\n- Explain how the orchestrator should capture the `run_id` and poll for the workflow status.\\r\\n\\r\\n 3. Operational Runbook: Error Handling & Rerun Framework\\r\\nGenerate a Markdown-formatted standard operating procedure (SOP) for the L1/L2 support team.\\r\\n- **Failure Identification:** How to read the Databricks job UI and identify which specific task failed in the DAG.\\r\\n- **The Rerun Strategy (Repair Run):** Step-by-step instructions (and the API `/api/2.1/jobs/runs/repair` JSON payload) on how to restart ONLY the failed task and its downstream dependents without reprocessing the successful upstream tasks.\\r\\n- **State Cleanup:** Instructions on how to manually clean up a Delta table or remove a bad file if a task failed midway through an upsert.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Deliverables Required**\\r\\n1. Complete Databricks Job JSON payload.\\r\\n2. ADF/Fabric trigger activity configuration (JSON or expressions).\\r\\n3. Markdown Operational Runbook covering failures and the Repair/Rerun framework.\",\n      \"output\": \"Databricks Multi-Task Workflow & Orchestration Suite\\r\\nThis agent provides a production-hardened configuration for automating enterprise data pipelines using the Databricks Jobs API 2.1. It delivers a modular, cost-efficient JSON payload that utilizes a shared Job Cluster to reduce startup latency and compute overhead while enforcing strict DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) dependencies. Furthermore, the agent provides the necessary integration logic for external orchestrators and a comprehensive Operational Runbook, enabling L1/L2 support teams to perform \\\"Repair Runs\\\" that resume failed workflows from the point of failure without redundant compute costs.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Configure job notifications (email, Teams, webhook)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Data Engineering Lead / DevOps Specialist** tasked with the design and implementation of a **Processing Layer Notification System** to alert the engineering team about transformation failures, data quality breaches, or performance anomalies.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Processing Engine**: {e.g., Databricks (PySpark) / Snowflake Tasks / dbt Cloud / Azure Synapse}\\r\\n• **Orchestrator**: {e.g., Azure Data Factory / Airflow / Databricks Workflows}\\r\\n• **Notification Channel**: {e.g., MS Teams Webhook / Slack / PagerDuty / Email}\\r\\n• **Alerting Scenarios**:\\r\\n  1. **Critical Failure**: Job failed completely (Code Error).\\r\\n  2. **Data Quality Breach**: Job finished, but dropped > 10% of rows due to bad data.\\r\\n  3. **Performance**: Job ran 2x longer than the 7-day moving average.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease provide the code snippets and configuration steps to implement these specific notifications:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Webhook Integration (Teams/Slack)**:\\r\\n  1. **Payload Design**: Create a JSON payload that includes Pipeline/Notebook Name, Run URL (Link directly to the Spark UI or Snowflake Query History for debugging), Error Message/Stack Trace (Truncated to first 200 chars), and Rows Processed vs. Rows Dropped.\\r\\n  2. **Implementation**: Show how to send this payload using Python (`requests.post`) or the specific Orchestrator's \\\"Web Activity\\\".\\r\\n• **Conditional Alerting Logic**:\\r\\n  1. Write the logic to only trigger an alert if specific conditions are met (e.g., if failure_count > 0 OR if duration_minutes > 120).\\r\\n  2. Explain how to suppress \\\"noise\\\" (e.g., don't alert on retries, only on final failure).\\r\\n• **Email Configuration (for Business Stakeholders)**:\\r\\n  1. If using Email, draft a cleaner, non-technical template for \\\"SLA Missed\\\" or \\\"Daily Processing Summary\\\" (e.g., \\\"The Daily Sales Report is delayed\\\").\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Provide the exact Python/JSON Code Block to format and send the message.\\r\\n• Include a Variable List of environment variables (like `WEBHOOK_URL`) that need to be set in the cluster/environment.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide Python/JSON code block to format and send webhook payloads with pipeline details and run URLs, conditional logic to trigger alerts on failures or duration thresholds while suppressing retry noise, a non-technical email template for business stakeholders, and a clear list of required environment variables like WEBHOOK_URL needed to configure the notification system.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Monitor cluster utilization metrics\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a cloud data platform reliability engineer.\\r\\n\\r\\nImplement a comprehensive monitoring and alerting mechanism for cluster utilization metrics in a distributed data processing environment\\r\\n\\r\\nThe solution must cover:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Key metrics to monitor\\r\\n• CPU utilization\\r\\n• Memory usage\\r\\n• Disk I/O\\r\\n• Network I/O\\r\\n• Executor utilization\\r\\n• Job queue length\\r\\n• Autoscaling behavior\\r\\n• Shuffle read/write metrics\\r\\n• Spill metrics\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Threshold definitions\\r\\n• Warning vs Critical levels\\r\\n• Sustained vs spike-based thresholds\\r\\n• Environment-specific baselines (dev/test/prod)\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Alerting strategy\\r\\n• When alerts should trigger\\r\\n• Escalation policy\\r\\n• Integration with monitoring tools (e.g., Azure Monitor, Datadog, Prometheus)\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Visualization\\r\\n• Recommended dashboard layout\\r\\n• Capacity trend tracking\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Optimization guidance\\r\\n• How to detect underutilized clusters\\r\\n• How to detect resource bottlenecks\\r\\n• Scaling recommendations\\r\\n• Cost optimization insights\\r\\n\\r\\n6. Output format:\\r\\n• Structured document\\r\\n• Use clear sections\\r\\n• Include example threshold values\\r\\n• Provide actionable recommendations\\r\\n• Keep guidance implementation-ready\",\n      \"output\": \"CPU Utilization\\r\\nMetric: cluster.cpu.utilization_percent\\r\\n\\r\\nMemory Usage\\r\\nMetrics:\\r\\ncluster.memory.used_bytes / cluster.memory.total_bytes\\r\\ncluster.memory.heap_used_percent (JVM heap for Spark)\\r\\ncluster.memory.off_heap_used_percent (direct memory)\\r\\n\\r\\nDisk I/O\\r\\nMetrics:\\r\\ncluster.disk.read_throughput_mbps\\r\\ncluster.disk.write_throughput_mbps\\r\\ncluster.disk.iops_read, cluster.disk.iops_write\\r\\ncluster.disk.usage_percent (ephemeral storage)\\r\\ncluster.disk.io_wait_time_ms (queue depth)\\r\\n\\r\\nNetwork I/O\\r\\nMetrics:\\r\\ncluster.network.rx_throughput_mbps (receive)\\r\\ncluster.network.tx_throughput_mbps (transmit)\\r\\ncluster.network.packet_loss_percent\\r\\ncluster.network.retransmit_rate\\r\\n\\r\\nExecutor Utilization\\r\\nMetrics:\\r\\nspark.executor.active_tasks / spark.executor.max_tasks\\r\\nspark.executor.idle_time_percent\\r\\nspark.executor.gc_time_percent (GC overhead)\\r\\nspark.executor.jvm_memory_used_bytes\\r\\n\\r\\nAutoscaling Behavior\\r\\nMetrics:\\r\\ncluster.autoscale.current_nodes\\r\\ncluster.autoscale.target_nodes\\r\\ncluster.autoscale.scale_up_events_count\\r\\ncluster.autoscale.scale_down_events_count\\r\\ncluster.autoscale.time_to_scale_seconds\\r\\n\\r\\nShuffle Read/Write\\r\\nMetrics:\\r\\nspark.shuffle.read_bytes_total\\r\\nspark.shuffle.write_bytes_total\\r\\nspark.shuffle.read_time_ms (remote read latency)\\r\\nspark.shuffle.blocks_fetched_remote\\r\\nspark.shuffle.fetch_wait_time_ms\\r\\n\\r\\nMemory & Disk Spill\\r\\nMetrics:\\r\\nspark.executor.memory_spill_bytes (spilled to disk)\\r\\nspark.executor.disk_spill_bytes (read back from disk)\\r\\nspark.shuffle.spill_memory_bytes\\r\\nspark.shuffle.spill_disk_bytes\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Create required gold tables\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Principal Analytics Engineer responsible for designing, building, and optimizing production-ready data models in the Gold/Published layer for enterprise BI and analytics consumption.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nGenerate production-ready SQL scripts to create, optimize, and validate Gold/Published layer tables or views based on the provided business requirements and platform dialect.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need to construct the final curated presentation layer (Gold) that business users and BI tools will query. The logic must be modular, performant, and perfectly aligned with the target platform's SQL dialect.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Target Platform:** {Add Platform, e.g., Databricks (Spark SQL), Azure Synapse (T-SQL)}  \\r\\n**Object Type:** {Add Object Type, e.g., Materialized Table, Logical View}  \\r\\n**Object Name:** {Add Object Name, e.g., gold.vw_sales_performance_daily}  \\r\\n**Final Reporting View Description:** {Add Description, e.g., A daily aggregated view of sales performance by region and product category for the Executive Power BI dashboard.}  \\r\\n**Requirement Details (Columns & Metrics):** {Add Details, e.g., Needs Date, RegionName, CategoryName, TotalRevenue (sum of amount), TotalOrders (count distinct order_id), AverageOrderValue.}  \\r\\n**Base Tables & Joining Conditions:** {Add Joins, e.g., Left Join silver.fact_sales (fs) to silver.dim_date (dd) on DateKey. Left Join silver.dim_location (dl) on StoreID. Left Join silver.dim_product (dp) on ProductID.}  \\r\\n**Filter Conditions:** {Add Filters, e.g., Only include fs.is_active = true, exclude test regions (dl.region_name != 'Test'), and only include data from the last 3 years.}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nProvide the COMPLETE, copy-pasteable SQL code. Do not use conversational filler. Output code in structured markdown blocks. The SQL dialect MUST exactly match the target platform provided in the context (e.g., Spark SQL for Databricks/Fabric Lakehouse, or T-SQL for Synapse Dedicated SQL Pools). Use clear table aliases, explicit join types, and modular CTEs (Common Table Expressions) for readability.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **The Gold Layer DDL (Data Definition Language)**  \\r\\n1. Generate the complete CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW or CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) script based on the Object Type.  \\r\\n2. Step 1: Create a base CTE to apply the Filter Conditions early (predicate pushdown).  \\r\\n3. Step 2: Create a joining CTE applying the exact Base Tables & Joining Conditions. If doing LEFT JOINs, ensure the logic prevents data fan-out.  \\r\\n4. Step 3: Create an aggregation CTE applying the Requirement Details (SUM, COUNT DISTINCT, etc.).  \\r\\n5. Step 4: Final SELECT statement with clean column aliases (e.g., renaming SUM(amount) to TotalRevenue).\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Performance & Optimization (If Table)**  \\r\\n1. If the Object Type is a Table (not a View), append the appropriate physical optimization commands.  \\r\\n2. For Databricks/Fabric Delta: Provide the OPTIMIZE and ZORDER BY commands for the most frequently filtered columns.  \\r\\n3. For Synapse T-SQL: Define the Distribution style (e.g., HASH(RegionName)) and Index (e.g., CLUSTERED COLUMNSTORE INDEX).\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Gold Layer Validation Queries (Sanity Checks)**  \\r\\n1. Provide 2–3 short diagnostic SQL queries that the developer can run immediately after creating the object to prove the logic is sound.  \\r\\n2. Validation 1: Check for duplicate rows at the expected reporting grain (e.g., Date + Region + Category).  \\r\\n3. Validation 2: Ensure the TotalRevenue measure does not contain NULLs (or handles them via COALESCE).  \\r\\n4. Validation 3: Verify the exact Filter Conditions worked (e.g., SELECT MIN(Date), MAX(Date) FROM view to prove the 3-year filter).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**:  \\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown\\r\\n\\r\\n• The complete SQL DDL/DML script utilizing modular CTEs code block  \\r\\n• The optimization commands (if creating a physical table) code block  \\r\\n• The SQL Validation diagnostic queries code block\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent provides a production-ready SQL framework designed to deliver high-performance, curated data assets in the Gold/Published layer. It generates a modular DDL/DML script utilizing Common Table Expressions (CTEs) to enforce predicate pushdown and clean join logic, ensuring the final reporting view is optimized for enterprise BI tools like Power BI. Additionally, the agent provides platform-specific optimization commands (such as ZORDER or Distribution Styles) and a suite of validation queries to guarantee data integrity, grain consistency, and adherence to business filter conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Publish curated tables to ADLS/OneLake for analytics consumption\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Data Engineer** tasked with publishing the curated dataframe to the **Data Publishing (Gold) layer** for analytics consumption. Reference the active dataframe in my code that holds the final transformed data.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Storage Destination**: {e.g., ADLS Gen2 / OneLake}\\r\\n• **Target Storage Path**: {Insert Target ABFSS Path}\\r\\n• **Write Mode**: {e.g., Overwrite / Append / Merge}\\r\\n• **Partition Columns**: {Insert Partition Columns, or state 'None'}\\r\\n• **Table Name**: {Insert Catalog.Schema.TableName}\\r\\n• **Primary Key Column**: {Insert Primary Key Column - needed if Write Mode is Merge}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease scaffold the code block that handles the following:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Implementation Style**: \\r\\n  1. Use PySpark and Delta Lake format.\\r\\n• **Logic**:\\r\\n  1. Write the dataframe to the target storage path using the specified write mode.\\r\\n  2. If partitioning is needed, partition the data by the specified columns.\\r\\n• **Action**:\\r\\n  1. Save the data to the specified storage path and register it as a managed/external table named in the Metastore/Catalog.\\r\\n  2. If the write mode is 'Merge', use the Primary Key Column as the merge condition.\\r\\n  3. Include standard Delta maintenance commands (OPTIMIZE and VACUUM) immediately after the write operation.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Insert the code block at the end of the script/notebook, directly after the final transformation logic.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a PySpark/Delta Lake code block for writing the curated dataframe to the Gold layer with the specified write mode (Overwrite/Append/Merge), partitioning, metastore table registration, and OPTIMIZE/VACUUM maintenance commands.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Publish Delta tables to Databricks Unity Catalog (catalog/schema/table)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Create a production-ready module for Databricks that publishes Delta tables to Databricks Unity Catalog.\\r\\nSystem Context:\\r\\n**Provide system context here**\\r\\ncatalog_name, schema_name, Table, source, publish mode.\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements:\\r\\nCreate script to publish the data in databricks unity catalog as per the above system context provided.\\r\\n\\r\\nValidate that:\\r\\nCatalog exists (create if not exists optional flag)\\r\\nSchema exists (create if not exists optional flag)\\r\\nHandle schema evolution if merge_schema=True\\r\\nReturn success/failure status\",\n      \"output\": \"It will generate a production-ready script that validates whether the specified catalog and schema exist in Unity Catalog and creates them if they do not exist, and publishes Delta tables to Unity Catalog according to the given mapping\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Apply column-level security\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Data Security Engineer** tasked with applying **Column-Level Security (CLS)** to the published table in the data publishing (Gold) layer. Reference the active SQL script or table definition in my code.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Implementation Style**: {e.g., Databricks Unity Catalog / Microsoft Fabric SQL / Synapse SQL}\\r\\n• **Target Table**: {Insert Catalog.Schema.TableName}\\r\\n• **Sensitive Columns**: {e.g., SSN, Salary}\\r\\n• **Authorized Role/Group**: {Insert Authorized Role/Group}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease provide the SQL commands that handle the following:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Security Logic**:\\r\\n  1. Restrict access to the Sensitive Columns.\\r\\n• **Action**:\\r\\n  1. Generate the SQL commands to either grant column-level SELECT permissions only to the Authorized Role/Group.\\r\\n  2. Alternatively, create a secure view that replaces the sensitive columns with NULL for unauthorized users using functions like `is_member()`.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Provide the SQL code block to enforce this security rule.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide SQL commands to restrict sensitive column access to authorized roles, or a secure view that masks columns with NULL for unauthorized users using is_member().\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Apply row-level security (RLS)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Data Security Engineer** tasked with applying **Row-Level Security (RLS)** to the published table in the data publishing (Gold) layer so users only see data relevant to their region/department. Reference the active SQL script or table definition in my code.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Implementation Style**: {e.g., Databricks Unity Catalog Row Filters / Microsoft Fabric RLS / Secure Views}\\r\\n• **Target Table**: {Insert Catalog.Schema.TableName}\\r\\n• **Filter Column**: {e.g., Region, TenantID}\\r\\n• **Admin Role**: {Insert Admin Role}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease provide the SQL commands that handle the following:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Security Logic**:\\r\\n  1. Filter the rows based on the Filter Column.\\r\\n• **Action**:\\r\\n  1. Create a row filter function or security predicate that checks the current user's identity or group membership (e.g., using `current_user()` or a mapping table). \\r\\n  2. Apply this filter to the target table so that users in the Admin Role see all rows, but standard users only see their assigned rows.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Provide the SQL code block containing the function/predicate definition and the command to apply it to the table.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a row filter function or security predicate that restricts data visibility by region/department, ensuring admins see all rows while standard users see only their assigned data based on current_user() or a mapping table.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement masking logic (Dynamic Masking / Custom Masking)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Data Security Engineer** tasked with implementing **Dynamic Data Masking** on sensitive columns in the data publishing (Gold) layer to obfuscate PII for unauthorized users. Reference the active SQL script or table definition in my code.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Implementation Style**: {e.g., Databricks Unity Catalog Data Masks / Microsoft Fabric Dynamic Data Masking}\\r\\n• **Target Table**: {Insert Catalog.Schema.TableName}\\r\\n• **Column Name**: {e.g., Email, PhoneNumber}\\r\\n• **Masking Logic**: {e.g., '***@***.com', partial redaction, or default mask}\\r\\n• **Authorized Role/Group**: {Insert Authorized Role/Group}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease provide the SQL commands that handle the following:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Security Logic**:\\r\\n  1. Mask the data in the specified Column Name.\\r\\n• **Action**:\\r\\n  1. Create a masking function/rule. The mask should format the data according to the Masking Logic.\\r\\n  2. Apply the mask to the Target Table.\\r\\n  3. Ensure that members of the Authorized Role/Group bypass the mask and see the plaintext data.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Provide the SQL code block containing the masking function definition and the ALTER TABLE command to apply the mask.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a masking function definition and ALTER TABLE command to obfuscate PII columns for unauthorized users, while allowing authorized roles to bypass the mask and see plaintext data.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement purge process for outdated or expired data\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are an experienced data engineer who wants to clean up the workspaces by implementing a data purge process for outdated or expired records.\\r\\nCreate a script for the below: \\r\\nRequirements:\\r\\n1. Identify records eligible for purge based on:\\r\\n• Retention policy (e.g., older than X days/months/years)\\r\\n• Expiration date column\\r\\n• Status flag (e.g., IsActive = false)\\r\\n2. Parameterize retention period (config-driven, not hardcoded)\\r\\n3. Support soft delete (flag update) OR hard delete (physical removal)\\r\\n4. Log purge activity including:\\r\\n• Table name\\r\\n• Number of records deleted\\r\\n• Execution timestamp\\r\\n5. Handle large tables efficiently (batch deletes if needed)\\r\\n6. Include error handling and transaction control\\r\\n7. Make the process reusable for multiple tables\\r\\nFollow best practices for performance, auditability, and recoverability.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a scalable, auditable, and configurable script for purging.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement table versioning and change management process\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Senior DataOps Engineer specializing in Delta Lake internals, Azure DevOps automation, and database version control. You have deep expertise in maintaining the stability, performance, and auditability of large-scale lakehouses.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context & Vision**  \\r\\nThe goal is to implement a robust framework for Delta Lake table versioning, compatibility checks, and change management. We need to ensure that every structural or data change is tracked, validated for schema compatibility, and backed by an automated rollback strategy. This framework bridges the gap between Git-based code deployments (Azure DevOps) and the transactional state of Delta tables (Time Travel).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Project Configuration (Variables)**  \\r\\n• **Storage Layer**: <e.g., Delta Lake on ADLS Gen2 / Fabric>  \\r\\n• **Versioning Method**: <e.g., Delta History / Time Travel / VERSION AS OF>  \\r\\n• **CI/CD Platform**: Azure DevOps Pipelines  \\r\\n• **Metadata Store**: table_audit_trail  \\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements & Constraints**\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 1: Change Tracking & Auditing**  \\r\\n1. **History Capture**: Create a function to query DESCRIBE HISTORY and log critical metadata (Version, Timestamp, Operation, User, NotebookID) into a centralized **table_audit_trail**.  \\r\\n2. **Git Integration**: Ensure the solution allows logging of Azure DevOps Release Tags or Commit SHAs into the Delta table properties for end-to-end traceability.\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 2: Schema Enforcement & Compatibility**  \\r\\n1. **Pre-Deployment Check**: Implement a \\\"Compatibility Gate\\\" that compares the incoming code/schema version against the current table state before an UPDATE or ALTER is executed.  \\r\\n2. **Logic**: Define the behavior for handling breaking changes vs. additive changes (e.g., fail the pipeline if a column is dropped).\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 3: Automated Rollback & Recovery**  \\r\\n1. **Recovery Script**: Design a PySpark/SQL script that utilizes Delta Time Travel (VERSION AS OF or TIMESTAMP AS OF) to restore a table to its last known healthy state if a post-load Data Quality (DQ) check fails.\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 4: Maintenance & Performance Monitoring**  \\r\\n1. **Health Metrics**: Provide logic to identify \\\"Sudden File Growth\\\" or \\\"Small File Fragmentation\\\" (high file count vs. low data volume).  \\r\\n2. **Self-Healing**: Trigger VACUUM or OPTIMIZE operations automatically based on these metrics to maintain storage efficiency.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Audit Trail Script**:  \\r\\n(SQL/PySpark logic for capturing and storing DESCRIBE HISTORY)\\r\\n\\r\\n2. **Schema Compatibility Function**:  \\r\\n(Code to validate incoming schema against the target)\\r\\n\\r\\n3. **Automated Rollback Logic**:  \\r\\n(A \\\"Restore-on-Failure\\\" script for Azure DevOps integration)\\r\\n\\r\\n4. **Performance Maintenance Query**:  \\r\\n(A monitoring script for file fragmentation and vacuuming)\\r\\n\\r\\n5. **Azure DevOps Pipeline Snippet**:  \\r\\n(YAML logic showing where these checks fit into the CI/CD flow)\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent provides a DataOps framework for Delta Lake change management that includes table versioning, schema compatibility checks, and automated rollback capabilities. The solution captures Delta table history (version, timestamp, operation, user) using DESCRIBE HISTORY and logs it into a metadata audit table for tracking changes.\\r\\n\\r\\nBefore updates, the framework performs schema enforcement checks to ensure the incoming deployment is compatible with the current table schema. If a data quality check fails, the system can automatically restore the table using Delta Time Travel (VERSION AS OF).\\r\\n\\r\\nIt also includes monitoring logic to detect sudden file growth or small file issues, triggering OPTIMIZE or VACUUM operations. The framework integrates with Azure DevOps CI/CD pipelines, logging Git deployment version tags to maintain full traceability of table changes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Configure monitoring and alerting for published layer refresh/load failures\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Cloud Solutions Architect specializing in Azure Monitor, Log Analytics, and Microsoft Fabric. You have deep expertise in building distributed observability frameworks that provide real-time visibility into data mesh and medallion architectures.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context & Vision**  \\r\\nThe goal is to configure an Observability framework for a published data layer to detect and alert on refresh or load failures. We need to move beyond basic error logs by creating a unified telemetry stream that connects Fabric Spark execution with Azure Log Analytics, enabling Fabric Activator to trigger automated responses and \\\"Smart Alerts\\\" based on failure patterns.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Project Configuration (Variables)**  \\r\\n• **Orchestration Platform**: Microsoft Fabric (Notebooks/Pipelines)  \\r\\n• **Telemetry Store**: Azure Log Analytics Workspace  \\r\\n• **Monitoring Table**: ops_audit_log  \\r\\n• **Alerting Engine**: Fabric Activator  \\r\\n• **Visualization**: Kusto Query Language (KQL) / Power BI  \\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements & Constraints**\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 1: Telemetry Capture & Ingestion**  \\r\\n1. **Custom Logging**: Write a PySpark script to push granular execution metadata (Job ID, Table Name, Rows Processed, Execution Time, Error Code) directly to Azure Log Analytics.  \\r\\n2. **Integration**: Ensure the script utilizes the Log Analytics Data Collector API or the Fabric-native monitoring integration.\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 2: Automated Response (Fabric Activator)**  \\r\\n1. **Trigger Logic**: Define the configuration for a Fabric Activator item that monitors the ops_audit_log Delta table.  \\r\\n2. **Condition**: Trigger an action for any new record where status == 'FAILED'.\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 3: Smart Alerting & Classification**  \\r\\n1. **Pattern Recognition**: Design logic to distinguish between:  \\r\\n   • **Transient Errors**: (e.g., Network timeouts) that trigger an automated retry.  \\r\\n   • **Hard Failures**: (e.g., Schema mismatch, Auth failure) that require immediate manual intervention.  \\r\\n2. **Notification**: Route alerts to the appropriate channel (Teams/Email/ServiceNow) based on the error classification.\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 4: Observability & Trend Analysis**  \\r\\n1. **KQL Dashboarding**: Generate a Kusto Query Language (KQL) query to visualize pipeline health trends over a 30-day rolling window.  \\r\\n2. **Metrics**: Include Success Rate %, Average Execution Time, and Top 5 Failing Tables.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Telemetry PySpark Utility**:  \\r\\n(A reusable function for logging execution metadata from Fabric Spark jobs to Azure Log Analytics)\\r\\n\\r\\n2. **Fabric Activator Configuration**:  \\r\\n(Step-by-step configuration logic defining the trigger condition and automated actions)\\r\\n\\r\\n3. **Error Classification Logic**:  \\r\\n(A mapping or code snippet implementing smart alert routing for transient vs hard failures)\\r\\n\\r\\n4. **Health Trend KQL Query**:  \\r\\n(The complete Kusto query used for a 30-day pipeline health dashboard)\\r\\n\\r\\n5. **Alerting Workflow Diagram**:  \\r\\n(A text-based / ASCII diagram illustrating the end-to-end failure detection and notification flow)\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent provides an observability framework for monitoring published data layers in Microsoft Fabric using Azure Monitor and Log Analytics. The solution includes a PySpark logging script that pushes operational telemetry (Job ID, Table Name, Rows Processed, Execution Time, Status) to Azure Log Analytics for centralized monitoring.\\r\\n\\r\\nA Fabric Activator trigger monitors the ops_audit_log table and automatically detects records where status = 'FAILED', enabling real-time alerting. The framework also implements smart alerting logic to differentiate between transient errors (retryable) and hard failures (manual intervention required).\\r\\n\\r\\nAdditionally, it provides a KQL query for dashboards to visualize pipeline health, failure trends, and execution metrics over the last 30 days, supporting proactive monitoring and operational insights.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Create the semantic model dataset in Power BI / Fabric\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as a **Senior Data Engineer / Power BI Developer** tasked with creating a **Semantic Model dataset** in Power BI from scratch. \\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Data Source**: ADLS Gen2 Container\\r\\n• **ADLS Endpoint/URL**: {Insert your ADLS workspace URL or connection details}\\r\\n• **Authentication Method**: {e.g., Organizational Account / Entra ID / Account Key}\\r\\n• **Tables to Import**: {\\r\\n  1. `FactBR`\\r\\n  2. `DimProduct` (Key: ProductID)\\r\\n  3. `DimDistributor` (Key: DistributorID) }\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease provide step-by-step instructions on how to build this model from scratch, including:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Data Ingestion**:\\r\\n  1. How to connect Power BI Desktop to the specific ADLS container.\\r\\n  2. How to navigate the file system to locate and import the required tables (Parquet/CSV).\\r\\n• **Power Query / Transformation**:\\r\\n  1. Any required steps to promote headers or change data types before loading the data into the model.\\r\\n• **Data Modeling & Relationships**:\\r\\n  1. How to establish the relationships between `FactBR` and the dimension tables (`DimProduct` and `DimDistributor`).\\r\\n  2. Specify the expected Cardinality (e.g., 1 to Many) and Cross-filter direction for each relationship.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Provide a clear, numbered step-by-step guide starting from opening a blank Power BI file to finalizing the relationship view.\\r\\n• Include any best practices for naming conventions or hiding foreign keys in the report view.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Import data or configure Direct Lake / Direct Query connection\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Connect to Power BI Desktop with name **Provide name here**.\\r\\nImport the given **source** as a table with name **table name**.\",\n      \"output\": \"The prompt will connect to Power BI and generate the code for importing the table.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Create calculated columns\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Connect to Power BI Desktop with name **Provide name here**.\\r\\nCreate a Calculated column named **Calculated Column name** with logic **Provide Logic here**\",\n      \"output\": \"Prompt using Power BI MCP:\\r\\nConnect to Power BI Desktop with name 'Billed Reporting'.\\r\\nCreate a Calculated column named CalculatedFM with logic FiscalMonth * 10\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput:\\r\\nCreates the calculated column as follows \\r\\nCalculatedFM = [FiscalMonth] * 10\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Create DAX measures\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Connect to Power BI Desktop with name **Provide name here**.\\r\\nCreate a measure named **Measure name** with logic **Provide Logic here**\",\n      \"output\": \"Prompt Using Power BI MCP:\\r\\nConnect to Power BI Desktop with name 'Billed Reporting'.\\r\\nCreate a measure Revenue PY which calculates the revenue generated in the previous year\\r\\nOutput:\\r\\nCreated a measure with name Revenue PY with logic implemented to calculate the previous year's revenue\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Create hierarchies (Date, Product, Customer, etc.)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Connect to Power BI Desktop with name **Provide name here**.\\r\\nCreate a Hierarchy in **Provide Table name** table with columns **Provide list of columns**\",\n      \"output\": \"Prompt using Power BI MCP:\\r\\nConnect to Power BI Desktop with name 'Billed Reporting'.\\r\\nCreate a Hierarchy in Test_excel table with columns  FiscalMonth and CalculatedFM\\r\\nOutput:\\r\\nCreated the hierarchy FiscalMonth Hierarchy with both the columns\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement Row-Level Security (RLS) rules\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Connect to Power BI Desktop with name **Provide name here**.\\r\\nCreate a role for RLS as **Provide RLS logic**\",\n      \"output\": \"Prompt using Power BI MCP:\\r\\nConnect to Power BI Desktop with name 'Billed Reporting'.\\r\\nCreate a role PartnerOneRole in Test_excel with PartnerOne = USERPRINCIPALNAME()\\r\\nOutput:\\r\\nIt creates the role in Power BI with the provided DAX.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement Perspectives (consumer-friendly views)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Principal Power BI & Fabric Semantic Model Architect responsible for designing enterprise-scale, consumer-friendly semantic models and automating metadata deployments.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nGenerate automated scripts (Tabular Editor C# and TMSL JSON) to create and assign Perspectives (consumer-friendly views) within an enterprise semantic model.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need to programmatically define and deploy curated views of our enterprise semantic model to ensure business users only see the tables, measures, and columns relevant to their specific domain, while hiding technical columns like foreign keys.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Target Semantic Model**: {Add Model Name, e.g., Enterprise_Sales_Model}  \\r\\n**Perspective Name 1**: {Add Perspective 1 Name, e.g., Executive_Summary}  \\r\\n**Objects for Perspective 1**: {Add Objects 1, e.g., Tables: ['Sales', 'Date', 'Region']. Measures: ['Total Revenue', 'Gross Margin']}  \\r\\n**Perspective Name 2**: {Add Perspective 2 Name, e.g., HR_Analytics}  \\r\\n**Objects for Perspective 2**: {Add Objects 2, e.g., Tables: ['Employees', 'Date']. Measures: ['Headcount', 'Attrition Rate']}  \\r\\n**Hide Foreign Keys?**: {Add YES or NO, e.g., YES - automatically exclude columns ending in 'ID' or 'Key' from all perspectives}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nProvide the COMPLETE, copy-pasteable scripts. Do not use conversational filler. Output scripts in structured markdown blocks. Provide two distinct solutions: A Tabular Editor C# Advanced Script, and a TMSL (Tabular Model Scripting Language) JSON payload. The scripts MUST gracefully handle cases where the perspective already exists (idempotent execution).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Tabular Editor C# Advanced Script**:  \\r\\n1. Generate a complete C# script that a developer can paste into Tabular Editor's Advanced Scripting window.  \\r\\n2. Initialization: Create the perspectives defined in the User Context if they do not already exist.  \\r\\n3. Bulk Assignment: Loop through the entire model. If a Table, Column, or Measure matches the Objects for Perspective arrays, assign ShowInPerspective[\\\"PerspectiveName\\\"] = true.  \\r\\n4. Consumer-Friendly Cleanup: If Hide Foreign Keys? is YES, dynamically loop through all columns in the assigned tables. If the column name ends with ID or Key, ensure it is NOT added to the perspective, ensuring business users only see clean dimensions and facts.\\r\\n\\r\\n• **TMSL JSON Payload (For XMLA Endpoint Deployment)**:  \\r\\n1. Generate the TMSL JSON script to create the perspectives and assign a sample table/measure to them.  \\r\\n2. Provide the createOrReplace or alter command structure targeted at the Target Semantic Model.  \\r\\n3. Include the perspectives array at the model level.  \\r\\n4. Show the exact JSON schema required to map a table and a measure to the newly created perspective.\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Operational Runbook (Validation and Deployment)**:  \\r\\n1. Provide a brief, 3-step Markdown runbook for the CI/CD or BI team.  \\r\\n2. Step 1: Execute the C# script locally against a .pbip or Model.bim file.  \\r\\n3. Step 2: Deploy the TMSL payload via SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS) or a Fabric deployment pipeline using the XMLA endpoint.  \\r\\n4. Step 3: Validate the perspectives using the Personalize visuals or Analyze in Excel features to ensure the consumer views are correctly restricted.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**:  \\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Complete Tabular Editor C# Advanced Script code block**  \\r\\n2. **TMSL JSON Payload script code block**  \\r\\n3. **Operational Runbook details for Validation and Deployment**\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent will generate a production-ready automation suite designed to curate semantic model metadata into functional Perspectives. It will provide a modular C# script for bulk object assignment within Tabular Editor, a TMSL JSON payload for direct XMLA deployment, and a technical runbook to ensure idempotent execution and metadata integrity across your Power BI or Fabric environments.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Run Vertipaq Analyzer (compression, dictionary size)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"You are a Power BI specialist.\\r\\nYou are provided with the vertipaq analyzer output. Analyze the data and provide a detailed storage engine analysis which can impact the query and model performace. \\r\\n**Provide the vertipaq analyzer output here for context**\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide analysis in below direction:\\r\\n• Model Size Analysis\\r\\n• Cardinality Analysis\\r\\n• Compression & Encoding Analysis\\r\\n• Column-Level Storage Analysis\\r\\n• Model Design Evaluation\\r\\n\\r\\nTypes of Suggestions that you can expect:\\r\\n• Remove unused columns/tables\\r\\n• Replace text keys with integer surrogate keys\\r\\n• Reduce column cardinality\\r\\n• Optimize data types for better compression\\r\\n• Split DateTime into Date and Time\\r\\n• Convert calculated columns to measures\\r\\n• Flatten snowflake schema into star schema\\r\\n• Create aggregation tables\\r\\n• Implement incremental refresh\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Optimize model size (remove unused columns/tables)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Principal Power BI & Fabric Semantic Model Architect responsible for model optimization, VertiPaq memory efficiency, and metadata governance across enterprise-scale datasets.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nGenerate a robust diagnostic suite and Tabular Editor C# Advanced Script to safely identify, flag, and optimize unused columns and tables to reduce the VertiPaq memory footprint.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need a safe, metadata-driven approach to identify columns that have no dependencies within the semantic model (Measures, RLS, Relationships, etc.) while respecting vital system exclusions. Because Tabular Editor cannot see report-level visual usage, we will flag and segregate these columns for final human validation.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Target Model:** {Add Model Name, e.g., Enterprise_Retail_Dataset}  \\r\\n**Exclusion List:** {Add Exclusions, e.g., Columns ending in 'Key' or 'ID', or system columns like 'RowTimestamp'}  \\r\\n**Segregation Strategy:** {Move all model-unused columns into a Display Folder called \\\"_TO_BE_DELETED\\\" and set IsHidden = true}  \\r\\n**Output Format Required:** {Print a TSV/CSV list to the screen containing TableName, ColumnName, DataType, and DataCategory}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nProvide the COMPLETE, copy-pasteable Tabular Editor C# Advanced Script. Do not use conversational filler. Output code in structured markdown blocks. The C# script MUST utilize Tabular Editor's native dependency tracking to determine if a column is actively used. DO NOT generate a script that automatically deletes columns.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Tabular Editor C# Script: The Unused Column Scanner**  \\r\\n1. **Dependency Checking**: Loop through Model.AllColumns. Check if the column is referenced by Measures, Calculated Columns, Calculation Items, Relationships, Hierarchies, RLS, or SortBy columns.  \\r\\n2. **Exclusion Logic**: Apply the Exclusion List rules to skip vital columns even if they appear unused in metadata.  \\r\\n3. **Action and Segregation**: If a column has zero model dependencies and is not excluded, apply the Segregation Strategy (e.g., hide and move to a specific folder).  \\r\\n4. **Audit Logging**: Output the flagged columns using the Output Format Required (TSV/CSV).\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Best Practice Analyzer (BPA) JSON Rules**  \\r\\n1. **Rule 1**: Flag columns with the DateTime data type to suggest splitting into Date and Time for better compression.  \\r\\n2. **Rule 2**: Flag non-key String columns in Fact tables to highlight potential compression bottlenecks.\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Operational Runbook: The Report Visuals Warning**  \\r\\n1. Step 1: Run the C# Scanner Script.  \\r\\n2. Step 2: Use third-party tools or Fabric Scanner APIs to cross-reference the list against report visual dependencies.  \\r\\n3. Step 3: Delete the folder only after business confirmation regarding ad-hoc usage.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**:  \\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown\\r\\n\\r\\n### 1. Tabular Editor C# Script: The Unused Column Scanner\\r\\n\\r\\n```csharp\\r\\n/* Unused Column Scanner & Segregation Utility\\r\\n   Principal Power BI & Fabric Semantic Model Architect\\r\\n*/\\r\\n\\r\\nusing System.Text;\\r\\nusing System.Text.RegularExpressions;\\r\\n\\r\\nvar sb = new StringBuilder();\\r\\nsb.AppendLine(\\\"TableName\\\\tColumnName\\\\tDataType\\\\tDataCategory\\\");\\r\\n\\r\\nint count = 0;\\r\\n\\r\\nforeach (var col in Model.AllColumns)\\r\\n{\\r\\n    // 1. Dependency Check\\r\\n    bool isUsed =\\r\\n        col.ReferencedBy.Any() ||\\r\\n        col.UsedInRelationships.Any() ||\\r\\n        col.UsedInHierarchies.Any() ||\\r\\n        col.IsUsedInSortBy;\\r\\n\\r\\n    // 2. Exclusion Logic\\r\\n    bool isExcluded = Regex.IsMatch(col.Name, @\\\"(Key|ID|RowTimestamp)$\\\", RegexOptions.IgnoreCase);\\r\\n\\r\\n    if (!isUsed && !isExcluded)\\r\\n    {\\r\\n        // 3. Action & Segregation Strategy\\r\\n        col.DisplayFolder = \\\"_TO_BE_DELETED\\\";\\r\\n        col.IsHidden = true;\\r\\n\\r\\n        // 4. Audit Logging (TSV)\\r\\n        sb.AppendLine(string.Format(\\\"{0}\\\\t{1}\\\\t{2}\\\\t{3}\\\",\\r\\n            col.Table.Name,\\r\\n            col.Name,\\r\\n            col.DataType,\\r\\n            col.DataCategory));\\r\\n\\r\\n        count++;\\r\\n    }\\r\\n}\\r\\n\\r\\n// Final Output\\r\\nsb.Insert(0, \\\"TOTAL UNUSED COLUMNS FLAGGED: \\\" + count + \\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\");\\r\\nsb.ToString().Output();\\r\\n\\r\\nInfo(\\\"Scan Complete. Unused columns have been hidden and moved to '_TO_BE_DELETED'. Review the output list before final deletion.\\\");\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent provides a highly specialized diagnostic framework designed to maximize the VertiPaq memory efficiency of your enterprise semantic models. It delivers a comprehensive C# \\\"Scanner Engine\\\" that utilizes native Tabular Editor dependency tracking to identify objects with zero model-level references while strictly protecting your vital technical keys and system columns. By automating the segregation of these candidates into a \\\"_TO_BE_DELETED\\\" folder, the agent facilitates a safe, metadata-driven cleanup process that bridges the gap between model development and report-level visual dependencies.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Build report navigation and information architecture (home page, sections, drill paths)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as an **Expert Power BI Architect and UX Designer** tasked with designing the **Information Architecture and Navigation Flow** for a new Power BI report.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Report Purpose**: {e.g., Enterprise Financial Performance, Global Supply Chain Tracking, HR Employee Retention}\\r\\n• **User Personas**: {e.g., Executives who need quick summaries, Analysts who need row-level detail, Regional Managers who only look at their territory}\\r\\n• **Key Subject Areas/Modules**: {e.g., Overview, Revenue Analysis, Cost Breakdown, Regional Performance, Transaction Details}\\r\\n• **Data Granularity**: {e.g., High-level aggregated KPIs down to individual invoice/employee level}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease provide a comprehensive architecture plan based on the provided context, including:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Navigation & Structure**:\\r\\n  1. **Overall Navigation Pattern**: Suggest the best structural model (e.g., Hub & Spoke, Left-hand Navigation Menu, Top-tabbed Navigation, App-style) and explain why.\\r\\n  2. **Home Page / Landing Page Strategy**: What should the user see first? How should it guide them to other sections?\\r\\n  3. **Page/Section Breakdown**: Provide a logical mapping of the report pages, including the primary focus of each page.\\r\\n• **Interactivity & UI**:\\r\\n  1. **Drill Paths**: Give specific recommendations for Drill-through pages (e.g., moving from a regional summary to a specific store's details), Drill-downs within visuals (hierarchies), and the use of custom Report Page Tooltips to save space.\\r\\n  2. **UI/Navigation Elements**: Provide recommendations on using Power BI native features (Page Navigators, Bookmark buttons, hidden filter panes, back buttons) to create a seamless app-like experience.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Provide a well-structured Markdown response.\\r\\n• Include clear layout recommendations and navigational blueprints.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide an optimal navigation model, landing page strategy, page breakdown, drill-through/drill-down paths, and Power BI native UI element recommendations (Navigators, Bookmarks, filter panes, back buttons) for an app-like experience.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Build report pages (overview, detail, trend, exception)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as an **Expert Power BI Developer** tasked with designing four specific pages (Overview, Detail, Trend, and Exception) for a multi-page Power BI report and providing exact specifications to accelerate visual creation.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Core Subject**: {e.g., Retail Sales, Manufacturing Defects, Website Traffic}\\r\\n• **Key Metrics (Facts)**: {e.g., Total Sales, Quantity, Discount Amount, Profit Margin}\\r\\n• **Key Dimensions**: {e.g., Date, Product Category, Store Location, Customer Segment}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease provide a layout plan and exact visual configurations for the following four pages so I can build them quickly:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Overview Page (Executive Summary)**:\\r\\n  1. **Focus**: High-level KPIs, current status, and top-level breakdowns.\\r\\n  2. **Visuals**: Suggest 3-4 KPI cards and 2-3 high-level summary charts.\\r\\n• **Trend Page (Time-Series Analysis)**:\\r\\n  1. **Focus**: Historical performance, seasonality, and period-over-period comparisons (YoY, MoM).\\r\\n  2. **Visuals**: Suggest visuals that best show changes over time and cumulative totals.\\r\\n• **Detail Page (Granular/Row-Level Data)**:\\r\\n  1. **Focus**: Deep dive into specific categories, suitable for a Drill-through destination.\\r\\n  2. **Visuals**: Suggest matrix/table layouts, cross-filtering interactions, and detailed slicers.\\r\\n• **Exception Page (Anomalies & Actionables)**:\\r\\n  1. **Focus**: Outliers, bottom performers, targets missed, or data quality issues.\\r\\n  2. **Visuals**: Suggest visuals that highlight variances (e.g., Waterfall, Scatter plots) and specific conditional formatting rules to draw attention to issues.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Format the output as a clear blueprint for each page.\\r\\n• Provide exact visual configurations specifying the Power BI native visuals to use (Visual Type, X-axis/Rows, Y-axis/Values, Legend, and Tooltips).\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide visual type and field-well configurations (X-axis, Y-axis, Legend, Tooltips) for four pages: KPI summary, time-series trends, drill-through detail tables, and exception/variance visuals with conditional formatting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Build visuals, charts, cards, KPIs, matrices\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Connect to Power BI Desktop with name '**Provide report name here**'.\\r\\n**Provide the requirements context with respect to what metric has to be shown and at what level**\\r\\nWhich visual type best suits the requirement?\\r\\n**Provide context with respect to data size**\",\n      \"output\": \"It will assist you in selecting the visual types best suited to the metrics and the levels provided.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Apply conditional formatting (threshold-based, color semantics, icons)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as an **Expert Power BI Developer** tasked with applying **conditional formatting** to my Power BI report using threshold-based rules, color semantics, and icons.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Metric 1**: {e.g., Profit Margin}\\r\\n  • **Target/Threshold**: {e.g., Target is 20%. <15% is bad, 15-20% is okay, >20% is good}\\r\\n  • **Direction**: {e.g., Higher is better}\\r\\n• **Metric 2**: {e.g., Customer Churn Rate}\\r\\n  • **Target/Threshold**: {e.g., Target is <5%. >8% is bad, 5-8% is warning, <5% is good}\\r\\n  • **Direction**: {e.g., Lower is better}\\r\\n• **Visual Types Used**: {e.g., Matrix table, KPI Cards, Bar Charts}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease generate the exact rules, HEX color codes, and DAX measures needed:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Color Semantics & HEX Codes**:\\r\\n  1. Recommend a color palette (Red/Yellow/Green or accessible alternatives like Orange/Blue) with exact HEX codes for Good, Warning, and Bad states.\\r\\n• **DAX Color Measures**:\\r\\n  1. Write the DAX measures using the `SWITCH(TRUE(), ...)` pattern that I can use for \\\"Field value\\\" based      conditional formatting (Background Color and Font Color).\\r\\n• **Icon Rules**:\\r\\n  1. Suggest which native Power BI icons (e.g., arrows, traffic lights, flags) to use for each metric.\\r\\n  2. Provide the exact percentage/number thresholds to configure in the conditional formatting UI.\\r\\n• **Data Bars**:\\r\\n  1. Provide recommendations on where to apply Data Bars and how to configure the positive/negative axis colors.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Provide the DAX code blocks clearly formatted.\\r\\n• Include a well-structured Markdown response mapping the HEX codes, DAX, and icon rules to the provided metrics.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide HEX color palettes, DAX SWITCH(TRUE(), ...) color measures, icon set configurations, and data bar recommendations mapped to the provided metrics and thresholds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Add drill-through pages, bookmarks, and interactions\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Based on the following requirements:\\r\\n**Provide the system context here**\\r\\n• Metrics to be displayed\\r\\n• Required granularities\\r\\n• Variations for user interactions\\r\\n\\r\\nRecommend an optimal report design approach that prioritizes usability and performance.\\r\\n\\r\\nSpecifically:\\r\\n• Advise whether to use bookmarks, drill-through pages, tooltip pages, or drill-down hierarchies.\\r\\n• Suggest the best layout structure for intuitive navigation.\\r\\n• Recommend interaction patterns that minimize performance overhead.\\r\\n• Consider best practices for handling the dataset size: \\r\\n• **Provide data size context **\",\n      \"output\": \"It will assist in designing the report layout based on the system context provided\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Apply corporate themes & branding (color palettes, fonts, styles)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as an **Expert Power BI Developer and UI/UX Designer** tasked with creating a custom **Power BI Theme JSON file** to automatically apply corporate branding (colors, fonts, and visual styles) to my reports.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Theme Name**: {e.g., Corporate Standard Theme 2024}\\r\\n• **Primary Data Colors (Hex codes)**: {e.g., #004B87, #00A3E0, #F2A900, #7A99AC, #E1E8EC, #333333}\\r\\n• **Semantic/Status Colors**: {e.g., Good: #28A745, Neutral: #FFC107, Bad: #DC3545}\\r\\n• **Background & Foreground**: {e.g., Page background: #F3F4F6, Visual background: #FFFFFF, Text color: #252423}\\r\\n• **Typography / Font Family**: {e.g., Segoe UI, Arial, or Tahoma}\\r\\n• **Visual Styling Preferences**: {e.g., Turn on visual borders with a 5px radius and light gray color, set visual titles to 14pt bold, set data labels to 10pt}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease generate a complete, valid Power BI Theme JSON code block based on the provided guidelines:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **JSON Configuration**:\\r\\n  1. Include the `name` and `dataColors` arrays.\\r\\n  2. Map the `good`, `neutral`, and `bad` sentiment colors.\\r\\n  3. Create a `visualStyles` section that applies the font family globally (`*`) to all text classes (label, title, callout, header).\\r\\n  4. Apply global visual formatting for borders, backgrounds, and tooltips based on the visual styling preferences.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output**:\\r\\n• Provide the complete, valid JSON code block ready to be imported into Power BI Desktop.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a complete, valid Power BI Theme JSON file with corporate dataColors, sentiment color mappings, global font styling across all text classes, and visual-level formatting for borders, backgrounds, and tooltips — ready to be imported directly into Power BI Desktop.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Development\",\n      \"task\": \"Optimize report performance (DAX optimization, visual reduction, query folding)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Prompt - 1 Using Power BI MCP \\r\\nConnect to Power BI Desktop with name '**Provide report name here**'.\\r\\nAnalyze and provide suggestion to optimize the report performance with respect to Measure optimizations, number of visuals per page, query folding.\\r\\n**Provide context with respect to data size**\\r\\n\\r\\nPrompt - 2 Using Github copilot\\r\\nAnalyze the query plan and provide the performance bottlenecks with respect to formula engine and storage engine with the suggestions of possible optimizations .\\r\\nConsider below suggestion in addition to your inputs.\\r\\n1) DAX Optimization\\r\\n• Identify inefficient measures (SUMX, FILTER, ADDCOLUMNS, nested CALCULATE, excessive context transition).\\r\\n• Replace iterators with aggregations where possible.\\r\\n• Convert row-by-row logic into storage-engine friendly calculations.\\r\\n• Introduce VAR to prevent repeated evaluation.\\r\\n• Suggest moving calculated columns to measures when appropriate.\\r\\n• Rewrite slow measures and provide before/after comparison.\\r\\n• Reduce formula engine usage.\\r\\n\\r\\n2) Data Model Optimization\\r\\n• Detect non-star schema design.\\r\\n• Identify bi-directional relationships.\\r\\n• Highlight many-to-many relationships.\\r\\n• Detect unused columns.\\r\\n• Recommend surrogate integer keys instead of text joins.\\r\\n• Suggest disabling auto date/time if applicable.\\r\\n\\r\\n3) Query Folding (Power Query M)\\r\\n• Identify steps that break query folding.\\r\\n• Suggest pushing transformations to source system.\\r\\n• Recommend filtering early.\\r\\n• Rewrite M queries to preserve folding.\\r\\n• Highlight row-by-row operations.\\r\\n**Provide the query plan obtained from DAX studio for context**\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a comprehensive Power BI performance tuning report that diagnoses Formula engine and storage engine bottlenecks and provides actionable DAX, data model, and query folding optimizations with some suggested examples.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"Deployment\": [\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Deployment\",\n      \"task\": \"Set up Azure DevOps pipelines for ADO (CI/CD)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Role:\\r\\nYou are a Senior Azure Data Engineer and Azure DevOps Engineer responsible for designing secure, enterprise-grade, production-ready CI/CD pipelines using Azure DevOps.\\r\\n\\r\\nGoal:\\r\\nDesign a reusable CI/CD pipeline using Azure DevOps YAML that automates build, validation, and deployment of Azure data workloads across Dev, Test, and Prod environments. The pipeline must follow Azure and DevOps best practices, be secure, and serve as a standard template for future projects.\\r\\n\\r\\nContext:\\r\\nWe want to eliminate manual deployments and implement automated, secure, and auditable deployments using Azure DevOps CI/CD pipelines.\\r\\n\\r\\nEnvironment:\\r\\n\\r\\nRepository:\\r\\n- <Add Azure DevOps Repo Name>\\r\\n\\r\\nResources to Deploy:\\r\\n- <Add Resource 1> (ADF, Synapse, Fabric, Databricks, Function App, Bicep, Terraform, etc.)\\r\\n- <Add Resource 2>\\r\\n\\r\\nAzure Subscription:\\r\\n- <Add Subscription Name>\\r\\n\\r\\nResource Groups:\\r\\n- Dev: <Add RG>\\r\\n- Test: <Add RG>\\r\\n- Prod: <Add RG>\\r\\n\\r\\nEnvironments:\\r\\n- Dev\\r\\n- Test\\r\\n- Prod\\r\\n\\r\\nBranch Strategy:\\r\\n- <Add branch mapping>\\r\\n  Example:\\r\\n  - feature/* → Dev\\r\\n  - develop → Test\\r\\n  - main → Prod\\r\\n\\r\\nAuthentication:\\r\\n- Service Connection: <Add Name>\\r\\n- Authentication Type: Workload Identity Federation (preferred) or Service Principal\\r\\n\\r\\nTask:\\r\\nCreate a production-ready CI/CD setup guide and YAML pipelines.\\r\\n\\r\\nRequirements:\\r\\n\\r\\nCI Pipeline:\\r\\n- Trigger on pull request and commits\\r\\n- Validate and build project\\r\\n- Run validation checks if applicable\\r\\n- Publish build artifacts\\r\\n\\r\\nCD Pipeline:\\r\\n- Multi-stage deployment: Dev → Test → Prod\\r\\n- Use environment-specific variables and variable groups\\r\\n- Use approval gates for Test and Prod\\r\\n- Use secure service connections\\r\\n- Integrate Azure Key Vault for secrets\\r\\n- Support rollback to previous version\\r\\n\\r\\nPipeline Architecture:\\r\\n- Use YAML template-based structure\\r\\n- Recommend folder structure\\r\\n- Follow naming conventions\\r\\n\\r\\nSecurity:\\r\\n- Use least privilege RBAC\\r\\n- Do not hardcode secrets\\r\\n- Use Key Vault and secure variables\\r\\n- Follow enterprise security best practices\\r\\n\\r\\nOperations:\\r\\n- Include rollback strategy\\r\\n- Include troubleshooting guidance\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput Format:\\r\\n\\r\\nProvide:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. CI pipeline YAML example\\r\\n2. CD multi-stage pipeline YAML example\\r\\n3. Template-based pipeline structure\\r\\n4. Recommended repo folder structure (text diagram)\\r\\n5. Azure CLI commands to configure service connection and permissions\\r\\n6. Step-by-step implementation guide\\r\\n7. Best practices checklist\\r\\n\\r\\nFormat everything in Markdown.\\r\\n\\r\\nMake it:\\r\\n- Production-ready\\r\\n- Secure\\r\\n- Reusable\\r\\n- Easy to understand\\r\\n- Suitable for enterprise deployment\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Deployment\",\n      \"task\": \"Implement parameterized deployments across environments\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Act as an **Expert DevOps & Data Engineer** tasked with the design of a comprehensive **Parameterized Deployment Strategy** for a specific data ingestion pipeline. The solution must support multiple environments (DEV, QA, PROD) using a \\\"Build Once, Deploy Many\\\" approach.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context to Provide** (Please fill in these details):\\r\\n• **Ingestion Pattern**: {e.g., Batch ETL / Real-time Streaming / CDC / File-Based}\\r\\n• **Primary Processing Tool**: {e.g., Azure Data Factory / Databricks (PySpark) / AWS Glue / Kafka / Python Script}\\r\\n• **Orchestration/CICD**: {e.g., Azure DevOps (YAML) / GitHub Actions / Jenkins / Airflow}\\r\\n• **Configuration Storage**: {e.g., JSON files / Environment Variables / Azure App Config / HashiCorp Vault}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\nPlease provide a step-by-step implementation guide including:\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Configuration Management Strategy**:\\r\\n  1. How to separate code from configuration for this specific stack.\\r\\n  2. Example structure for config files (e.g., config.dev.json vs config.prod.json) or variable groups.\\r\\n• **Code/Script Modifications**:\\r\\n  1. Show a code snippet of the Ingestion Logic (in the language of the Primary Processing Tool) demonstrating how to read these parameters dynamically at runtime, preventing hard-coded values.\\r\\n• **CI/CD Pipeline Template**:\\r\\n  1. Provide a skeleton YAML/Script for the deployment pipeline.\\r\\n  2. Show specifically how to inject environment-specific variables (Storage Accounts, Connections Strings, Compute Sizing) during the Build and Release phases.\\r\\n• **Security & Secrets**:\\r\\n  1. Explain how to handle sensitive credentials (passwords, API keys) for this stack without committing them to Git.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**:\\r\\n• Use standard Markdown.\\r\\n• Include distinct blocks for code (Python/SQL/YAML).\\r\\n• Provide a \\\"Best Practices\\\" checklist at the end.\",\n      \"output\": \"It will provide a standard Markdown implementation guide featuring configuration management strategies with example file structures, code snippets for dynamic parameter reading, a skeleton CI/CD YAML pipeline for multi-environment deployments, instructions on securing credentials without committing them to Git, and a concluding best practices checklist.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"Support\": [\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Support\",\n      \"task\": \"Create Databricks Runbook (execution, restart logic, troubleshooting)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"I need to create a runbook for engineers working on the attached system. Draft one that includes sections for execution, restart logic, and troubleshooting.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Provide context here**\",\n      \"output\": \"In the attached workspace, I have two folders.\\r\\nIn Synapse, I have all the notebooks, and in Pipelines I have orchestration pipelines.Based on the system context, it will generate the runbook for engineers.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Support\",\n      \"task\": \"Create data contract for all published datasets (schema, SLAs, DQ rules, lineage)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"Create a standardized Data Contract framework for all published datasets in our data platform.\\r\\n**Provide system Context**\\r\\nThe solution should include:\\r\\n1. Data Contract Structure\\r\\nDefine a reusable template (YAML or JSON) that includes:\\r\\n• Dataset metadata (name, domain, owner, steward, description)\\r\\n• Versioning (semantic versioning support)\\r\\n• Schema definition:\\r\\n        o\\tField name\\r\\n        o\\tData type\\r\\n        o\\tNullable flag\\r\\n        o\\tDefault value\\r\\n        o\\tDescription\\r\\n        o\\tExample value\\r\\n        o\\tPII classification\\r\\n        o\\tAllowed values \\r\\n• Primary keys\\r\\n• Foreign keys\\r\\n• Partitioning strategy\\r\\n• Data freshness expectations\\r\\n• Retention policy\\r\\n• Access classification (public/internal/confidential/restricted)\\r\\n\\r\\n2. SLAs / SLOs Section\\r\\nInclude:\\r\\n• Refresh frequency\\r\\n• Data availability SLA (e.g., 99.9%)\\r\\n• Freshness threshold (e.g., data < 2 hours old)\\r\\n• Incident response time\\r\\n• Escalation contacts\\r\\n• Backfill policy\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Data Quality Rules\\r\\nSupport:\\r\\n• Not null constraints\\r\\n• Uniqueness constraints\\r\\n• Referential integrity checks\\r\\n• Accepted values\\r\\n• Range checks\\r\\n• Regex validation\\r\\n• Volume anomaly detection\\r\\n• Freshness validation\\r\\n• Custom SQL checks\\r\\nProvide example DQ rules in Great Expectations or SQL format.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Lineage Definition\\r\\nInclude:\\r\\n• Upstream source systems\\r\\n• Source tables\\r\\n• Transformation layer (e.g., Bronze → Silver → Gold)\\r\\n• Transformation logic reference (dbt model, Spark job, etc.)\\r\\n• Downstream consumers\\r\\n• BI dashboards using the dataset\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Validation & Automation\\r\\nGenerate:\\r\\n• CI/CD validation strategy\\r\\n• Schema drift detection logic\\r\\n• Backward compatibility rules\\r\\n• Contract enforcement example in Python\\r\\n• Example integration with dbt\\r\\n\\r\\n6. Output Requirements\\r\\n• It should be automated and reusable and should be able to do it for all the tables under the provided unity catalog and schema.\\r\\n• Use YAML format\\r\\n• Include inline documentation comments\\r\\n• Ensure it is production-ready and enterprise-grade\",\n      \"output\": \"It will generate the script for framework handling:\\r\\nStandardized dataset governance\\r\\nEnforceable schema stability\\r\\nTransparent SLAs\\r\\nEmbedded data quality\\r\\nAutomated drift detection\\r\\nEnterprise-grade auditability\\r\\nScalable support for all Unity Catalog schemas\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Support\",\n      \"task\": \"Create lineage documentation for each published dataset\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a **Principal Data Architect** and **Python Developer** responsible for designing automated metadata management, documentation, and **data lineage extraction frameworks**.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Task**:\\r\\nGenerate a **complete, automated Python framework** that extracts **data lineage** from the specified data platform and dynamically generates a **Markdown documentation file** containing a **Mermaid.js lineage diagram**.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context**:\\r\\nWe need a **robust utility** to automatically trace **data lineage (upstream sources and downstream consumers)** for specific **data assets** and generate **visual documentation** that is **syntactically valid for Mermaid.js and Markdown**.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Target Platform:** {Add Target Platform, e.g., **Databricks Unity Catalog** OR **Microsoft Fabric**}  \\r\\n**Authentication Method:** {Add Auth Method, e.g., **Databricks PAT Token via env vars**, OR **Azure Service Principal (Client ID/Secret)**}  \\r\\n**Target Asset ID/Name:** {Add Target Asset, e.g., **catalog_name.schema_name.table_name** OR **Fabric_Workspace_ID**}  \\r\\n**Extract Column-Level Lineage?:** {Add YES or NO, e.g., **YES - map source columns to target columns**}  \\r\\n**Output File Path:** {Add Output Path, e.g., **./docs/data_dictionary_and_lineage.md**}\\r\\n\\r\\n**Instructions**:\\r\\nProvide the **COMPLETE, copy-pasteable Python script**. Do **not use conversational filler**. Output code in **structured markdown blocks**. The Python code **MUST handle API pagination and rate limiting (HTTP 429)** gracefully. The **Mermaid.js output MUST be syntactically valid** (e.g., stripping out spaces or special characters from table names that break **Mermaid rendering**).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements**:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **The Lineage Extraction Client (LineageAPIClient)**\\r\\n\\r\\n• Generate a **robust Python class** tailored strictly to the **Target Platform** and **Authentication Method**.  \\r\\n• If **Databricks**: Use the **databricks-sdk** (w.system_schemas or **/api/2.0/lineage-tracking**).  \\r\\n• If **Fabric**: Use **requests** and the **MSAL library** to hit the **Power BI Scanner/Admin REST APIs**.  \\r\\n• The class must traverse **upstream (sources)** and **downstream (consumers/dashboards)** based on the **Target Asset ID/Name**.  \\r\\n• Return a **standardized Python dictionary**: {\\\"nodes\\\": [{\\\"id\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"name\\\": \\\"...\\\"}], \\\"edges\\\": [{\\\"source\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"target\\\": \\\"...\\\"}]}.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. **The Markdown and Mermaid Generator (LineageDocWriter)**\\r\\n\\r\\n• Generate a **Python class** that parses the **standardized dictionary** into **documentation**.  \\r\\n• Implement **clean_mermaid_id(name)** as a helper function to replace spaces, dots, and dashes with underscores so **Mermaid does not crash**.  \\r\\n• Implement **build_mermaid_graph(lineage_dict)** to loop through nodes and edges to construct a valid **graph LR (Left-to-Right)** Mermaid block. Differentiate **node shapes** (e.g., **[(Database)] for tables**, **([Dashboard]) for Power BI reports**).  \\r\\n• Implement **build_markdown_page(lineage_dict, mermaid_string)** to construct the final **.md layout** containing: an **H1 Header with the Target Asset Name** and a **Last Updated timestamp**, the **embedded Mermaid.js block**, a **Markdown table listing all Upstream Sources**, and a **Markdown table listing all Downstream Consumers**.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. **Execution and Error Handling**\\r\\n\\r\\n• Provide the **if __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\":** block.  \\r\\n• It must **instantiate the client**, **fetch the data**, **pass it to the writer**, and **save it to the Output File Path**.  \\r\\n• Include a **try/except block** to catch **Asset Not Found** or **API Unauthorized errors** and **log them clearly instead of throwing a massive stack trace**.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**:\\r\\n\\r\\nFormat everything in **Markdown**\\r\\n\\r\\n**The LineageAPIClient Python class code block**  \\r\\n**The LineageDocWriter Python class code block**  \\r\\n**The main execution script code block**\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent provides a high-performance Python framework designed to bridge the gap between live platform metadata and static documentation. It delivers a specialized LineageAPIClient to interface with your target platform's REST APIs (handling complex authentication and rate-limiting) and a LineageDocWriter that translates raw API payloads into syntactically perfect Mermaid.js diagrams and Markdown tables. By automating the extraction of upstream and downstream dependencies, this framework ensures your data lineage documentation remains synchronized with your actual production environment, reducing manual overhead and increasing metadata transparency.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Support\",\n      \"task\": \"Ensure compatibility for downstream semantic model consumption (Power BI)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Lead Data Engineer and Power BI Architect specializing in the intersection of Delta Lake storage and Power BI Semantic Modeling. You have deep expertise in maintaining \\\"Data Contracts\\\" to ensure that upstream pipeline changes never break downstream dashboards or report visualizations.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context & Vision**  \\r\\nThe goal is to implement a PySpark framework to update Delta tables while ensuring zero-downtime and compatibility for downstream Power BI semantic models. We need to move beyond simple data loading by treating the table schema as a \\\"Published Contract.\\\" Any change that would break a Power BI measure or relationship (such as dropping or renaming a column) must be intercepted before the Delta table is committed.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Project Configuration (Variables)**  \\r\\n• **Target Delta Table**: <Insert Table Name>  \\r\\n• **Power BI Workspace**: <Insert Workspace Name/ID>  \\r\\n• **Semantic Model (Dataset)**: <Insert Model Name>  \\r\\n• **Compatibility Threshold**: 100% (Strict Mode)  \\r\\n• **Rollback Strategy**: Delta Lake RESTORE  \\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements & Constraints**\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 1: Semantic Contract Validation**  \\r\\n1. **Schema Comparison**: Compare the incoming DataFrame schema against the \\\"Published Contract\\\" (the current Delta table schema used by Power BI).  \\r\\n2. **Breaking Change Logic**: If a column required by Power BI is renamed or dropped, the update must be blocked immediately.  \\r\\n3. **Additive Changes**: Allow new columns to be added as they do not break existing Power BI models (Schema Evolution).\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 2: Versioning & Lineage**  \\r\\n1. **Commit Tracking**: Capture commit_info from Delta Lake (DESCRIBE HISTORY) to explicitly map which Delta version corresponds to a specific Power BI report refresh.  \\r\\n2. **Audit Trail**: Log these version mappings into a **bi_sync_audit** metadata table for end-to-end traceability.\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 3: Monitoring & Quality Gates**  \\r\\n1. **Compatibility Score**: Implement a function to calculate a \\\"Compatibility Score.\\\"  \\r\\n2. **Decision Logic**:  \\r\\n   • If **100%**: Proceed with the MERGE or OVERWRITE.  \\r\\n   • If **<100%**: Quarantine the data and trigger an automated alert to the BI/Reporting team.\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 4: Zero-Downtime & Recovery**  \\r\\n1. **Idempotency**: Use Delta Lake RESTORE to provide an instant rollback mechanism if post-load validation fails.  \\r\\n2. **Metadata Auditing**: Use Spark SQL to perform all metadata and history checks for high performance.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Contract Validation Script**:  \\r\\n(PySpark logic to compare schemas and identify breaking changes)\\r\\n\\r\\n2. **Compatibility Scoring Engine**:  \\r\\n(Function to calculate the % score and determine the \\\"Go/No-Go\\\" status)\\r\\n\\r\\n3. **Delta Versioning & Sync Logic**:  \\r\\n(Code to log Delta history and map it to BI refresh cycles)\\r\\n\\r\\n4. **Rollback & Recovery Script**:  \\r\\n(A pre-configured RESTORE command for emergency intervention)\\r\\n\\r\\n5. **Alerting Template**:  \\r\\n(Sample log entry or notification format for the BI team when schema drift or compatibility issues are detected)\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent provides a PySpark framework for safely updating Delta tables while maintaining compatibility with downstream Power BI semantic models. The solution performs a semantic schema comparison between the incoming DataFrame and the existing table (published contract). If a column used by Power BI is renamed or dropped, the update is blocked to prevent report failures.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe framework also captures Delta Lake commit information to map table versions with Power BI refresh cycles, ensuring traceability between data updates and BI reports.\\r\\n\\r\\nA compatibility score is calculated during validation. If the score is 100%, the update proceeds; if it is below 100%, the data is quarantined and an alert is sent to the BI team. The system also supports rollback using Delta RESTORE and maintains metadata auditing through Spark SQL.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Support\",\n      \"task\": \"Document published dataset SLAs (availability, freshness, latency)\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"**Role**:\\r\\nYou are a Lead Data Architect and Documentation Specialist specializing in Data Governance, Reliability Engineering (SRE for Data), and Technical Writing. You have expertise in defining measurable performance metrics that align data engineering outputs with business expectations.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Context & Vision**  \\r\\nThe goal is to create a structured SLA documentation template for a published dataset to be hosted in the Azure DevOps Wiki. This document serves as a \\\"Social Contract\\\" between the Data Engineering team and downstream consumers (BI, Data Science, Product). It must move beyond vague promises by defining strictly measurable targets for availability, freshness, and latency, backed by a clear Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) for breach recovery.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Project Configuration (Variables)**  \\r\\n• **Dataset Name**: <Insert Dataset Name, e.g., Global_Sales_Gold>  \\r\\n• **Primary Consumer**: <e.g., Finance Leadership, Marketing Analytics>  \\r\\n• **Data Layer**: Gold (Consumption Layer)  \\r\\n• **Platform**: Azure DevOps Wiki / Microsoft Fabric  \\r\\n\\r\\n**Requirements & Constraints**\\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 1: Performance Benchmarks (The SLA)**  \\r\\n1. **Availability**: Define the uptime percentage for the dataset (e.g., 99.9% availability) and the maintenance windows.  \\r\\n2. **Freshness**: Specify the Data Refresh Frequency (e.g., T+1, Hourly, or Real-time).  \\r\\n3. **Latency**: Define the maximum allowable delay (in minutes/hours) between the initial data ingestion and its availability in the Gold layer.  \\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 2: Operational Governance (The SOP)**  \\r\\n1. **Incident Response**: Provide a clear \\\"Breach Protocol\\\" section detailing the steps taken if an SLA is missed.  \\r\\n2. **Communication Plan**: Define who is notified (Stakeholders) and the estimated time to provide an Initial Root Cause Analysis (RCA).  \\r\\n3. **Escalation Matrix**: List the contact points for Tier 1, Tier 2, and Tier 3 support levels.  \\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 3: Monitoring & Validation**  \\r\\n1. **Metric Tracking**: Briefly describe how these SLAs are measured (e.g., \\\"Measured via Azure Monitor custom logs\\\").  \\r\\n2. **Review Cycle**: Define how often the SLA is reviewed and updated (e.g., Quarterly).  \\r\\n\\r\\n• **Pillar 4: Formatting Standards**  \\r\\n1. **Markdown Optimized**: The output must be formatted specifically for Azure DevOps Wiki (supporting tables, bolding, and clear hierarchies).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output Format**\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **SLA Header & Metadata Table**:  \\r\\n(A section containing dataset metadata including dataset owners, version, status, and review cycle)\\r\\n\\r\\n2. **Service Level Objectives (SLOs) Section**:  \\r\\n(Visual tables defining Availability, Freshness, and Latency performance targets)\\r\\n\\r\\n3. **Standard Operating Procedure (SOP)**:  \\r\\n(A structured step-by-step incident response flow explaining detection, response, escalation, and recovery)\\r\\n\\r\\n4. **Escalation Matrix**:  \\r\\n(A clear table defining Tier 1, Tier 2, and Tier 3 contacts with responsibilities)\\r\\n\\r\\n5. **Glossary of Terms**:  \\r\\n(Definitions for T+1, Latency, Downtime, Availability, and Data Freshness to ensure shared understanding)\",\n      \"output\": \"Agent generates a structured SLA documentation template in Markdown format for publishing datasets in the Azure DevOps Wiki. The template defines key SLA components including availability targets, data refresh frequency, and maximum latency between ingestion and Gold layer availability.\\r\\n\\r\\nIt also includes a Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) section outlining incident response steps if an SLA breach occurs, ensuring clear operational guidance. The result is a standardized, ready-to-publish documentation framework that supports governance, reliability, and clear communication with data consumers.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"area\": \"Support\",\n      \"task\": \"Create incident response playbooks\",\n      \"copilotPrompt\": \"I need to create a incident response playbook for engineers working on the attached system. Draft different sections involved in the incident response process. \\r\\n\\r\\n**Provide context here**\",\n      \"output\": \"It will draft a playbook which includes purpose, roles and responsibilities, severity classification etc.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"
          },
